"""Original Security teaching packs. Each body is instructor prose."""

BODIES = {
    'Cloud responsibility split': {
        'body': "Shared responsibility on a cloud service means the provider and the customer each own different layers, and that cut moves when the service model moves. An infrastructure rental leaves buildings, power, physical hosts, and the hypervisor with the provider. The customer still patches the guest operating system, creates identity roles, and sets storage permissions. A platform rental adds the operating system and the runtime to the provider side. Application code, accounts, and the records that code stores stay with the customer. A finished application lets the provider patch the running platform, while sharing rules, account life cycle, and typed content remain customer work.\n\nThe mechanism is a written matrix, not a slogan on a status page. Each row names a layer such as physical access, host hardware, hypervisor, guest operating system, runtime, application, identity, logging, backup, and encryption keys. The party that can actually change that layer is the party that owns the control. Customer-managed keys are a different row from provider-managed keys, because a snapshot the provider cannot open is not the same exposure as a snapshot support staff can read during a ticket. Independent audit reports describe the provider half. They do not install the guest patch the customer still owes.\n\nA clinic platform lead opens the duty sheet for the billing virtual machine after a hardware notice. The provider ticket states that a failed host was replaced overnight. On the guest, the package history shows the last kernel update sixty-two days ago, and the unattended update log ends with a held package. Ticket CLD-441 records the split in one line: host replacement is the provider row, and the guest kernel is the clinic row. Nobody treats the hardware notice as proof that the virtual machine is patched.\n\nDefenders keep the matrix beside the architecture diagram and revise it when a queue, a function, or an object store is added, because those services do not inherit the worksheet written for the first virtual machine. They name an owner for log export, backup tests, identity roles, and key custody. They tie every provider-owned duty to an exit path so a console lockout cannot trap images and logs. During an incident they ask who can see the relevant log and who is allowed to change the control, and they write that answer into the timeline before they edit anything.\n\nStudents mix this split with infrastructure as code. Declared configuration is text a peer can review the way software is reviewed, and it can describe either party's settings. It does not decide which party owns a layer. Another miss is claiming the provider patches the guest on an infrastructure rental, or claiming a finished application removes the customer's duty for accounts and sharing. Shared responsibility means each side owns different layers. It does not mean both sides perform the same jobs.",
        'r0': 'Three service models cut the layers in different places. On an infrastructure rental the provider keeps physical gear and the hypervisor, while the customer patches the guest operating system and owns identity plus data. On a platform rental the provider also maintains the operating system and runtime, and the customer still owns application code and the rows it writes. On a finished application the provider patches the platform, yet account settings, sharing rules, and stored content stay customer configuration. Key custody is its own row: customer-managed keys and provider-managed keys change who can read a snapshot. Audit reports cover the provider half only. A new service type needs a new row, because a function and an object store do not copy the virtual-machine worksheet.',
        'r1': 'Each side owns different layers. The cut moves with the service model. You still patch a rented virtual machine. The provider keeps hosts and the hypervisor. A platform rental shifts system care outward. You still own application code and records. A finished app leaves sharing rules with you. Name an owner for logs and keys. Redraw the sheet for every new service. Legal duty for the records stays with you. Customer held keys differ from provider held keys. A brochure does not assign the work.',
        'r2': 'Picture a medical building with rented suites. The landlord keeps the roof, the boilers, the elevators, and the structural walls in safe condition. A practice that rents empty rooms still locks its own door, chooses the staff list, and updates the computers it rolled in. A practice that rents a fully staffed ward lets the building crew maintain those ward systems, and it still decides which people may open a chart. The empty suite and the staffed ward are two different cuts of the same building. In a cloud design the provider and the customer each own different layers, and the cut moves when the rental changes from raw machines to a finished application.',
        'r4': 'Open the duty sheet for one production service. Write the provider beside physical hosts and the hypervisor. Write your team beside guest patches if the rental is a virtual machine. Note who owns identity, backups, log export, and keys. Do not change a firewall rule during this pass. File a ticket for any blank row. Repeat the sheet when a new service type is added.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to say the provider patches the guest operating system on an infrastructure rental. That guest stays with the customer. A finished application still leaves sharing rules and typed content with the customer. A second trap treats infrastructure as code as if it chose the owner. Declared files are configuration reviewed like software. They do not move a layer from one party to the other. Shared responsibility means each party owns different layers.',
    },
    'Hybrid cloud': {
        'body': 'Some systems stay on premises and some systems run at a provider. A hybrid cloud is the choice to keep both of those locations, and the path between the owned side and the provider is part of the design. Identity, or records that cannot leave the building, can remain on the owned side. Mail, a public site, or extra compute can live with the provider. The path may be a private circuit, an encrypted tunnel, or a tightly filtered route, and each of those path types fails in its own way. Treating the path as a trusted hallway is a common failure, because staff then open wide routes in both directions.\n\nAccount rules should match on both sides, because a weak directory on the owned floor becomes the front door for workloads at the provider. The name service should have one authoritative answer, so clients do not collect two addresses for one service name. Logs belong on both estates and on the path, because an incident often starts on one side and continues on the other. A directory copy should carry only the attributes the far side needs, so a compromise at the provider does not export every secret field from the owned floor. Owned systems that were built to keep working alone should keep running through a provider outage. Administration of the path should ride a narrow management route, separate from the wide route ordinary applications use.\n\nPriya leads platform work at a clinic. She keeps the identity server in the clinic building and places mail at a provider. Ticket 318 allows the clinic firewall to build an IPsec tunnel with the provider peer on UDP 500 and UDP 4500, and it allows nothing else on that outside interface. Inside the tunnel, the policy permits TCP 587 to the mail hosts and denies a proposal that would route the whole staff subnet into the provider network. Priya can show those lines and name the records allowed to cross. The provider-side policy names the same mail hosts, rather than a copy of every internal service.\n\nDefenders review both ends whenever a new workload is added, because an opening on only one side is a quiet hole. They name an owner for the circuit, the tunnel policy, the routes, and the credentials that bring the path up. They test by stopping one side at a time and by following one sensitive record to see whether it was allowed to leave. They keep a separate duty matrix for each provider service, since the owned floor does not inherit the provider patch calendar. They watch for identity rules that were copied once and then edited on only one side.\n\nHybrid cloud is not the picture of two providers with no owned floor, and it is not a closet of gear with no provider half. The separating fact is the mix of on-premises systems and provider systems, plus a path that is filtered, logged, and tested. A duty split still applies to the rented half. That split does not move patching of the clinic identity server onto the provider.',
        'r0': 'A hybrid cloud places some systems on premises and some with a provider, and the path is a control with its own rules. One clinic keeps its identity server in the building and runs mail at a provider. When that path is an IPsec tunnel, the outside firewall allows UDP 500 and UDP 4500 to the provider peer and does not open every application port beside the tunnel. Inside the tunnel, TCP 587 may reach the mail hosts, and a route for the whole staff subnet is denied. Directory copy is limited to the attributes mail actually needs. Stopping the provider side should leave the owned identity server able to log staff in. The duty split on the rented mail service does not patch the owned server.',
        'r1': 'Some systems stay in your building. Some run at a provider. The road between them is part of the design. Do not treat that road as a trusted hall. Match account rules on both sides. Limit which records may cross. One side should keep working if the other stops. Name a person who owns the road. Draw the road as a real control. Copy only the account fields you need. Test each side failing on its own. Log admin crossings of the road. Review openings at both ends when you add a system.',
        'r2': 'Picture a bakery that bakes in its own kitchen and stores cakes in a rented cold room across town. The van between them matters as much as either building, because a spill on that road is its own problem. The family still decides which cakes may leave, even when the rented room is larger. A private road does not let the staff at the rented room copy every recipe. Blocking the road for an afternoon shows whether the kitchen can still serve alone. The kitchen you own and the cold room you rent are the two estates in this design. Some systems stay on premises, some run with a provider, and the road between them is a control you filter, log, and test.',
        'r4': 'Draw the owned systems and the provider systems on one page. Mark the path and write which records may cross. Name the person who owns the path. Do not open a new route during this pass. Test what still works if the provider side stops. Note any directory field copied that the other side does not need. Compare the allow rules at both ends of the path. Leave the live firewall unchanged until that review is written down.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to call any use of a provider a hybrid cloud. A hybrid cloud keeps some systems on premises and some with a provider, and the path between them is part of the design. Two providers and no owned floor are a different picture. Another trap treats the path as a trusted hallway because both ends belong to one organization. A wide route both ways fails that test. A further trap moves patching of the owned identity server onto the provider. The duty split covers the rented half only.',
    },
    'Third-party cloud vendors': {
        'body': "A third-party cloud vendor runs the platform an organization consumes. The organization still configures its tenant, its accounts, and the data it places there, and it still needs a contract plus an exit plan. The vendor is not a slogan and not a logo. The vendor is the operator who can touch the hosts, the storage, and the support tools. A reseller on the invoice may be a different company from the operator who holds the disks. The duty conversation starts by naming the operator.\n\nThe contract is the mechanism that makes the relationship enforceable. It should state uptime targets, support hours, incident notice, where data may sit, how long copies remain after termination, and which log types the operator will actually export. Subprocessors that can read or store the data belong on the same page, because a silent storage partner is still a party with access. Support access to production should be time bounded and visible in a log the organization can read afterward. An exit drill exports a real sample in the format the contract names, while changing the contract is still possible.\n\nA records manager reviews the imaging vendor before any chart is uploaded. The data-processing addendum lists a storage subprocessor the clinic had not heard in the sales call. The support appendix says security logs are available for fourteen days. Ticket VND-220 records that fourteen-day limit next to the clinic's thirty-day investigation standard, and the migration stays on hold. The manager also asks which administrative actions only the vendor can perform. The answer shows that deleting a forgotten snapshot is a vendor ticket, not a button in the tenant.\n\nDefenders assess the vendor with the contract, the audit report the contract allows, and a configuration review inside the tenant. They do not accept a questionnaire with no evidence. They keep a named internal owner who reads status notices and brings gaps back to leadership. They compare exit cost, including export time and the shape of the data, alongside the subscription price. They forbid secrets in ordinary vendor tickets and give staff a separate channel when a credential must be shown for a repair. They re-review the vendor on a calendar because regions and subprocessors change after the first signature.\n\nStudents treat the vendor relationship as if it erased the responsibility split. The vendor runs the building. The customer still chooses the locks on the tenant, the accounts, and the content. A questionnaire is not an exit plan, and a reseller is not automatically the operator. Legal duties attached to regulated records stay with the organization that collected them, even when every host is in the vendor's hall.",
        'r0': "A third-party cloud vendor is the platform operator, bound by contract, subprocessors, and a tenant the customer still configures. Judge the deal by log export, incident notice, region, retention, deletion, and a rehearsed exit, not by the sales region map. A fourteen-day vendor log may be shorter than the customer's investigation window, and that gap belongs in the risk ticket before data moves. Audit reports speak to the vendor half. Tenant reviews speak to identity, sharing, and content, which remain customer work under the responsibility split. Break-glass vendor access should be logged and time bounded. Name the operator separately from any reseller on the invoice.",
        'r1': 'The vendor runs the platform you configure. You still choose tenant accounts and data. Sign a contract before any records move. Ask which logs you can actually export. Test the exit while changes are still easy. Name the operator apart from the reseller. List partners who can touch the data. Keep legal duties with your organization. Review the tenant, not only the logo. Log any vendor support session. Put an internal owner on status notices. Keep secrets out of ordinary tickets.',
        'r2': "Think of a print shop that owns the presses, the ink store, and the loading dock. The client still chooses the manuscript and who may see the proofs. Before the plates are made, the client asks which scraps the shop keeps, which proofs can leave the building, and how fast the shop calls after a spoiled run. A famous shop does not excuse the client from reading the proof, because a wrong page the client approved is still the client's page. The work order also names the binder down the street, and it sets a date to carry the manuscript out in a form another shop can read. That shop is the vendor: they run the machinery, and you still configure your job, your readers, and your way out.",
        'r4': 'Read the contract for logs, notice, region, and retention. Write down the operator separately from the reseller. List every subprocessor that can store or read the records. Export a sample while the contract can still change. Do not upload production records during this review. Note any log window shorter than your investigation need. Assign an internal owner for vendor notices.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to think a vendor signature moves identity, sharing, and content off the customer. The vendor runs the platform. The customer still configures the tenant. A questionnaire without evidence and without an exit path is not an acceptance. Another trap treats the reseller on the invoice as the company that holds the disks. Ask which logs will be handed over and how long they are kept. Legal duty for regulated records does not move with the building.',
    },
    'Infrastructure as code': {
        'body': 'Infrastructure as code is declared configuration for servers, networks, and policies, reviewed the way application code is reviewed. The desired end state lives in files a peer can read, replay, and roll back. A tool converges the live environment toward that file, and a preview lists what will be created, changed, or destroyed before anything touches production. A click made at midnight, with no file and no second reader, is not this practice even if someone later describes the click in a ticket.\n\nThe live environment should match the file. An edit made only in a console is drift, and the next preview should reveal that drift rather than hide it. Secrets stay in a vault, and the file holds a reference, because a secret written into the file will be copied with every duplicate of that file. Lab and production keep separate state and separate variables, so a lab trial cannot rewrite the production network. The automation account holds only the permissions that account needs. An account that can change anything deserves the same suspicion as a shared administrator password. Fixed version numbers on shared modules stop a surprise module change from rewriting production during an unrelated edit.\n\nElena reviews the bank firewall change for ticket 552. The preview reports 1 to change and 0 to destroy. The changed lines allow TCP 443 from the staff subnet to the billing hosts. A second line in the same file opens TCP 22 from the whole internet to those hosts. Elena rejects the file, so the open administration port never reaches the live firewall. The file history shows her name, the rejected lines, and the earlier version that is still in force. Nobody has to remember a click.\n\nDefenders put the same review on identity rules and network rules that they already use for application changes. Automated checks can reject a storage setting that is open to the world, or a role that can perform every action, before the apply step. State files need the same protection as the environment they describe, because state often holds resource identifiers and sometimes secrets. An emergency change is logged and folded back into the file the same day, so drift does not become the normal source of truth. Each change carries the ticket number, and an auditor can walk from a live rule back to the approval.\n\nStudents confuse this practice with software-defined networking. A controller that pushes paths is network control from software. Declared files can describe that policy, and they can also describe hosts, roles, and storage. The separating fact is the declaration of desired state, which peers can review, not the kind of device. Another miss is believing the file chooses which party owns a layer. A duty split still answers the ownership question. The file makes the chosen setting visible, repeatable, and able to be undone.',
        'r0': 'Infrastructure as code is declared configuration reviewed like software. A preview runs before the apply step and reports how many resources will be created, changed, or destroyed. In the bank example the preview reports 1 to change and 0 to destroy. The intended change allows TCP 443 from the staff subnet to the billing hosts. The rejected line opens TCP 22 from the whole internet. Secrets stay in a vault, and the file holds a reference rather than the secret itself. Lab state stays separate from production state. The automation account is limited to the actions that change requires. A console edit outside the file is drift the next preview should show. Rollback is a return to the last approved file and a fresh apply.',
        'r1': 'The setup lives in files people can read. A peer can reject a dangerous edit. A preview shows the change before it goes live. You can roll back by restoring the last good file. A click at midnight is not this practice. Keep secrets out of those files. Keep a lab copy apart from production. Limit what the automation account can do. Reject a file that opens storage to everyone. Treat a console edit outside the file as drift. Tie each change to a ticket. Read the file when you want the truth.',
        'r2': 'Picture a carpenter who builds from a written plan that a second carpenter can mark, instead of from a memory of last winter. The second carpenter can circle a dangerous stair before anyone cuts wood. Going back to last month is possible only when the building still matches the pages. The lock combination for the tool room stays in a drawer, and the plan only names that drawer. A change made on site after dark has to be copied onto the plan the same day, or the next reader will trust the wrong page. The plan is declared configuration for a building. Infrastructure as code is that same habit for servers and networks: reviewed like software, then repeated or rolled back from the file.',
        'r4': 'Open the configuration file for one live firewall rule. Read the preview and write down anything it would destroy. Do not apply the change during this pass. Check that secrets are references to a vault, not values in the file. Confirm lab state is separate from production state. Note the permissions of the automation account. Write the ticket number stored with the change. If a console edit has no matching file line, record the drift and leave the console alone.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to treat a midnight click as infrastructure as code because someone wrote a ticket. The practice is declared configuration, reviewed like software, with a preview and a way to roll back. Another trap swaps this practice for software-defined networking. A controller pushes network paths. A declared file can describe many resources, including a policy a controller might apply. The file does not decide which party owns a layer. A duty split still answers that question. Drift and secrets stored in the file are defects.',
    },
    'Serverless computing': {
        'body': 'Serverless computing runs small functions on workers the provider supplies for each event, so there is no server for you to patch, image, or sign into. You still own the function code, the permissions, and every store of data those permissions can reach. The usual failure is a role wider than the job. A function that should read one invoice prefix is given a role that can read every bucket in the account. Time limits, memory size, and a cap on how many copies may run at once are security settings, because a runaway function can spend the budget and can amplify a bug.\n\nThe disk on a worker is ephemeral, so secrets should be fetched from a vault when the function runs, not packed into the uploaded bundle. The trigger that starts the function has to prove who sent the event. A public trigger in front of a powerful role is an open door into that role. Retries are part of the threat, because a second delivery of the same event can write a payment twice when the function is not safe to repeat. Each function should have its own role, so a flaw in one handler cannot inherit the rights of the others. The provider patches the workers. The provider does not review the libraries you packed beside the code.\n\nSam operates the invoice function at a billing company. The settings on that function are a 15 second time limit and 256 megabytes of memory. Ticket 774 quotes a log line in which the role named invoice-render reads the payroll bucket during a run that should only have touched the invoice prefix. The trigger is a web request, and the same log shows the request was accepted with no signed-in user. Sam writes that the role must be cut to the invoice prefix and that the trigger must require a known caller before the next release. The function has no guest operating system for Sam to patch, and that fact does not close the finding.\n\nDefenders start from a role that is too small and add the single missing action, rather than starting from an administrator template. They put a rule on the data store itself so a mistaken role still cannot read a collection the store refuses. They remove functions and triggers that no current feature calls, because an abandoned handler with a wide role remains a door. They keep the role, the trigger, and the function version in one reviewed file so those three change together. They watch shared event paths that let another team invoke the function without a new review.\n\nStudents treat serverless computing as the end of security work, or they describe it as a virtual machine they must patch. There is no server for you to patch, and the code, the permissions, and the reachable data remain yours. The neighboring design is a set of small services that stay up and ship on their own deploy cycle. A function lives for one event and then goes away, taking its local scratch space with it.',
        'r0': 'Serverless computing runs functions without a server you patch. Ownership of the code, the permissions, and the reachable data stays with you. The invoice function in the example is limited to 15 seconds and 256 megabytes. Its role can read every bucket, and the log shows a read of the payroll bucket when the job needed only the invoice prefix. The trigger accepted a web request with no signed-in user. A safer design gives that function its own role, fetches secrets at run time, and refuses a second write when a retry would duplicate a payment. The provider patches the workers. Libraries inside the bundle stay on the customer review list.',
        'r1': 'You do not patch a server for these functions. You still own the code. You still own the permissions. You still own the data the function can reach. Give each function a small role. A role that can read every store is too wide. Check who is allowed to start the function. Fetch secrets when the function runs. Do not bake secrets into the package. A repeat of the same event can write twice. Remove functions nobody calls. Set a short time limit on each run.',
        'r2': 'Picture a laundromat where you rent the machines by the load and you do not repair the motors. You still choose the clothes, the soap, and who may open your basket. Trouble starts when the key on your basket opens every basket in the shop, including the payroll coats in the back. A short wash does not make a bad key safe. The soap list stays in your pocket and comes out for the wash, rather than being printed on the basket. A second run of the same load can charge you twice if nobody checks the receipt. The machines are the workers you do not patch. The clothes, the key, and the basket are your code, your permissions, and the data a function can reach.',
        'r4': 'List one function, its trigger, and the data it can read. Write the time limit and the memory size. Do not widen the role during this pass. If the role can read every store, note the stores the job does not need. Check that the trigger requires a known caller. Confirm secrets are fetched at run time, not stored in the package. Find functions no current feature calls, and write them down for later removal. Leave the provider workers alone, and record that the code is still yours.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to hear that there is no server to patch and conclude that no duty remains. The code, the permissions, and the reachable stores remain your duty. A role that can read every bucket is wider than the one job. Another trap calls this design a virtual machine you must patch. There is no guest operating system in your hands. Small services that stay up and ship on their own cycle are the neighbor. A function runs for an event and exits.',
    },
    'Microservices': {
        'body': 'Microservices split one application into small services that call one another, and each service keeps its own deploy cycle. The split multiplies network doors, identities, and ways to fail, compared with one process shipped in one release. Each service should prove its own identity on every call. A shared administrator token passed among all of the services is a design error, because theft of that token is theft of every service that trusts it. A public entry can sit at a gateway. Calls inside the estate still need checks, because an internal network address is not an identity.\n\nBoth ends of a call should prove who they are, which stops a stranger from impersonating a dependency. Each service is limited to the actions it needs next, so an order service cannot read a personnel store merely because both sit behind one gateway. The gateway and the call map are high value, because a bad route sends traffic to the wrong place without a loud crash. Old credentials should expire when a service is retired, so a replaced build cannot keep calling with a copied key. The rights used to ship a service stay separate from the identity that service uses while it runs.\n\nMaya ships the order service for an online store. On Tuesday the order service moves to build 204 while the payment service stays on build 198. That gap is the separate deploy cycle, and the release log shows the two build numbers side by side. A call from build 204 presents one administrator token that every service received at the first launch. The payment service rejects the token. The log line reads payment-service denied shared admin token from order-service, trace 9f3c. Ticket 640 records the rejection and the follow-up that issues a distinct identity for the order service. A test read of the personnel store by the order identity is refused as well.\n\nDefenders deny internal traffic by default and then allow the pairs the call map names. They log a trace identifier on every hop so an incident becomes one story rather than a pile of unrelated lines. They give each service its own data store when they can, because one shared database login rebuilds a single pile of data behind a false boundary. They test by impersonating one service from another, and they expect the call to fail. They keep business rules inside the service, not only in the network, so a stolen identity still cannot perform an action the business forbids.\n\nStudents call any group of containers a microservice design, including one large application packed as a single image. The separating facts are the independent deploy cycle and the independent identity, not the packaging. A function that runs for one event and exits is the other neighbor, and that function does not stay up between calls. A shared administrator token across the small services is the choice that should be marked wrong. The public gateway does not replace checks on calls between the services.',
        'r0': 'Microservices are small services with their own deploy cycle, and each one should carry its own identity. A shared administrator token across them is a mistake. In the store example the order service moves to build 204 on Tuesday while the payment service stays on build 198. The payment service denies the shared administrator token, and the log names trace 9f3c. The order identity is also refused on a read of the personnel store. Internal calls stay denied unless the call map allows that pair. The credential that ships a service stays separate from the identity used while the service runs. The reach of a stolen service identity is the set of calls and stores that identity can touch.',
        'r1': 'Each small service ships on its own cycle. Each one needs its own identity. Do not share one administrator token across them. Lock calls between services, not only the front door. Limit each service to the stores it needs. Log a trace number across every hop. Deny inside traffic unless a call is named. Keep shipping rights apart from running rights. A stolen identity should fail a call it does not own. Test by pretending one service is another. Expect that pretend call to fail. Write down the call map.',
        'r2': "Picture a warehouse split into cages, each with its own lock and its own day for new stock. A master key that opens every cage turns one lost key into a lost warehouse. The front dock checks a delivery, and a handoff between cages is checked again. The invoice cage cannot open the personnel cabinet just because the cages share an aisle. Loading stock for one cage on one morning does not force every cage to change that day. The cages are small services that call one another, and each stock day is that service's own deploy cycle. Each lock is an identity, and a master key is the shared administrator token this design rejects.",
        'r4': 'Write the list of services and the order they call one another. Note the build number running on each service today. Do not ship a new build during this pass. Check that no single administrator token is accepted by every service. Confirm internal calls are denied unless the pair is named. Separate the credential that deploys a service from the credential used while it runs. Pick one stolen identity and write down every store it could read. Leave the business checks inside the service.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to accept one shared administrator token because the services belong to one product. Each small service needs its own identity, and that shared token should be rejected. Another trap locks only the public gateway and trusts every internal call. Calls between services need their own checks. A third trap calls one large application in one container a microservice design. The separating facts are an independent deploy cycle and an independent identity. Measure the risk by what a stolen service identity can reach.',
    },
    'Air-gapped networks': {
        'body': 'Air-gapped networks have no network path to the outside and no network path to any other network. There is no route, no wireless radio, and no host with one cable on the protected side and another cable anywhere else. People carry the data across, under a procedure the team controls. A firewall rule is not this design, because a rule is still a path that a later change can reopen. A one-way diode is not this design either, because the diode is still a network path even when replies cannot come back.\n\nThe transfer procedure names who may carry media, which station scans it, how the media is labeled, and where it is stored afterward. The scanning station is not wired into the protected network, so the inspection does not become a bridge. Every import and every export is logged with the person, the media identifier, the time, and the reason. Phones and laptops that nobody manages stay out of the protected room, because a radio is a path even when nobody configured a route. A maintenance laptop, when it must enter, has a written window and leaves when that window ends. Updates arrive as rare tested packages on the same kind of scanned media, since the protected side cannot call a vendor for a fix.\n\nJordan runs the safety network for a water lab. The safety consoles have no cable to the office network. From a safety console, a name lookup fails and a ping fails, and the routing table shows no default route. The Tuesday update arrives on a drive whose serial is 1844. The scan station, which has no wire into the safety network, records a clean result. The import log reads 1844 scanned clean, carried by Jordan, at 09:40. Ticket 901 holds that log line and a note that the drive returns to a locked cabinet. Jordan refuses a request to email the same package from an office workstation.\n\nDefenders look for accidental bridges, including unexpected interfaces, unexpected routes, and wireless names that should be absent. They can split the scan duty from the carry duty when the data is sensitive enough to need two people. They turn off automatic launch when media is inserted, so the approved drive cannot start a program that came along for the ride. They keep local accounts strong, because a gap does not police a person who is already at the console. They arrange on-site help before the day it is needed, because an outside specialist cannot connect to a network that has no path.\n\nStudents select a strict firewall, or a diode, or a VLAN, and they call that choice an air gap. The separating fact is the absence of any network path. Logical separation by tags or rules can be edited back into a connection. An encrypted tunnel is a path built on purpose, which is the opposite of a gap. A scanned drive, a named carrier, and a log line are the data path that fits this design. The gap still leaves account control in place for anyone who can touch the console.',
        'r0': 'An air-gapped network has no network path to the outside or to any other network. Carried media is the only way data crosses that boundary. A firewall rule and a one-way diode are still paths, so neither choice is a gap. In the water lab the safety consoles have no cable to the office, no wireless radio, and no default route. A name lookup and a ping from a safety console both fail. Drive serial 1844 is scanned on a station that is not wired to the safety network, and the import log records the person, the serial, and the time 09:40. A maintenance laptop needs a written window and must leave when the window ends. Local accounts still matter for anyone already at the console.',
        'r1': 'There is no network path to any other network. No cable and no radio leave the room. A firewall rule is still a path. A one-way box is still a path. Data moves only on a carried drive. Scan that drive on a separate station. Log the person, the drive, and the reason. Do not email a file across the gap. A laptop with two cables is a bridge. Test with a lookup that must fail. Keep phones and extra radios out. Remove a repair laptop when the window ends.',
        'r2': 'Picture a records room with no telephone, no slot to the street, and no window that opens onto the public counter. Paper enters only in a sealed pouch that a clerk checks in a side room before anyone carries it through the inner door. The side room must not open into the records room, or the check becomes a hallway. A clerk who reads a page aloud to a colleague outside has built a path the room was built to avoid. Seeing the street through a solid wall does not create a road. The records room is the protected network, and the pouch is the only transfer this design allows. A telephone, a slot, or a second door would be a network path, which an air gap refuses.',
        'r4': 'From a protected console, try a name lookup and a ping, and write down both results. Record whether any default route, radio, or second cable exists. Do not connect a repair laptop during this pass. Read the import log and note the person, the media identifier, and the reason for the last carry. Confirm the scanning station has no wire into the protected network. Find any request to email a file across, and record that request as a break. Write the date a maintenance machine must leave. Leave local account checks in place.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to call a strict firewall, a one-way diode, or a VLAN an air gap. Those controls still leave a network path. An air-gapped network has no network path to the outside and no path to other networks. A named person carries scanned media, and that carry is logged. An encrypted tunnel is a path built on purpose. Separation by tags or rules can be edited back into a connection. The gap does not replace account control for a person at the console.',
    },
    'Logical segmentation': {
        'body': 'Logical segmentation separates traffic with a VLAN, a route, a virtual network, or a rule, even when the cables share one building. It is not a new building, and it is not an air gap. Staff and guests can share a switch and still sit in different broadcast domains with no route between them. A later rule, a wrong port setting, or a new route can reconnect what the tags currently keep apart. That possibility of a future path is what separates this control from a missing cable.\n\nThe ordinary pattern denies traffic between segments and then allows the specific flows an application needs. Users may reach a front end and may not reach a database on their own. Printers, badge readers, and building controls sit on segments that can do their job and cannot start sessions toward stores of records. Management addresses sit on a segment ordinary wall ports cannot route to, so a conference-room port is not a way to administer a switch. Each segment has a written purpose, because a segment with no name becomes a drawer where risky devices gather. Unused ports land in a VLAN that reaches nothing. A default segment that can reach everything undoes the design the day a new device is plugged in.\n\nChen maps the lobby closet at a law office. Staff computers use VLAN 20. Guest laptops use VLAN 30. One switch carries both tags, and the cables never leave the building. A guest laptop at address 10.1.30.40 opens TCP 445 toward the staff file server at address 10.1.20.15. The switch log reads deny vlan 30 to 10.1.20.15 port 445. Ticket 226 stores that line beside a diagram of one building, one switch, and no route between the tags. Chen does not describe the closet as a second building or as a network that has no path at all.\n\nDefenders test from the low-trust side and expect the probe to fail. During a rollout they log denied flows and repair a broken application with a narrow allow, rather than by deleting the boundary. They enforce the split on the network device and again on the host, so one wrong tag does not silently join the two sides. They name an owner for each segment, and that owner approves every new flow. They also separate a lab from production with distinct virtual networks, even inside one provider account. A segment boundary does not encrypt the traffic that is allowed to cross it, so a sensitive flow may still need its own protection.\n\nStudents treat a VLAN or a rule as if it were an air gap, or they think real separation requires a new building and a new set of cables. Logical separation shares the gear, and a later route can put the sides back together. An air gap has no network path to edit back into service. Encryption is a different control and does not come free with a tag. A guest password on a wireless network is not a substitute for placing that wireless network on its own segment.',
        'r0': 'Logical segmentation uses a VLAN or a rule to separate traffic on gear that still shares a building. It is not a new building and not an air gap, because a route can be added later. In the law office, staff computers use VLAN 20 and guest laptops use VLAN 30 on one switch. A guest at 10.1.30.40 is denied on TCP 445 toward the staff file server at 10.1.20.15. The switch log records that deny. Unused ports should land in a VLAN that reaches nothing. Management addresses stay off ordinary wall ports. The boundary does not encrypt the flows it allows. A test from the guest side should fail, or the separation is only a name on a diagram.',
        'r1': 'Separate traffic with tags or rules. You do not need a new building. Staff and guests can share one switch. They should not share a route. Deny cross traffic unless a flow is named. Let users reach only the public front. Keep management addresses off ordinary wall ports. Put unused ports where they reach nothing. Test from the guest side. Expect that test to fail. A later rule can reconnect the two sides. Tags do not encrypt the traffic they allow.',
        'r2': 'Picture a school that keeps the public lobby and the records office in one building with one boiler. A locked door and a painted line keep visitors out of the records hall. The shared boiler does not make the two halls one room. A teacher who leaves the door open has joined the halls again without constructing a second school. The lock can be changed later, which is different from a records office on an island with no bridge at all. The lobby and the records hall are the segments that share a building. The locked door is a tag or a rule on shared gear, not a new building and not a missing road.',
        'r4': 'Draw the segments that share one building and write the purpose of each one. Note which flows are allowed between them. Do not add a new allow during this pass. Find unused ports and record whether they sit where they reach nothing. Check that management addresses are off ordinary wall ports. Schedule a test from the guest side and write the deny you expect. Name the person who may approve the next flow. Note any allowed flow that still needs encryption, and do not treat the segment rule as that encryption.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to treat a VLAN or a rule as an air gap, or to think separation requires a new building. Logical segmentation shares the cables and the building, and a later route can reconnect the sides. An air gap has no network path that an edit can restore. Another trap assumes the boundary encrypts traffic. A tag does not encrypt anything. A guest password is not a substitute for a separate segment. The design fails when a default segment can reach everything, or when a test from the guest side succeeds.',
    },
    'Software-defined networking': {
        'body': 'Software-defined networking controls the network from software. Paths and policy are described once, reviewed, and pushed to many forwarding devices, instead of being typed differently on each switch. The control plane, where decisions are made, is separate from the data plane, where packets are forwarded, and the two planes are protected as different assets. The controller and its management interface are high value, because a stolen controller credential can rewrite the network faster than a person walking from rack to rack. The desired policy is text a peer can review.\n\nAn overlay rides on a physical underlay. A careful overlay does not excuse an underlay whose device management is open on every port. Change control belongs on controller updates, because one bad push can drop traffic or open a segment boundary in a single step. The log should show who changed which policy and which devices acknowledged the push, so an outage can be tied to a change rather than to a mystery cable. Controller administration sits on a management segment, not on the user network. Accounts that can only read a dashboard stay separate from accounts that can push. The design states what the data plane does when the controller cannot be reached, and that behavior is tested before anyone trusts the network.\n\nLeah adds a guest segment for a campus. She describes the segment in the controller and pushes one policy to 12 switches, rather than typing a private version on each switch. Ticket 118 lists acknowledgements from 11 switches. Switch 7 still forwards under the old rule, which let guest devices reach staff servers. Leah does not walk to switch 7 and type a private fix. She repairs the push, then confirms the twelfth acknowledgement before she closes the ticket. The dashboard account used for the first look cannot push. A second account, with push rights, is the one the log records.\n\nDefenders keep versions of the policy and practice the return to the last known good push. They authenticate the instructions between the controller and the devices, so a stranger cannot feed a switch a false policy. They look for devices that missed a push and still forward under old rules, because a partial rollout brings back the drift the project was meant to end. They try a new deny on a lab copy of the network before they touch the live underlay. During an incident the same push can change segmentation quickly, and that speed is safe only when the push is authenticated and logged.\n\nStudents point at one firewall with a long menu and call that box software-defined networking. The separating fact is consistent control of many forwarding devices from software, not a single appliance. Declared configuration files are the neighboring practice. A file is how a setting is reviewed, and a controller is how a network path is pushed. A file may describe a policy the controller then applies, and the file and the controller are still two objects. A design that never says what forwarding does when the controller is down is unfinished.',
        'r0': 'Software-defined networking controls the network from software. A controller holds the path and the policy, and a push sends that policy to forwarding devices instead of a different typed version on each switch. The control plane makes the decisions. The data plane forwards the packets. In the campus example one guest policy is pushed to 12 switches. Ticket 118 shows acknowledgements from 11 switches, and switch 7 still uses an old rule that lets guests reach staff servers. The fix is to repair the push, not to type a private rule on switch 7. Accounts that can push stay separate from accounts that can only read. The design must state what the data plane does when the controller is unreachable, and that behavior has to be tested.',
        'r1': 'The network map lives in software. A controller pushes one policy to many devices. Review the change before it goes out. Roll back a bad push if it breaks a path. Guard the controller like a key cabinet. Keep push rights off ordinary users. Write down what forwarding does if the controller stops. Check that every device accepted the push. Do not type a private rule on one switch. A software map does not fix sloppy wiring. Log who sent each change. Practice the rollback on a quiet day.',
        'r2': "Picture a theater where one lighting plot sets every lamp, instead of each electrician inventing a private cue at dusk. The board that stores the plot is the prize, because one edit can darken the stage or blind the house. A perfect plot does not repair a dead cable above the seats. Yesterday's plot should be ready to restore, and the crew should have practiced that restore on a quiet afternoon. Every lamp should confirm it received the new plot, because one lamp left on the old cue ruins the scene. The plot and the board stand for the controller that holds network policy. Software-defined networking is that arrangement: paths are controlled from software, and a device that missed the push is still on the old path.",
        'r4': 'Find the controller and write down who can push a change. Confirm that read-only accounts cannot push. Do not push a new policy during this pass. Read the last push and note any device that did not acknowledge it. Write what forwarding does if the controller cannot be reached. Check that device management on the physical network is not open to every port. Confirm that instructions from the controller to the devices are authenticated. If a rollback has never been practiced, write that gap down and leave the live policy as it is.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to call one firewall with many features software-defined networking. The idea is control of the network from software, with one reviewed policy pushed to many forwarding devices. The controller is the high-value asset, because a stolen push right can rewrite paths quickly. Another trap ignores what the data plane does when the controller is down. That behavior has to be chosen and tested. Declared configuration is the neighbor: files reviewed like software. A file can describe policy, and a controller decides how packets are forwarded.',
    },
    'On-premises infrastructure': {
        'body': "On-premises infrastructure is the compute, storage, and network gear an organization houses itself. The organization owns the stack: it usually patches the gear, powers it, cools it, and protects it physically. There is no provider to blame for a host nobody patched, a room nobody badged, or a disk that left the building without a wipe. The closet, the power, the cooling, and the badge reader are part of the system, because a server in an unlocked room is still exposed. Renting the room is not the same choice as handing the stack to a cloud provider.\n\nA restore that someone has actually performed belongs on the same duty list as the application. Management interfaces sit on a network visitors cannot reach, and standing next to the server is still not permission to sign in. Patch windows are staffed by the local team, including switch firmware a cloud customer never sees. Badge events sit beside system logs, so a console login can be matched to a person at the door or shown to have no matching badge. Disks are wiped or destroyed before they leave. Backups are a local duty, and a copy at another site must survive a building loss.\n\nNora runs the file server in closet B at a clinic. She patched its operating system last Tuesday, and the package log names her account rather than a provider change. The badge reader records Nora at 07:10, and the console log records her login at 07:14. Ticket 64 notes a cooling alarm from the closet sensor and a filter change the same week, because the room climate is her team's job. Disks from the retired server that shared the closet were wiped in the room, and the wipe result is in the ticket. No provider page lists this host. The clinic owns the stack from the badge reader to the patch.\n\nDefenders inventory every device in the room, including small boxes people forget, so an unknown wireless device cannot live on a shelf. They escort vendor technicians, give each visit a window, and remove remote tools a technician leaves behind. They test a power loss and write down which systems must ride through on battery and which must shut down cleanly. They separate the closet network with the same care they would give a provider network, because owning the switch does not make a flat network acceptable. When someone proposes moving the file server to a provider, they write a new duty split before any records move.\n\nStudents describe a closet server as if a cloud duty split already covered it, or they treat the share as if there were no machine to patch. The organization that houses the gear owns the stack, including patching, power, cooling, and physical protection. A provider rental moves some of those jobs, and a written matrix should say which jobs moved. A function platform with no server to patch is a different design. A hybrid layout can keep this closet as the owned half, while the provider half remains another estate with its own path and its own duties.",
        'r0': "On-premises infrastructure means the organization houses the gear and owns the stack. That ownership usually includes patching, power, cooling, and physical protection. In the clinic, the file server in closet B was patched last Tuesday under Nora's account, not under a provider ticket. The badge log shows her entry at 07:10 and the console login at 07:14. Ticket 64 records a cooling alarm and a filter change, because the room is part of the system. Disks are wiped before they leave the building. A backup copy at another site is proved by a restore, not by the mere existence of a file. Management addresses stay off the visitor network. A cloud duty split does not cover this host unless a provider truly operates that layer.",
        'r1': 'You house the gear and you own the stack. You patch, power, cool, and lock the room. There is no provider to blame for a missed patch. A server in an unlocked closet is still exposed. Wipe disks before they leave the building. Log badges beside console logins. Keep management off the visitor network. Prove a restore, not only a backup copy. Escort a vendor and remove tools left behind. Inventory the small devices people forget. Test what happens when the power drops. Own the firmware, not only the application.',
        'r2': 'Picture a workshop lathe the owner powers, oils, locks, and repairs. A neighbor who walks in because the door was left open is a failure even when the lathe is well adjusted. Spare blades sit on a shelf, and a written note explains how to start the lathe after a power cut. A visitor who comes to repair the lathe gets a timed visit and does not keep a personal key. Old measurement sheets are destroyed in the shop before they go out with the trash. The lathe stands for gear you house and maintain yourself. On-premises infrastructure means you own that stack, from the power and the lock to the patches and the physical room.',
        'r4': 'Pick one server that sits in a room you control. Write who patched it last and the date. Do not patch it during this pass. Match a recent console login to a badge event. Note who can open the room. Check whether disks are wiped before they leave. Find the backup copy that would survive a building loss, and do not delete anything. Write whether a visitor network can reach the management address. List one device in the room that the inventory missed, or write that the shelf check found none.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to apply a cloud duty split to gear you house, as if a provider patched the host, cooled the room, and badged the door. On-premises infrastructure means you own the stack. Another trap treats a file server as a function platform because the conversation is about the share and not the machine. A function platform has no server for you to patch. This machine still needs patches, power, cooling, and a lock. A hybrid design can keep the closet as the owned half. The provider half is a different estate.',
    },
    'Centralized versus decentralized design': {
        'body': 'Centralized design puts a function in one place. That place is easier to watch, easier to audit, and easier to take down. Decentralized design spreads the same function across many places. A single loss hurts less, and consistent rules become harder to keep. The choice is not a moral label. It is a decision about control and about failure. One identity service can be simple to review and can also be the outage that stops every login. Many identity services can keep a region working and can also drift into different password rules.\n\nThe mechanism shows up in where state lives and who is allowed to change it. A central directory, a central log collector, or a central key service gives one policy, one backup story, and one administration team. It also creates a queue, a database, or a site whose failure stops dependents. A decentralized layout copies the function closer to the work, sometimes with local decisions and later reconciliation. Governance then needs a way to detect drift: a scheduled comparison, a signed policy bundle, or an alert when one site no longer matches the approved baseline. High availability can add a second node beside a central service. That second node is still one logical service. It is not the same decision as letting every branch invent its own service.\n\nOn a Monday the clinic runs one directory server named id-01. The disk fills, logins fail, and the help desk ticket HD-9041 counts several hundred failed sign-ins in twenty minutes. Nurses see a domain error on every workstation. A second directory in another building would have answered those logins. The architecture note from last year had rejected that second directory because password-policy audits were already inconsistent between the clinic and a small imaging office that kept its own accounts. The outage and the audit finding are the two sides of the same choice.\n\nDefenders write down, for each function, what a single outage would stop and what a second copy would make harder to govern. They test the central failure on purpose, during a window, and they measure who cannot work. They also sample decentralized sites for policy drift instead of assuming the original image is still in place. They do not buy a pair of servers and call the design decentralized when both servers still serve one control plane. They name an owner who can force a site back to the baseline or accept an exception in writing.\n\nStudents swap this topic with high availability. A hot spare can keep a central service up. A hot spare does not spread authority. Students also swap it with a cloud region pair that exists only on a slide. If every login still depends on one directory, the design is centralized even when the web pages are copied widely. Name what you concentrated and what you spread.',
        'r0': 'Centralized design keeps one control point, such as a single directory, which is straightforward to audit and is also a single outage for every dependent login. Decentralized design places the function in many sites so one loss is survivable, and it demands a drift check because local admins will diverge. A second node behind one directory is high availability for a central service, not proof that authority was spread. Measure the choice with a planned failure of the central box and with a policy sample from each remote site. Record the owner who may accept an exception when one site must differ. The imaging office that keeps its own accounts is the decentralized case the audit has to see.',
        'r1': 'One place is easier to watch and easier to lose. Many places survive a single loss. Many places are harder to keep consistent. A second node can still be one service. Test what stops when the center fails. Sample remote sites for rule drift. Write down who may allow an exception. Do not call a spare a spread of authority. Name what you concentrated. Name what you spread. Put the choice in the design note. Review it after the next outage.',
        'r2': 'Imagine a warehouse with one dispatch desk and a radio that every forklift must call. The desk is easy to supervise, and a closed desk stops every aisle. A warehouse that lets each aisle keep its own clerk survives a closed desk, and those clerks start writing different pickup rules by Friday. Adding a second person at the same desk keeps the desk alive. It does not create a clerk in every aisle. The desk and the aisle clerks are the real choice between one control point and many. In the system design, centralizing a function makes it easier to govern and easier to lose, and spreading it helps survival while making the rules harder to keep aligned.',
        'r4': 'Pick one function, such as login or logging. Write where that function runs today. Write what stops if that one place fails. Do not add a server during this pass. Note whether a second copy would still be one service. Sample one remote site for a rule that differs. Record who may approve that difference. Leave any spare-server purchase for a later decision.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to call two servers high availability and also call them a decentralized design. A spare for one directory is still one logical service. Centralized design is easier to audit and easier to take down. Decentralized design survives a single loss and is harder to govern. A slide that draws many regions does not spread a function that every login still reaches in only one directory. Name the control point you kept and the control points you actually copied.',
    },
    'Containerization': {
        'body': 'Containerization packages an application together with the libraries that application needs, and it runs that package on a shared host kernel. The package is not a full virtual machine, and it does not bring its own kernel. Separate views of processes and files, plus limits on memory and processor time, create the boundary. A weak setting undoes the boundary. Giving the container the host control socket opens a path to the host and to every other container on that host. A broad mount of host directories hands over the host file tree no matter how tidy the rest of the package looks.\n\nImages should be scanned before they run, including base layers taken from a public registry, because a clean application build can sit on a tainted base. The process should run as an ordinary user. A need to run as root is a written exception. Images change by building a new version, not by editing a live container. A registry the organization controls should accept only signed images the build system produced. Secrets arrive from a vault when the container starts. They do not live in a layer anyone who can pull the image can read. Network rules between containers are a form of segmentation on the cluster. The cluster control account stays separate from the application identity.\n\nPatel reviews the billing image marked 1.4.2 at a retailer. The image contains the billing application and the libraries it needs, and the process is set to user 1001 rather than root. The admission log reads denied host socket mount. A second line in ticket 390 reads denied mount of the host file tree. Patel refuses an exception on either line. The node that will run the image still needs a kernel patch, and Patel assigns that patch to the host owner. The image has no kernel of its own, so the scan is not a host patch.\n\nDefenders reject a privileged setting that grants power close to the host, and they remove extra powers the platform would otherwise leave in place. They set resource limits so one noisy container cannot exhaust the node. They keep lab images away from production secrets. They rebuild when a base image has a known flaw, on a schedule the team owns, because the cluster operator does not patch the inside of the customer image. They send logs outside the container, because a log inside a replaced image disappears. They remove former staff from the registry as quickly as from the user directory.\n\nStudents call a container a virtual machine, or they call the package a wall that no mount can cross. The separating fact is the shared kernel and the libraries packed beside the application. A guest machine has its own kernel and runs on a virtual machine monitor. A container does not. The host control socket, a privileged setting, or a broad mount of host files undoes the separation even when the image was scanned and signed. Patching the node remains a host duty for the same shared-kernel reason.',
        'r0': 'Containerization packages an application with its libraries and runs that package on a shared host kernel. It is not a virtual machine, and it does not bring its own kernel. The billing image marked 1.4.2 runs as user 1001. Admission denies a mount of the host control socket and denies a mount of the host file tree. Either mount would undo the separation even if the image scan were clean. Secrets should arrive at run time from a vault. Only signed images from the organization registry should be admitted. The node kernel is patched as a host duty. A privileged setting that grants host-level power is rejected rather than waived so a release can move faster.',
        'r1': 'A container packs an application with its libraries. A container is not a full guest machine. The host kernel is shared on purpose. Do not hand a container the host control socket. A broad mount of host files breaks the wall. Scan the image, including the base layers. Run the process as a normal user. Admit only images your build system signed. Block a privileged setting that mimics host power. Put network rules between containers. Patch the host, because the kernel is shared. Fetch secrets at run time, not from the image.',
        'r2': 'Picture labeled meal boxes on one kitchen counter. Each box holds a dish and the sauce that dish needs, which is handy, and none of the boxes is a separate dining room. Trouble starts when a box is given the key to the whole kitchen or is tied open against the pantry shelves. The cook at the door refuses a box with a broken seal before it is set on the counter. The sauce recipe stays in a drawer and is added when the box is served, not printed on the lid where anyone can read it. The counter is the shared host that every box relies on. Containerization is the meal box: an application packed with its libraries, not a guest machine with its own walls.',
        'r4': 'Pick one image that is running and write the user account it uses. Do not deploy a replacement image during this pass. Check the admission log for a host control socket or a broad mount of host files. Confirm the image was signed by your build system. Note whether the base layers were scanned. Find any privileged setting and write it down for a later decision. See where secrets come from, and mark secrets packed into the image. Record who patches the host kernel. Leave the running image unchanged today.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to treat a container as a virtual machine. Containerization packages an application with its libraries and shares the host kernel. A guest machine has its own kernel and runs on a virtual machine monitor. Another trap treats the package as a wall that mounts cannot cross. The host control socket and a broad mount of host files are paths to the host. A privileged setting that grants host-level power belongs in the same miss. Signing and scanning the image do not replace a patch on the node.',
    },
    'Virtualization': {
        'body': "Virtualization runs guest machines on a virtual machine monitor, the host program that sits under every guest. Several operating systems share the hardware, and each guest brings its own kernel. The host and the monitor are the valuable layer, because a flaw there can reach every guest on that machine. Whoever operates the host patches the monitor on a stricter schedule than any single guest. On premises the owner of the hardware patches the monitor. A provider that rents the guest usually operates the monitor, and the customer still patches the guest. Whose name is on the building is a separate question.\n\nManagement of the monitor stays on a network the guests cannot reach. A compromised guest must not receive the console that can mount every disk. Snapshots are copies of live data and need the same access limits as the guest. A snapshot kept on the same disks as the live guest is not a backup, because one storage failure takes both copies. Extra hosts can restart guests after hardware loss, and that restart is not a backup. Exporting a guest disk bypasses the file permissions inside the guest, so few accounts should hold that right. Administration uses named accounts rather than one shared root password.\n\nRenee operates host 4 for a credit union. She patches the virtual machine monitor every Tuesday. The payroll guest on that host last received a kernel patch 40 days ago, and the patch record shows the gap. The management address 10.8.0.4 cannot be reached from the guest network, and a connection test from the payroll guest fails in the way the design expects. Ticket 512 describes a snapshot of the payroll guest sitting on the same disk shelf as the live files. The ticket states that the snapshot is not the backup, and it points to a copy at another site that was restored during a drill last month. Export of a guest disk on host 4 requires Renee's named account.\n\nDefenders remove sample accounts that came with a template before that guest joins the production network. They cap memory and processor time so one guest cannot starve the others or the host. They list guests that are powered off, because an unpatched guest becomes a surprise on the day someone turns it on. They authenticate a live move of a guest from one host to another, since memory and disk cross that path. They keep lab hosts away from the production monitor when a test could disturb every guest at once.\n\nStudents confuse virtualization with containerization. A container packages an application and its libraries on a shared kernel. A guest machine has its own kernel and runs on the virtual machine monitor. Students also call a snapshot a backup. The copy on the same shelf is lost when the shelf is lost. Another miss treats the guest as the layer that matters and leaves the monitor for a later month. The monitor sits under every guest, so the operator of the host patches it first. Renting a guest does not make the customer the operator of that monitor.",
        'r0': 'Virtualization runs guest machines on a virtual machine monitor, and that monitor sits under every guest. Each guest has its own kernel, which separates this design from a container that shares a host kernel. Whoever operates the host patches the monitor on a stricter schedule than any one guest. On host 4 the monitor is patched every Tuesday, while the payroll guest kernel is 40 days behind. The management address 10.8.0.4 is not reachable from the guest network. A snapshot on the same disk shelf is not a backup. The backup is the copy at another site that was restored in a drill. Disk export requires a named account. When a provider operates the monitor, the customer still patches the guest and does not claim the monitor layer.',
        'r1': 'Guest machines run on a host program. The host program sits under every guest. Patch that program before any one guest. Keep management off the guest network. A snapshot is a sensitive copy, not a backup. Use extra hosts so hardware loss can restart guests. Still copy backups away from that same storage. Log named administrators, not a shared root password. Limit who can export a guest disk. Remove guests that are powered off and unpatched. Each guest has its own kernel. A container does not give you that boundary.',
        'r2': 'Picture apartments built inside one converted warehouse. Each apartment has its own walls, its own lock, and its own small heater, and a crack in the shared foundation reaches every unit. The superintendent keeps the master keys in a closet the residents cannot enter from the hall. A photo of a room, left on the same shelf as the furniture, is not a second home in another town. Moving a resident into a spare apartment helps when one unit floods, and the family still wants the lease stored off the property. The apartments are guest machines, and the foundation is the virtual machine monitor under all of them. Virtualization treats that foundation as the layer to patch first, and a photo on the same shelf is a snapshot rather than a backup.',
        'r4': 'Write the host name and the date the virtual machine monitor was last patched. Compare that date with the oldest guest kernel. Do not patch anything during this pass. Check whether the management address answers from a guest network. Find snapshots stored on the same disks as the live guest, and do not delete them yet. Note who can export a guest disk. List guests that are powered off. Record whether a restore from another site was ever performed. Leave live guest moves unchanged until a later change.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to patch the guest and leave the virtual machine monitor for later. The monitor sits under every guest, so the host operator patches it first. Another trap calls a snapshot a backup. A snapshot on the same disks shares one failure with the live guest. Containerization is the neighbor: libraries on a shared kernel, not a guest with its own kernel. Renting a guest does not make you the operator of the monitor. In that rental you patch the guest, and the provider owns the monitor.',
    },
    'Internet of things': {
        'body': 'People call the small networked objects in a building the internet of things. A camera, a badge reader, a sensor, or a thermostat fits that name when it joins a network, keeps a login, and ships with a weak default password. Updates arrive rarely, and the object may stay on the wall for years after the installer has gone. The exposure is a networked thing with a login and a long quiet life, not the fact that the object is small.\n\nThe vendor often prints one password in a manual or stamps the same password on every unit, so anyone who can read the manual can sign in. Memory on the device is small, so the device rarely runs the protective software a laptop runs. Firmware may arrive only a few times in the life of the product. Many units also call the vendor on their own, which opens a path from a closet to the internet even when nobody is touching a screen. Expect a weak default and a quiet outbound call before you expect a clever alert.\n\nMaya, who handles facilities at a clinic, unpacks a thermostat for the lobby. The paper manual lists the password admin. Maya will not join that thermostat to the office network until the password is replaced with a unique value stored beside the other building accounts. The thermostat then sits on a segment that can reach only the building controller. The firewall records deny TCP 443 from 10.20.4.15 to 198.51.100.20. The denied line shows the thermostat calling the vendor, which is the result Maya wanted.\n\nA defender writes every unit into the inventory, including cameras a facilities vendor mounted, because an unknown camera is still a computer. Published passwords get changed, unused services get turned off, and inbound administration from the internet stays closed. When the vendor no longer ships firmware, the defender isolates the device and writes a replacement date. Detection is an outbound call to a destination that is not on the short allow list, or a login that still matches the manual. The segment, the unique password, and the replacement date are the mitigation. Size alone does not make the object harmless.\n\nStudents mix these devices up with embedded systems. An embedded system is a computer built into a product, and that computer may never have a network address at all. The separating fact is the network plus the weak default. A door board that never speaks on the network is embedded. A thermostat that joins wireless with a password from a manual is an internet-of-things device. Industrial control is the other neighbor. Those systems run physical machines, and a casual restart can be unsafe. A lobby thermostat does not become an industrial controller because someone bolted it to a wall.',
        'r0': 'Small networked devices often leave the factory with a password printed in a manual and with firmware the vendor rarely replaces. Treat calls to a vendor on TCP port 443 as expected, and allow only the destinations the deployment needs. Place the devices on a segment that can reach their controller and cannot open sessions to file servers or identity stores. Change the published password before the device joins a shared network, and store a unique value per unit rather than one sticker password for the fleet. Inventory cameras and sensors a facilities vendor mounts. If signed firmware has stopped, set a replacement date and keep the device isolated until then. A lobby thermostat with a manual password is this problem. A process controller that can injure someone on a casual restart belongs to industrial practice instead.',
        'r1': 'These are small devices on a network. They often ship with a weak password. The password may be printed in a manual. Change that password before the device joins. Give each unit its own password. Put the devices on their own segment. They should not reach files or identity systems. Block management from the open internet. Watch for calls to unexpected places. Replace a device that cannot be updated. Write down who owns each device. A camera still counts as a computer. Small does not mean safe. Test that the device cannot reach records.',
        'r2': 'The clinic kitchen fills up with small appliances, each with a sticker code, and several phone the maker as soon as they are plugged in. Drug cabinet keys do not live in that kitchen, and a toaster may not open the cabinet. Staff change every sticker code, put the appliances on their own circuit, and write down each box a vendor leaves on the counter. A unit the maker no longer repairs is removed and replaced. A test tries to reach the medicine cabinet from the toaster outlet, and success means the wiring is wrong. Those appliances are the small networked devices, and their own circuit is the segment that keeps them away from records. Changing the sticker code before the appliance may use the office outlet is the password change required before the device joins.',
        'r4': 'Look at every small networked device, including cameras a vendor mounted. Write down the owner, the segment, and where the password is stored. Change each published password before the device joins an office segment. Do not join the device to the office network yet if the manual password is still set. Keep the devices on a segment that cannot start sessions toward records. Block inbound management from the internet. Write a replacement date when firmware updates have stopped. Do not install a heavy agent the device cannot run.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to treat every small device as an industrial controller or as an ordinary server. Industrial control runs physical machines, and a casual restart can be unsafe. They join a network with a weak default and seldom receive updates, so the work is a segment, a unique password, and replacement when firmware stops. A manual password still works from inside the building. Embedded gear is the neighbor that may have no network address at all.',
    },
    'Industrial control systems': {
        'body': "Industrial control systems run physical processes such as pumps, valves, heaters, and conveyors. Availability and safety outrank a casual reboot or an aggressive scan. The plant is not a general office network with a different wallpaper. A wrong packet can move a real machine. Engineers treat uptime and safe state as the first questions, and they treat confidentiality as a requirement that must not be pursued by knocking the process over.\n\nThese systems are usually described in levels. Field devices and controllers sit close to the equipment. Human-machine interfaces and supervisory servers sit above them. The office network should meet that stack only through a controlled boundary, often a demilitarized zone where historians and jump paths are explicit. Controllers speak purpose-built protocols. Modbus on TCP port 502 is a common example, and it was not designed as a hardened office protocol. Many devices cannot tolerate a surprise restart, and some cannot run extra security agents. Segmentation, monitored paths, and a change window tied to production are the ordinary controls. An air gap is a stronger choice when there is truly no routable path, and a firewall rule is not that gap.\n\nThe plant engineer refuses a vulnerability probe of the fill-line controller while a batch is running. That controller exchanges Modbus with the operator screen on TCP port 502. Last quarter a similar probe reset a pump, and the incident note OT-17 still says the line halted for forty minutes. The change ticket for Sunday at 02:00 allows a passive copy of traffic and forbids an aggressive scan until the line is idle. The engineer writes the allowed source address of the test station into the ticket so a laptop on the office network is not the thing that speaks to the controller.\n\nDefenders keep an asset list of controllers, firmware versions, and the protocols they speak. They segment the plant from office workstations and from casual remote tools. They collect passive records from a tap rather than probing live controllers during production. They patch on the vendor's safe procedure, often with the vendor present, after a backup of the logic and a plan to return to a known program. They treat a laptop that has visited the internet and then plugs into a controller as a serious event. They practice who is allowed to stop a process, because stopping can be safer than scanning and can also be a production incident of its own.\n\nStudents confuse these systems with a real-time operating system. A controller may run a real-time operating system so answers arrive on time. The industrial system is the process, the controllers, and the safety requirement. The operating system is one component. Students also treat a routine office scan as harmless. On this network a scan can be an unsafe act. Do not poke a live process to satisfy a weekly office checklist.",
        'r0': 'Industrial control systems run physical equipment, so safety and availability outrank a casual reboot. A common supervisory path is Modbus on TCP port 502 between a controller and an operator station, and that protocol does not bring modern authentication with it. Keep the plant levels separated from the office, with an explicit boundary rather than a flat route. Prefer a passive copy of traffic during production, and schedule any active probe for an idle window with a named source. Firmware changes follow the vendor procedure after the control logic is backed up. An extra agent that is normal on a laptop can be unacceptable on a controller that has no spare cycles and no safe restart.',
        'r1': 'These systems move real machines. Safety and uptime come first. Do not reboot a controller to test it. Keep the plant network apart from the office. Watch copies of traffic while the line runs. Save active probes for an idle window. Back up the control logic before a change. A laptop from the internet does not plug in here. Name who is allowed to stop a process. A firewall rule is not a missing cable. Office scan habits can be unsafe here.',
        'r2': "Picture a water plant where valves and pumps move real flow. Operators stand at a panel that speaks to those machines through a private hallway. Office staff do not wander that hallway with a clipboard of surprise tests while water is moving. A careful inspection is booked for a quiet hour, with the panel's program copied first and a person who knows how to return the valves to a safe position. The hallway can have a window for watching. It should not be a door from the public lobby. Those machines and that hallway are the industrial system: availability and safety outrank a casual poke, and the office network stays on the other side of a deliberate boundary.",
        'r4': 'List controllers, firmware, and the protocols they speak. Trace the path from those controllers to the office. Write down any route that should not exist. Do not run an active scan during production. Schedule passive watching first. Record who may stop a process. Note the backup of control logic before any firmware visit. Write the idle window when an active probe would be allowed.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to treat a plant controller like an office server that can be rebooted or aggressively scanned at noon. Availability and safety come first. Another trap swaps the whole industrial system for the real-time operating system inside one controller. Timing promises are a separate topic. A firewall between office and plant is not an air gap. An air gap has no routable path. Passive collection during a batch is the safer evidence step.',
    },
    'Real-time operating systems': {
        'body': 'A real-time operating system promises timing. A late answer can be a failure even when the answer is numerically correct. Ordinary operating systems try to be fast and will pause a task for a disk, a driver, or a background scan. A real-time system schedules work so a deadline is part of correctness. Missing the deadline means the valve, the brake, or the sample was late. Speed on average is not the same property.\n\nThe mechanism is a scheduler that knows priorities and deadlines. A control task is given a priority that preempts housekeeping. Designers measure worst-case execution, not only the typical run, because the rare stall is the stall that misses the deadline. Priority inversion, where a low-priority task holds a lock a high-priority task needs, is a classic way to blow the timing. Extra agents, verbose logging, and unbounded network calls steal the cycles the deadline assumed. Many real-time systems run on small hardware with no room for a full office security suite. The safe pattern is a minimal image, a measured budget for every added task, and a watchdog that resets the controller when a deadline is missed. That reset is a safety behavior and also an availability event.\n\nA packaging line must close a valve within ten milliseconds of a sensor trip. An administrator installed a file-inventory agent that was normal on clinic laptops. The trace from the controller shows the control task arriving at fourteen milliseconds and the watchdog resetting the board. The computed valve position was correct. The batch still failed because the answer was late. Ticket RT-33 records the agent removal and a rule that no package is added until the timing budget is remeasured. The log line operators keep is "deadline miss 14 ms, watchdog reset."\n\nDefenders ask, before any software is added, whether the platform is real-time and what the deadline is. They refuse to judge health by average CPU alone. They keep the vendor image intact except for changes that have a timing test. They segment the controller so it is not asked to defend itself with heavy host tools. They alert on watchdog resets as process incidents, not as routine reboots to ignore. They separate the conversation about patching a general server from the conversation about a controller that cannot spare jitter.\n\nStudents hear "real-time" and think "fast workstation." A fast laptop can still pause a task for a tenth of a second and remain fine for mail. A controller with a ten-millisecond deadline cannot. Students also merge this topic into industrial control systems. The plant is the process and the safety case. The real-time operating system is the timing promise inside a computer that may sit in that plant or in a medical device. On time matters as much as correct.',
        'r0': 'A real-time operating system treats a deadline as part of correctness. A valve that must close within ten milliseconds has failed if the right answer arrives at fourteen milliseconds. The scheduler preempts lower work, and designers budget worst-case execution rather than average speed. Priority inversion and extra agents are common ways to miss that budget. A watchdog reset after a deadline miss is both a safety action and an incident to record. Do not load a laptop inventory agent onto that controller until the timing run is repeated. This is not the same claim as "the computer is quick." Jitter is the measurement that matters. ',
        'r1': 'On time matters as much as a correct answer. A late right answer can still fail. Average speed is not a deadline. The scheduler protects the control task. Extra programs can steal the spare time. Measure the worst case, not the usual case. A watchdog reset means a deadline was missed. Do not add tools before a timing test. Keep heavy laptop agents off these boxes. Segment the device if it cannot defend itself. Write the deadline in the change ticket. Treat a reset as a process incident.',
        'r2': 'Picture a railroad crossing where the gate must be down before the train reaches the road. A correct decision to lower the gate, delivered after the train has passed, is a failed decision. The signal cabin runs a strict order of tasks so the gate motor is never waiting behind a long chore. Adding a new chore without timing the cabin again is how the gate starts to lag. A second person with a stopwatch is more useful here than a person who only says the cabin feels quick. That stopwatch is the real-time promise: the system fails when the answer is late, even if the answer would have been right on a slower schedule.',
        'r4': 'Find systems that advertise a deadline. Write the deadline in milliseconds beside the hostname. List every program added since the vendor image. Do not install a new agent during this pass. Ask for the last watchdog reset and the timestamp. Note whether worst-case timing was remeasured after the last change. Separate these boxes from ordinary server patch lists. Record the watchdog text if a reset already happened.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to treat real-time as a synonym for a fast ordinary computer. A late correct answer is still a failure when the deadline is part of the job. Another trap folds this operating system into the entire industrial process. The process and the safety case are the industrial system. The timing promise is the operating system property. Extra agents that are normal on a laptop can break the deadline. Ask for the deadline and the worst case, not the average load.',
    },
    'Embedded systems': {
        'body': 'An embedded system is a computer built into a product. The computer exists to run that product, such as a door, a pump display, or a badge reader, and it often has little memory left for extra security software. This is not a desktop that someone pushed into a cabinet. The product and the computer ship as one object, and the protection plan has to fit that limit.\n\nFirmware is the program stored on the board. An update replaces that image, sometimes only through a vendor tool, and a failed write can leave the unit dead. Debug ports and a serial console may still be open from the factory. The same password may be stamped into every unit of one model. Because the board cannot host a full anti-malware suite, the useful controls sit around it: a network segment, a tracked firmware revision, and closed debug ports. When the platform can check a signature at boot, an unsigned field update should not be allowed to run.\n\nLuis keeps the asset record for the server-room door controller. The record lists hardware revision 12 and firmware 4.2.1. The board cannot run an endpoint agent, so the controller sits on its own segment and is not permitted to open a session to the file servers. During a review Luis finds the serial console still enabled, plus a vendor account named service with the password printed on a sticker inside the panel. Luis closes the console, removes the account, and writes both facts on the ticket before the unit returns to service. A later firewall line shows a denied attempt from 10.30.2.8 to the file server on TCP port 445. That denied line shows the segment blocking the session.\n\nDefenders inventory hardware revision and firmware revision together, because two boxes that look alike can hide different risk. They verify firmware when the vendor signs it, and they test an update on a spare unit, including a power cut in the middle of the write. Keys are wiped before repair or disposal. One password is not reused across the fleet. Phone applications and cloud accounts that can open the same door belong in the same review, because those accounts carry the privilege of the board.\n\nStudents call every small board an internet-of-things problem. The separating fact is the shape of the limit. Internet-of-things work centers on a networked device with a weak default and rare updates. An embedded system is a computer built into a product, and the board may have no room for extra software even after the network path is already closed. A laptop on the same desk is not embedded, because the laptop was not built into the door and the laptop can run ordinary tools. Real-time systems are a further neighbor. They fail when an answer is late, even if the answer is correct. An embedded board may or may not owe that kind of deadline.',
        'r0': 'A computer built into a product usually has too little memory for a full anti-malware suite, so the compensating controls are segmentation, signed firmware, and a tracked version. Record hardware revision and firmware revision on the same asset line, as in hardware revision 12 with firmware 4.2.1, because identical cases can hide different boards. Close serial consoles and factory accounts before the unit enters service, and do not stamp one password into every unit. Test an image on a spare, including a power cut halfway through the write, before the image reaches the live fleet. A door controller that must not open sessions to file servers should be proven with a denied attempt on TCP port 445. Wipe keys before repair or disposal. Phone applications that can command the same door are part of the device.',
        'r1': 'This is a computer built into a product. There is little room for extra security tools. The maker stores the program as firmware. Track the firmware version with the hardware version. Close factory debug ports before real use. Do not use one password on every unit. Put the device on its own segment. Test an update on a spare unit first. Include a power cut in that test. Wipe stored keys before repair or disposal. Review phone apps that can command the device. A laptop on the desk is a different kind of computer. Looks alone do not prove two units match.',
        'r2': 'The oven in the clinic kitchen has a small computer in the door that times every bake. There is no shelf in that door for a toolbox or a stack of manuals. Only the shop that built the oven should replace the little board, and the kitchen writes down which board sits in which oven. A panel left open from the factory would let a stranger change the timing, so the crew screws that panel shut before the oven is used. When a board goes out for repair, the crew clears the stored codes first. Two black ovens can hide different boards. That oven computer is an embedded system, a computer built into the product with no room for extra software. The written board number and the shut panel are firmware tracking and a closed factory hatch.',
        'r4': 'Look at the hardware revision and the firmware revision on the same ticket. Write down any open serial console, factory account, or sticker password. Do not load new firmware onto the live fleet yet. Test the image on a spare unit, and cut power once during the write. Close debug ports and remove factory accounts before production use. Segment the device away from file servers and identity stores. Wipe keys before repair or disposal. Do not reuse one technician password across the fleet.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to grade every small board as an internet-of-things device. Those devices are a network join plus a weak default and rare updates. An embedded system is a computer built into a product, often with no room for a full protective suite. A laptop is not embedded merely because it sits near a door. Real-time practice is the other neighbor: a late answer can fail even when the value is correct. An embedded board owes that deadline only when the product promised timing.',
    },
    'High availability': {
        'body': 'High availability means the service stays up when one part fails, because another instance is already ready to carry the work. The goal is continued service during the failure, not a claim that one server is unusually strong. Users keep working while one node is down. A backup on another disk does a different job. A backup is how the team rebuilds after data is lost. This design tries to avoid the visible outage in the first place.\n\nAt least two nodes sit behind a device that balances the load. A health check asks the application a real question, not merely whether a port is open, and a failed check stops new work from landing on the sick node. The survivor must have capacity for the real load, or the failover is only a drawing. The two nodes should not share one power strip, one switch, and one disk shelf unless the design openly accepts that shared fate. Heartbeats and quorum decide which side is primary, so a split-brain event does not let both sides write and corrupt the data. The front device, the database, and the sign-in service belong in the same picture. A second web node does nothing useful if sign-in has only one box.\n\nNoah runs the clinic portal on two web nodes. During a Tuesday test Noah stops the application on node web-02. The front device records that the health check failed for web-02 and removes web-02 from the pool. New sessions continue on web-01, and the portal page still loads. The ticket notes about 200 sessions carried by the remaining node, then a clean return when the stopped application starts again. The return matters as much as the jump. Some pairs fail only when the first node comes back and both nodes try to be primary.\n\nDefenders put the failover test on a calendar, size the survivor for the offered load, and separate power and network. They protect the health signal so a stranger cannot mark a healthy node dead with a forged packet. They write down whether the pair is active-passive or active-active. A second install is not highly available until someone has watched both the jump and the return. Dependencies outside the pair get named as well, or the picture stays incomplete.\n\nStudents treat a backup as this design. The separating fact is purpose and timing. A backup restores lost data after the service has already been down. This design keeps the service up through the failure of a part, and only when the second instance can carry the load. A device that balances the load in front of a single node is also not this design, because no second instance is ready. A real-time deadline is a different neighbor. A failover can be correct and still be too slow for a controller that must answer in milliseconds.',
        'r0': 'Keep a service up through failure of one part by having another instance ready, with capacity for the real load. Two web nodes behind a device that balances the load are the basic shape, and a health check must see a wedged application rather than only an open port. In one clinic test the surviving node carried 200 sessions after web-02 left the pool. Separate power and network so one strip cannot kill the pair. Use quorum so a split does not create two primaries and corrupt data. Name active-passive or active-active, and test both the failover and the return. A backup restores data after loss and does not keep the page up during the outage. A real-time deadline can still be missed by a failover that is correct but slow. Include the database and sign-in in the same test.',
        'r1': 'Another copy is ready to carry the work. The service should stay up when one part fails. A backup is a different idea. A backup helps after the outage. The survivor must be big enough for real load. Ask the application a real health question. Do not trust a mere open port. Keep the two copies from sharing one power source. Do not let both copies act as primary. Test the jump and the return. Include the database and sign-in. Watch the test before you trust the drawing. A slow handoff can miss a tight deadline.',
        'r2': "The clinic front desk is staffed by two people so the line keeps moving. When one person steps out, the other is already at the counter and knows the work. They do not share one chair and one phone in a way that a single spill stops both. Once a month the lead sends one person out on purpose, watches whether the line still moves, and watches the return so both do not grab the same chart. A folder of yesterday's papers in the back room can rebuild a lost chart later. That folder does not serve the person at the counter during the gap. The second person at the counter is the other instance that keeps the service up when one part stops. The folder is a backup, and a backup does not by itself keep the line moving.",
        'r4': 'Look at the pair, the health check, and the device that sends users to a live node. Write down active-passive or active-active, and write the capacity of the survivor. Do not pull a live node yet. Book a window, stop one node, and record whether sessions continue. Bring the node back and record whether both sides try to be primary. Do not delete backup copies yet. This design does not restore lost data. Separate power and network before calling the pair ready.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to answer backup whenever a question mentions survival. A backup restores data after the service has already been down. High availability keeps the service up through failure of a part only if another instance is ready and has carried the load in a test. One node behind a sharing device is not a pair. Active-passive and active-active both need a test of the jump and the return. A correct failover can still miss a real-time deadline. Two primaries during a split can corrupt data.',
    },
    'Architecture tradeoffs': {
        'body': 'Architecture tradeoffs are the explicit give-and-take among availability, resilience, cost, speed, scale, ease of deployment, who holds the risk, recovery, and whether a thing can be patched at all. Every design gives something up. The useful record names the thing that was given up, not only the benefit that was bought. A cheap single server is easy to deploy and is also a single outage and a single place to patch. A pair of sites raises resilience and also raises cost, operational skill, and the ways the two copies can disagree.\n\nThe mechanism is a decision note tied to a workload, with the rejected options still visible. Availability asks whether users can work during a failure. Resilience asks whether the design absorbs that failure without a heroic night. Cost is money and staff time. Speed may mean response time or the speed of a change. Scale asks what happens when demand grows. Ease of deployment asks whether a new environment can be rebuilt from a known description. Risk holder asks whether the organization or a provider carries a given failure. Recovery asks about the time and the point you can return to. Patchability asks whether the platform can take a fix without breaking a safety constraint or a vendor support rule. Skipping any of those leaves a surprise, and surprises are how a cheap choice becomes an incident.\n\nThe appointment site runs on one virtual machine because a single image deploys in an afternoon and the monthly cost fits the pilot budget. The architecture note ARCH-12 lists the give-up in plain lines: a disk failure ends the site, patching needs a reboot window, and there is no second site if the provider zone fails. The product owner signed that note. When the disk filled on a Friday, ticket INC-77 cited ARCH-12 instead of treating the outage as a mystery. The rebuild took four hours because backups existed and the note had already said four hours was the accepted recovery.\n\nDefenders refuse a design review that lists only benefits. They ask what failed option was set aside and why. They revisit the note when the pilot becomes the production system, because a tradeoff accepted for a trial is often unacceptable for payroll. They connect the note to backup tests and to patch windows so the accepted pain is real and not a sentence nobody budgeted. They do not hide a single point of failure behind a product name. They name the person who accepts the residual risk.\n\nStudents look for a universally best architecture. The exam question is usually which goal the design sacrificed. Students also confuse a tradeoff with a control catalog. Choosing a lock is a control choice. Saying the cheap lock is easy to fit and easy to defeat is the tradeoff. Name what you gave up.',
        'r0': 'Score a design on availability, resilience, cost, response speed, change speed, scale, ease of deployment, risk holder, recovery time, and patchability. A single virtual machine can win on cost and deployment time and lose on availability, because one disk ends the service and one reboot window applies the patch. Write the rejected option beside the chosen one so the give-up stays visible when the pilot becomes production. Tie the accepted recovery time to a tested backup, as in a four-hour rebuild that was signed before the outage. A product name does not erase a single point of failure. The owner of the residual risk should be a person, not a slide.',
        'r1': 'Every design gives something up. Write down the loss, not only the gain. A cheap single server is easy to launch. That server is also one outage. A second site costs money and skill. Ask who holds the risk. Ask how long recovery takes. Ask whether the thing can be patched. Revisit the note when a pilot goes live. Do not hide a single failure behind a product name. Name the person who accepts the leftover risk. Benefits alone are an incomplete review.',
        'r2': 'Picture a footbridge the town can build in a weekend with cheap timber. People cross it sooner, and a flood takes it down, and replacing a plank means closing the path. A steel bridge costs more, takes longer, needs a crew who can inspect cables, and stays up when the timber bridge would be gone. The town council writes which bridge it refused and which pain it accepted. A pretty drawing of only the chosen bridge hides the decision. The timber and the steel are the architecture tradeoff: name the availability, the cost, the recovery, and the skill you gave up, and name the person who accepted that bargain.',
        'r4': 'Select one production workload. List availability, cost, recovery, and patchability for the current design. Write the option that was rejected. Do not change the design in this pass. Name the person who accepted the leftover risk. Check whether a tested restore matches the recovery time in the note. Flag the note if the workload has grown past a pilot. Add patchability if that column is missing.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to pick a design because it is best in the abstract. The question is what the design gave up. A single cheap server can be easier to deploy and still be one outage and one patch window. High resilience is not free. It spends money, skill, and sometimes simplicity. Another trap lists controls without saying which goal lost. Name availability, resilience, cost, speed, scale, deployment, risk holder, recovery, and whether the thing can be patched.',
    },
    'Device placement': {
        'body': 'Device placement is the choice of where a control sits on the path. The box has to see the traffic it is meant to judge, and it should not spend its time on traffic it was never meant to judge. A sensor bought to watch staff reaching file servers learns nothing useful if the sensor is mounted on the outside of the firewall. That outside spot sees internet noise rather than the intranet. The decision is about the path the packets take.\n\nTraffic either flows through the device or a copy of the traffic is sent to the device. If the return packets take a different route, the device sees half the conversation and can misread it. An enforcing device must sit on the path that should be blocked, with no quiet bypass around it. A watching device can sit on a copy, so a crash of the watcher does not drop clinic traffic. Management interfaces belong on a management segment, not on the face that sees the raw internet. A proxy filters only the clients whose paths are forced through the proxy. A control that understands web requests has to sit where those requests are visible, not in front of a database protocol the control cannot parse.\n\nSam holds a ticket that says the new sensor should notice a workstation copying files inside the clinic. Sam first connects the sensor outside the firewall. An hour of sensor log shows scans from the internet and no file-server sessions. Sam moves the sensor to a tap on the internal server switch. The next line shows workstation 10.4.8.12 connecting to file server 10.4.1.20 on TCP port 445. That session is the traffic the purchase was meant to see. The first spot was not a tuning problem. The first spot was the wrong place on the path.\n\nDefenders draw the path, mark where each control sits, and name the traffic that control must see. They generate a known session and confirm that the session appears, or does not appear, in the expected log. They look for bypass routes, asymmetric routes, and management interfaces left on the public side. Move the box before you add rules that can never match a flow the box does not see.\n\nStudents confuse this choice with security zones. A zone groups systems that share a trust level, such as guest, user, and server. Placement is where one control sits so the right packets pass it. A sensor can sit in the correct zone and still be on the wrong side of a switch, seeing nothing useful. Inline versus tap is the other neighbor. Inline means the device is in the forwarding path, so the device can block and can also break the path if the device fails. A tap only copies. A tap in the wrong spot still copies the wrong traffic. The spot on the path is the fact that separates placement from those neighbors.',
        'r0': 'Put each control where the traffic it must judge actually passes, and keep it off traffic it must not see. A sensor left outside the firewall will log internet scans and will miss TCP port 445 from a workstation to a file server. A tap on the internal server switch can show that session, for example 10.4.8.12 to 10.4.1.20. If the return path differs, the control sees only half the conversation. Enforcement has to sit on the path with no bypass. A pure watcher can take a copy so a crash does not drop the flow. Management interfaces belong on a management segment. A proxy no client is forced to use does not filter. Confirm placement with one known session in the expected log before you tune rules.',
        'r1': 'Put the control where the right traffic passes. A box on the wrong path sees the wrong flow. Outside the front gate is a bad spot for inside traffic. A copy lets you watch without standing in the way. A blocker has to stand on the path itself. Leave no quiet way around a blocker. Watch for traffic that returns by another route. Keep management off the public side. Force clients through a proxy, or the proxy sees nothing. Generate a known session and find it in the log. Move the box before you write new rules. A group of similar systems is a different choice.',
        'r2': 'A warehouse has several dock doors, and one clerk is paid to check crates from the partner clinic. A clerk at the street door sees passing trucks and misses the partner crates, which use the side dock. Moving the clerk to the side dock makes the right crates visible. A second person who only writes a list, without standing in the doorway, can drop a pencil and the dock still works. The person allowed to turn a crate away must stand where the crate cannot slip through a side gate. The clerk spot is where the control sits on the path. A clerk at the street while the crates use the side dock is watching the wrong flow, and a new clipboard rule will not see crates that never pass that door.',
        'r4': 'Look at a path diagram and mark where each control sits. Write down the traffic it must see and the traffic it must not see. Generate one known session and check the expected log. Do not add blocking rules yet if that session never appears. Look for a bypass and for a management interface on the public side. Move the control onto the path that carries the traffic you care about. Leave a pure watcher on a copy when a watcher failure must not stop the flow.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to answer with a zone name when the question asks where a control sits. A zone groups systems that share trust. Placement is the spot on the path, so the sensor sees the traffic it was bought to judge. A sensor in the right zone can still sit on the wrong side of a switch. Inline versus tap is the other trap. Inline can block and can also break the path. A tap only copies, and a tap in the wrong place still copies the wrong traffic.',
    },
    'Security zones': {
        'body': 'Security zones group systems that share a trust level and a rule set. Guest, user, server, and management are the usual four in a clinic or office, and plants often add a zone for controllers. A zone is a policy bucket. It is not merely a picture of rooms. Systems in one zone are expected to need the same kinds of access and to face the same kinds of threats. Mixing a database and a lobby printer in one bucket forces the rules to satisfy the less careful member.\n\nThe mechanism is placement plus enforcement. Each zone maps to virtual networks, address blocks, or interface groups, and the policy between zones is default deny with narrow allows. User zones may reach a print service and a front-end application. They do not start sessions to database ports. Guest zones reach the internet and nothing internal. Server zones accept only the application flows. The management zone is where administrators reach switch, firewall, and hypervisor interfaces, and ordinary user addresses cannot route there. The zone boundary is only as real as the rule that blocks a new flow. A label on a diagram does not stop a packet. A later route, a wide security group, or a dual-homed laptop can stitch two zones back together.\n\nThe network engineer places printers in the device zone, VLAN 40. A test workstation in that zone tries to open TCP port 1433 on the billing database. The firewall log reads "deny src=10.40.0.15 dst=10.50.2.8 dpt=1433". Staff laptops in the user zone can reach printer port 9100 and cannot reach the database. Switch administration answers only from the management zone, VLAN 10. Ticket NET-61 records those three results. The engineer does not call the printer network trusted because the printers are owned by the clinic. Ownership is not the trust level. The printers still do not get to initiate database sessions.\n\nDefenders draw zones before they buy the next appliance, then test from the lower zone toward the higher one and expect the probe to fail. They put new and unknown devices into a quarantine zone until the device is identified. They keep management interfaces off user and guest paths. They review every new allow for both directions, because a one-way diagram often hides a return path that is wider than the application needs. They name an owner for each zone who approves new members. A zone nobody owns becomes a junk drawer.\n\nStudents confuse zones with the attack surface. The surface is every place an attacker can try. The zone is the grouping that lets you apply one rule set to systems of equal trust. Students also confuse a zone name with segmentation that has been tested. If the printer can open the database, the zones are labels. Group things that share a trust level, and prove the boundary denies the rest.',
        'r0': 'Use zones such as guest, user, server, device, and management so each group shares one trust level and one rule set. Map them to concrete networks, for example VLAN 40 for printers and VLAN 10 for administration. Default deny between zones. A printer may receive jobs on TCP 9100 and must not open TCP 1433 toward a database. Management interfaces should not answer from user or guest addresses. Prove the zone with a deny log, not with a color on a diagram. A new route or a dual-homed laptop can undo the grouping, so retest after changes. Unknown devices start in quarantine rather than in the server zone.',
        'r1': 'Group systems that share a trust level. Guests, users, servers, and managers differ. Give each group its own rules. Block by default between groups. Let users print without reaching databases. Keep management paths off guest networks. Prove the block with a test, not a drawing. Park unknown devices away from servers. Name a person who approves new members. A shared owner does not make two systems equal. Recheck after someone adds a route. Labels alone do not stop packets. Repeat the test after a network change.',
        'r2': 'Picture a hospital with a public lobby, a staff hallway, a records cage, and a small room where only facilities staff may touch the panels. People in the lobby can ask for directions. They cannot open the records cage. Staff can request a printout without being handed the panel-room key. The signs on the doors matter only because the doors actually latch. A propped door between the lobby and the cage makes the signs decorative. Those rooms are security zones. Group systems that share a trust level, and enforce different rules between the lobby, the staff hallway, the servers, and the management room.',
        'r4': 'List the zones you claim to have. Write two systems that sit in each zone. Note one flow that should be allowed and one that should fail. Do not add a new allow in this pass. Run or read a test from the lower zone. Save the deny log line. Name the owner who may approve a new member. Mark any zone that has no named owner.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to treat a zone label as a control. If a printer address can open a database port, the zones are not in force. Guest, user, server, and management are different trust levels and need different rules. Another trap confuses zones with attack surface. The surface is every place someone can try. The zone is the grouping of equal trust. Management interfaces do not belong on the guest or user path. Default deny, then allow the specific flow.',
    },
    'Attack surface': {
        'body': 'Attack surface is every place an attacker can try: listening ports, applications, accounts, exposed management pages, and physical access. It is an inventory of opportunity, not the name of an attack and not a product you buy. A smaller surface means fewer places to watch and fewer bugs that matter. Turning off three unused services shrinks the surface before any new product is purchased. The front door you like is only one item on the list.\n\nThe mechanism is enumeration followed by removal or exposure control. On a host, list listening ports and the programs behind them. On an application, list URLs, file uploads, and admin paths. On identity, list accounts, especially old administrators and shared logins. On the building, list doors, docks, and wireless that reaches the inside. Each item is either required for a named business task or it is a candidate to close. Required items still get hardening: a current patch, a strong sign-in, and a log. Unrequired items are disabled, removed, or placed where only a management zone can reach them. New projects add surface. A cloud storage bucket, a forgotten test site, and a vendor appliance with a default password are surface even when nobody drew them on the original diagram.\n\nThe analyst inventories the file server before a tool purchase. The listener list shows file sharing on TCP 445, remote desktop on TCP 3389, and an old automation console on TCP 8080 that no ticket owns. Remote desktop and the console are not used by the file-share workflow. Ticket HARD-55 closes 3389 and 8080 and records the remaining listener, 445, as accepted surface with a named owner. The same week the facilities log shows the closet door held open by a carton. That door is surface too. The analyst adds it to the same list so the conversation is not only about ports.\n\nDefenders recount the surface on a schedule and after every migration, because copied images inherit old listeners. They disable unused accounts and remove software the standard image does not need. They put administrative protocols where only the management zone can reach them. They treat a new internet name as new surface until someone shows the allow is required. They measure progress by the list getting shorter or more justified, not by the number of security products in the rack. A product that nobody feeds with a current inventory does not shrink anything.\n\nStudents call the surface the firewall, or they call a vulnerability scan the surface itself. The scan is a way to see part of the surface. The surface is the set of places. Students also think only of internet-facing ports. A local account, a wireless network, and a side door are places an attacker can try. Every window counts, not only the front door.',
        'r0': 'Attack surface is the set of opportunities: ports, applications, accounts, and physical access. On a file server the concrete set might be TCP 445 for the required share, with TCP 3389 and TCP 8080 present only because an image was never trimmed. Close the unused listeners and record the remaining one as accepted, with an owner. Repeat the count for accounts, admin URLs, wireless, and doors. A new bucket or a new DNS name adds surface even when the old diagram looks unchanged. Shrink the list before buying another product. A scanner helps you see listeners. The scanner is not the surface.',
        'r1': 'Count every place someone can try. Include ports, apps, accounts, and doors. Unused listeners should be closed. Required ones need an owner and a patch. A new website adds places to try. A side door counts as much as a port. Old admin accounts are part of the set. Buy tools after the list is shorter. A scan shows part of the set. The scan is not the set itself. Recheck after a move to a new host. Write down what you left open and why.',
        'r2': 'Picture a clinic building at night and walk the outside before you talk about alarms. Count the front door, the loading dock, the window left on a latch, and the roof hatch. Each one is a place someone can try, whether or not you have bought a camera yet. Board the hatch you never use, and put a name next to the dock because supplies still arrive there. A camera catalog does not shrink the building. Closing the unused hatch does. Those openings are the attack surface: every port, application, account, and physical way in, not only the front door you already like.',
        'r4': 'List listening ports on one important host. Mark each port as required or unused. Do not buy a new product during this pass. Close nothing until the owner confirms the unused ones. Add admin accounts and one physical door to the same list. Write a ticket for every unused item. Schedule a recount after the next migration. Count wireless entry points on the same inventory.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to treat attack surface as a product or as the scan report itself. The surface is every place someone can try: ports, apps, accounts, and physical access. Closing unused services shrinks it before a purchase. Another trap counts only internet ports and forgets local admin accounts and doors. A required service stays, with an owner. An unused listener does not stay because it was on the image. Name the place, then decide to close it or to harden it.',
    },
    'Connectivity choices': {
        'body': 'A connectivity choice decides how two systems are allowed to talk. The common options are a private circuit, a VPN, or the open internet. Each option changes who can reach the service and what a stranger on the outside can attempt. The decision is about the allowed path.\n\nA private circuit is a path the carrier provides between sites, and that path is not offered as a general internet route. A VPN builds an encrypted path across a network the clinic does not control, and a policy still has to name which systems may use the path. The open internet means a public address and a port that anyone can try. Routing and firewall rules are how the choice becomes real. If administrators can still reach a server on a public port, the private path is only a preference. Direction matters as well. A server that may open any outbound connection has chosen the internet for that direction, even when inbound administration arrives on a private circuit.\n\nNora reviews administration of the clinic servers. The firewall shows successful SSH on TCP port 22 from addresses in several countries, all aimed at 203.0.113.44. The public SSH path is the open internet, and that path is not the one the clinic wanted. Nora moves administrator access onto the private circuit between the clinic and the data center, and Nora removes the rule that allowed port 22 from any address. Staff at home use an encrypted tunnel that lands on one jump host, rather than on every server. The ticket records the removed public rule and the circuit identifier the carrier assigned. A test from a phone on the public network fails to open port 22, and Nora writes that failed test down as the proof.\n\nDefenders list each system, the path the system may use, and the path that must stay closed. Administration prefers a private path. When people outside need a tunnel, the landing place stays small and the session is logged. Check the choice again after a vendor adds a modem or a temporary public address. Encryption by itself is not the whole decision. An encrypted path into the wrong network is still the wrong network.\n\nStudents answer VPN whenever a question mentions a remote clinic. A VPN is one encrypted path. A VPN does not, by itself, decide that administration must stay off the open internet, and a VPN does not make the far end trustworthy. The separating fact is which path is allowed. How a person signs in after arrival is remote access, the neighbor topic. Remote access still needs a strong sign-in and a limited landing place. Connectivity is the earlier question of which road is even open.',
        'r0': 'Decide the allowed path before you celebrate a product name. The usual choices are a private circuit, a VPN, or the open internet. A public SSH listener on TCP port 22, such as successful logins to 203.0.113.44 from many countries, means administration is on the open internet. Move that administration onto the private circuit and remove the any-address rule for port 22. Prove the change with a failed connection attempt from a phone on the public network. A VPN can still carry a home user, but land that tunnel on one jump host and keep a policy of which hosts the user may reach next. Outbound any-to-any is a connectivity choice too, even when inbound administration is private. An encrypted path does not make the far network trustworthy and does not replace sign-in.',
        'r1': 'Choose how two systems are allowed to talk. A private line is one choice. An encrypted tunnel is another choice. The open internet is a third choice. A public door for administrators is the open internet. Prefer a private path for administration. Close the public door after the private path works. Home staff can use a tunnel to one landing host. Do not land that tunnel on every server. Write down which path each system may use. Check again after a vendor adds a new door. Encryption alone does not pick the path. The far side still needs its own sign-in.',
        'r2': 'The clinic keeps a staff corridor between the records room and the main building, and a public sidewalk runs along the street. Records clerks are supposed to use the corridor. If the sidewalk door is also propped open, the corridor is only a nicer walk. After-hours visitors can use a locked passage that ends at one desk, not a key to every room. The facilities lead writes which door each role may use, and tests from the sidewalk that the records door stays shut. A vendor who later props that door for a delivery has opened the street again. The corridor is a private circuit, the sidewalk is the open internet, and the locked passage is one encrypted path. Choosing which doors may be used, and shutting the others, is the connectivity decision.',
        'r4': 'Look at every admin path, including public addresses and vendor modems. Write down the path each system may use and the path that must stay closed. Do not remove the current admin rule yet. First prove the private circuit or the approved tunnel works. Then remove administration from any address on the internet. Land outside users on one jump host and log the session. Recheck after a temporary public address appears. Do not treat a tunnel as permission to reach every server.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to answer VPN for every question about a remote site. A VPN is one encrypted path across a network you do not control. It does not by itself close an open admin port, and it does not make the far end trustworthy. The separating fact is the allowed path: private circuit, encrypted tunnel, or open internet. Remote access is the neighbor: how a person signs in and where they land. A closed TCP port 22 still fails if a modem offers the same door.',
    },
    'Failure modes': {
        'body': 'A failure mode is the way a design behaves when a part breaks. The forms that matter here are fail open, fail closed, and fail to a reduced service. Fail open means the control stops enforcing and lets the action through. Fail closed means the control denies the action when the control cannot decide. The team has to pick that result on purpose. The hardware will do one of those things whether anyone wrote the choice down or not.\n\nControls lose power, lose the link to a policy server, or crash. A firewall that fails open passes packets it can no longer inspect. A firewall that fails closed drops those packets, which protects the inside and also stops the service. A door controller that loses power and unlocks has failed open. A lock that stays locked with the outside handle dead, while a person inside can still exit, has failed closed to the outside. Reduced service sits between those poles. A badge reader may allow only a cached list of staff while the identity server is unreachable. Life safety can require a hallway door to unlock so people can leave. That same unlock is a bad surprise on a server-room door if the team never chose it. The mode is a property of the design under failure, not a slogan on the product sheet.\n\nJordan uses a maintenance window on the server-room door. Jordan cuts power to the controller and watches. The badge reader goes dark, and the door unlocks. The camera still records the hallway. The ticket says power loss unlocks the server room, fail open, which is not the result the clinic wanted for that room. The hallway egress door is left fail open on purpose so staff can exit during a fire. The server-room hardware is then changed so a power loss does not unlock the outside handle, while the inside handle still lets a person leave. The test is repeated. The second time the outside handle stays shut. The ticket says fail closed on the outside handle when power is lost.\n\nFor each control, write the mode, the condition that triggers it, and the person who accepted it. Test by removing power or the policy link during a window, rather than waiting for an accident. Include firewalls, door hardware, and any proxy that might pass traffic when a license check fails. A spare server does not answer this question. A second server can keep a service up, and the lock can still fail open if that is how the lock is built.\n\nStudents pick high availability whenever they hear the word failure. High availability means another instance carries the work so the service stays up. A failure mode is what this control does when the control itself breaks, whether the path swings open or stays shut. A highly available door controller can still unlock when power is lost. The separating fact is the behavior during the break, not the presence of a second node. Fail open is not a defense. Fail open is the control stepping out of the way.',
        'r0': 'Name the behavior when a control loses power, loses its policy link, or crashes. Fail open passes the action, fail closed denies it, and a reduced mode may allow only a cached set. A firewall that fails open forwards packets it can no longer inspect. A firewall that fails closed drops them. Power loss that unlocks a server-room outside handle is fail open, even when a hallway exit is fail open on purpose so people can leave. After TCP port 636 to the directory fails, one reader serves a cached list of 40 badge holders and denies everyone else. That list is reduced service. Write the mode down and test it by pulling power in a window. A second server does not tell you whether the lock fails open.',
        'r1': 'Decide what happens when a control breaks. Fail open lets people or traffic through. Fail closed stops them. A reduced mode allows only a short list. A door that unlocks on power loss is fail open. A hallway exit may need that unlock. A server room should stay shut. A firewall can pass or drop when it cannot inspect. Write the choice down before the break. Test by cutting power in a planned window. A spare server does not answer this question. Two live servers can still hide a lock that opens. Fail open is the control stepping aside.',
        'r2': 'The warehouse gate has to do something when the power dies. One design lets the gate swing open so carts are not trapped in a fire. Another keeps the outside latch shut so the stock is not suddenly public, while a person inside can still push the bar and leave. A third opens only for names already on the night list. The team writes the choice on the gate card before they need it. They test by cutting power in a quiet hour and watching the latch. The side exit swings open so people can leave. A gate that swings open on power loss has failed open, and a gate that stays latched has failed closed. Writing that result down, and testing it, is how a team handles failure modes.',
        'r4': 'Look at each firewall, door control, and proxy. Write the behavior on loss of power or loss of the policy link, and write who accepted it. Do not swap hardware yet. Cut power in a maintenance window and record what the control actually does. Repeat the test after any change. Leave hallway exits able to open so people can leave. Do not assume a second server defines the mode. Do not treat fail open as protection.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to answer high availability for every failure. High availability means another instance carries the work so the service stays up. A failure mode is what this control does when it breaks: fail open, fail closed, or a reduced service. A highly available controller can still unlock on power loss. Fail open lets the action through, and it is not a defense. A hallway exit may fail open so people can leave. The same unlock on a server-room handle can be an accident. Test the mode rather than trusting the product name.',
    },
    'Active versus passive devices': {
        'body': 'An active device can block or change traffic. A passive device only observes. That difference decides whether a failure or a false judgment can interrupt the clinic, and whether a bad packet can be stopped in the moment or only written down. Firewalls, prevention engines, and proxies are active when they sit where they can drop, reset, or rewrite. A sensor on a copy of the traffic is passive. It can alert. It cannot reach into the forwarding path and stop the packet it just saw.\n\nThe mechanism is the path. If the device is in line, every session depends on it, and it may add delay or become a single point of failure. That same position lets it block. If the device receives a mirror or a tap copy, the original frames continue even when the device is powered off. The copy can be incomplete if the mirror drops frames under load, so a passive record is evidence with a limit, not a perfect film. Active devices need a fail mode: fail closed stops traffic when the device dies, and fail open lets traffic through unsafely. Passive devices do not choose that fail mode for the production path, because they were never on it. Tuning matters more on an active preventer, because a wrong signature blocks a real user.\n\nDuring a test the inline prevention appliance sees a known-bad pattern and writes "action=drop" while the client receives a reset. The passive sensor on a mirror of the same link writes a record of the pattern and does not change the outcome. When the engineer pulls the passive sensor\'s power, the application stays up and the link counters keep climbing. Ticket MON-19 records both results so nobody later claims the mirror port was protecting the billing site. The inline appliance remains the thing that can say no. The mirror remains the thing that can remember.\n\nDefenders decide, flow by flow, whether they need a block or a record. They put active devices where a block is worth the failure risk, and they document fail open or fail closed. They put passive devices where an outage of the monitor must not become an outage of care. They do not stack so many inline boxes that a single software crash stalls a hospital path. They review false positives on active systems the same week, because staff will demand a bypass if the block is clumsy. They keep clocks aligned so a passive record can be matched to an active drop.\n\nStudents swap this topic with inline versus tap. Inline and tap describe where the monitor sits. Active and passive describe whether the device can change the traffic. A tap is one way to feed a passive device. An inline preventer is one kind of active device. A firewall that rewrites addresses is active even when nobody calls it a monitor. An active device can stop traffic. A passive device only watches.',
        'r0': 'Active devices sit where they can drop, reset, or rewrite, so they can stop a bad packet and can also interrupt legitimate traffic. Passive devices receive a copy from a mirror or a tap and can alert only. Pulling power from a passive sensor should leave link counters climbing. An inline preventer should be able to show action=drop during a controlled test. Choose fail closed or fail open for every active box, and write that choice down. A mirror under heavy load can drop copied frames, so a missing passive alert is not proof the event never happened. Tune active signatures quickly. A clumsy block invites a bypass.',
        'r1': 'One kind of device can stop or change traffic. The other kind only watches. A watcher should not be able to stall care. A blocker can also break a good session. Decide which failure you accept. Test a block with a harmless known pattern. Pull the watcher and confirm the link stays up. Write down fail open or fail closed. Review false blocks the same week. Match the watch record to the block record. Do not call a copy a barrier. Do not call a barrier a harmless camera.',
        'r2': 'Picture a bridge with a guard who can lower the gate, and a painter on the bank who sketches every truck. The guard can stop a truck and can also stop the town by leaving the gate down. The painter can show you the truck later and cannot stop it, and the town still crosses if the painter goes home. Choose a guard where you truly need a stop, and accept that the gate can jam. Choose a painter where you need a memory and the road must stay clear. The guard is the active device, able to block or change the flow. The painter is the passive device, limited to observation.',
        'r4': 'Pick one security device on a production path. Write whether it can drop or only record. Do not move it during this pass. If it can drop, note fail open or fail closed. If it only records, confirm the path stays up when the device is off. Save one log line that shows a drop or a copy. Ask who tunes false blocks.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to say a passive copy can block. A passive device only observes. An active device can block or change traffic, and it can also become a point of failure. Another trap treats this as only a story about taps. Inline versus tap is about placement and whether the monitor sits in the path. Active versus passive is about the power to alter traffic. A firewall that rewrites addresses is active. A mirror that feeds an alert console is passive.',
    },
    'Inline versus tap monitoring': {
        'body': "Inline monitoring sits in the forwarding path. It can inspect and it can block, and it can add delay or become a single point of failure. A tap copies traffic without sitting in that path. The copy feeds a sensor. The original frames do not depend on the sensor staying alive. Inline inspection can block. A tap only copies traffic. Choosing between them is a placement decision with a failure consequence, not a brand decision.\n\nThe mechanism of a tap is optical or electrical splitting, or a switch mirror that sends a copy to a monitor port. The production link stays up if the monitor cable is unplugged. A mirror port can drop copies when it is oversubscribed, so the sensor may see a sample rather than every frame. Inline placement puts the sensor or preventer between two devices, often as a bridge or a routed hop. Latency goes up by the time the device spends looking. If the device fails closed, production stops. If it fails open, production continues and inspection stops. Bypass switches exist so a dead inline appliance can be jumped. Those switches must be tested, because an untested bypass is a diagram. Inline is how a prevention engine drops a packet. A tap cannot drop the original.\n\nThe clinic puts a tap on the link between the server switch and the main router. The sensor reads the copy. During a maintenance window the sensor process exits at 10:14. Ticket MON-30 notes that application sessions stayed up and the router interface counters kept incrementing. The same ticket contrasts last year's inline sensor, which stalled the morning medication interface when it rebooted, because that box was bridging the only path. The engineer wants the ability to block malware, and she places that active inspection on a narrower application path where a failure is tolerable, not on the only link to every clinical system. The tap remains the wide view that must not be allowed to break care.\n\nDefenders draw every monitor and mark it inline or tap. They refuse an inline box on a path whose outage would stop the operation, unless a tested bypass and a clear owner exist. They accept that a tap will not save them in the moment, and they pair it with a separate control that can block, such as a firewall rule or an endpoint action. They check that the tap or mirror is actually receiving both directions. A one-direction copy looks like a quiet network and hides the return traffic. They alarm when the sensor stops so a dead tap feed is not mistaken for a healthy week.\n\nStudents say a tap is safer and therefore better in every case. Safer for uptime is not the same as able to stop an attack. Students also say inline is always better because it can block. Inline can block and inline can break the path. Match the placement to whether you need a stop now or a record without becoming part of the outage.",
        'r0': 'Inline monitoring is in the forwarding path, so it can block and can add delay or stop the path if it fails. A tap or a mirror copies frames to a sensor, and pulling the sensor should leave production counters climbing. Mirrors can discard copies when oversubscribed, so absence of an alert is not absence of traffic. Use a tested bypass if an inline appliance must protect a link you cannot afford to lose. Confirm both directions on the copy. A one-way feed hides half the conversation. Place blocking inline only where a failure is an accepted tradeoff. The tap remains the view that must not be able to drop clinic sessions.',
        'r1': 'Sitting in the path lets you block. Sitting in the path lets you break it. A copy watches without joining the path. A dead copy should not drop care. A dead inline box might. Test that difference on purpose. Ask for both directions in the copy. A one way feed hides the return. Use a bypass only if you have tested it. Do not expect a copy to stop an attack. Put a real block somewhere else if you need one. Write the placement next to the outage risk.',
        'r2': 'Picture a toll road. A booth standing on the asphalt can refuse a car and can also trap every car if the arm sticks down. A rubber tube on the shoulder counts axles and never holds a car, and the count stops if the tube breaks while the cars keep moving. The booth is useful where you have decided a stop matters more than the risk of a jam. The tube is useful where you need the count and the road must stay open. Inline monitoring is the booth: it can block and it can stall the path. A tap is the tube: it only copies, and a failed collector does not stop the traffic.',
        'r4': 'Mark each monitor as inline or as a copy. For a copy, confirm production stays up if the sensor stops. For an inline box, write fail open, fail closed, or bypass. Do not relocate a box during this pass. Check that the copy includes both directions. Alarm on a silent sensor. Pair any copy-only view with a separate control that can actually block.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to say a tap can block the original packets. A tap only copies traffic. Inline inspection can block, and it can add delay or become a single point of failure. Another trap ignores fail open, fail closed, and bypass. Those choices decide whether a dead inline box stops care or lets traffic pass unseen. A mirror that drops frames under load can miss events. Do not treat a missing alert as a quiet network. Placement is the whole question.',
    },
    'Jump servers': {
        'body': 'A jump server is a bastion administrators pass through. It is not a general user desktop, and it is not a place where ordinary staff read mail. Administrators sign in there, usually with a strong factor, and only from there do they open administration sessions to the systems they manage. Direct administration from the internet, or from a daily workstation full of browsers and chat, stays closed. The bastion is a narrow door with a log, not a second office computer.\n\nThe mechanism is network policy plus a hardened host. Security rules allow administration ports on the servers only from the bastion\'s address. The bastion itself accepts connections only from an approved remote-access method or from the management zone, and it records the person, the time, and the next host. Local accounts on the bastion are few. Tools installed there are the admin tools, not a general software catalog. Session recording or a command log gives a later reader something more useful than "someone was on the box." Multi-factor sign-in sits on the bastion so a stolen password is not enough. The daily laptop never becomes that host by accident: if everyone uses it for web mail, it is no longer a bastion.\n\nThe database no longer accepts SSH from the internet. A platform administrator finishes a phone prompt and lands on bastion-01. From that host the administrator opens a shell on the database. The database log reads "Accepted publickey from 10.8.0.14", and 10.8.0.14 is bastion-01. The administrator\'s home address never appears there. Ticket ACCESS-14 records that a request to install a chat client on bastion-01 was refused. The same ticket notes a contractor\'s daily laptop was denied a direct database rule. Administration happens through the bastion. Ordinary daily work does not belong on that host.\n\nDefenders list who may sign in to the bastion and remove names the week a person leaves. They patch the bastion on a stricter schedule than a typical workstation, because it can reach everything it was built to reach. They alert on a bastion login that does not match a change ticket, and on any allow-rule that opens a server administration port to something other than the bastion. They keep the bastion free of personal data and browsing. They test that a workstation in the user zone cannot open the database administration port. They treat a lost bastion credential as an incident that includes every system the bastion can touch.\n\nStudents call any remote computer a jump server, including a virtual desktop used for daily clinical work. That desktop is a workplace. The bastion is the admin stepping stone. Students also think a virtual private network replaces the bastion. A tunnel can bring an administrator to the front of the bastion. It does not by itself limit the next hop. One hardened stepping stone, not a street door on every server.',
        'r0': "A jump server is a bastion, not a general user desktop. Permit administration protocols such as SSH on TCP port 22 only from the bastion address, and require multi-factor sign-in on the bastion itself. The database authentication log should show the bastion address, for example 10.8.0.14, rather than the administrator's home address. Refuse browsers, chat, and other daily tools on that host. Alert when a new firewall rule opens an admin port to any source except the bastion. Record the person and the next host. A virtual private network may deliver the admin to the bastion and still must not open every internal admin port to the far laptop.",
        'r1': 'Admins enter through one hardened host. That host is not a daily desktop. Ordinary staff do not get accounts there. Servers accept admin logins only from it. Require a second factor at that door. Log the person and the next host. Keep mail and chat off that host. Patch it faster than a normal workstation. A tunnel can end at that door. A tunnel is not a pass to every server. Remove accounts when people leave. Test that user machines cannot skip it.',
        'r2': 'Picture a harbor where every ship is reached only from one dock office. Pilots sign the book in the office, take a single launch, and do not hand the launch keys to every sailor who wants a snack. The ships do not keep a public gangway to the street. If the dock office starts selling coffee and lending desks to visitors, it is no longer a dock office. A storm that soaks that office matters more than a storm that soaks a cafe, because the office can reach every ship. The dock office is the jump server: a bastion administrators pass through, not a general desktop, and the systems behind it should not offer their own street doors.',
        'r4': 'Write the bastion hostname and who may sign in. Confirm admin ports on servers allow that address only. Do not install daily software during this pass. Read one server log and check the source address. Refuse a request that turns the bastion into a normal desktop. Note whether a second factor is required. Remove any name that has left the team.',
        'exam': 'A jump server is a bastion you administer through, not a general user desktop and not a place for ordinary mail. The trap is to call a daily virtual desktop a bastion because it is remote. Another trap says a virtual private network removes the need for the stepping stone. The tunnel can arrive at the bastion. Direct administration from the internet to each server stays closed. Logs should show the bastion as the source, and sign-in there should demand a strong second factor.',
    },
    'Proxy servers': {
        'body': 'A proxy makes requests on behalf of clients. The organization can filter, log, and hide internal addresses because the far side sees the proxy, not every workstation. This is a middleman the organization runs on purpose. It is different from a bastion an administrator shells into, and different from a tunnel that carries whatever the user already addressed. The proxy terminates the client request and creates its own request outward, which is why it can read the destination and apply a category or an allow list.\n\nForward proxies sit near users. Browsers, or a transparent redirect, send web requests to the proxy. The proxy authenticates the user, applies a rule, and either connects outward or returns a block page. Logs then show the person and the URL, which a plain firewall log often cannot. Reverse proxies sit in front of applications. Outside clients talk to the reverse proxy, and it fetches from internal servers whose addresses are not published. That position can end a protected session, inspect the web request, and spread load. A proxy that cannot see the content, because the client tunnels opaque bytes without inspection, is only a relay. Inspection requires the proxy to be trusted by the client for that session, which is a deliberate certificate and policy choice, not an accident.\n\nUser browsers at the clinic send web traffic to proxy 10.1.8.20 on port 8080. The proxy log shows "user=adewale url=http://updates.example/payload category=malware action=block". The workstation address does not appear at the far host. The block page is what the nurse sees. Ticket WEB-42 attaches that log line and the category rule. A separate reverse proxy in front of the patient portal presents the public name and forwards only healthy requests to the application tier. The portal\'s internal address is not placed on the internet router. Both boxes are proxies. The two proxies face opposite directions.\n\nDefenders require authentication on the forward proxy so the log names a person, not only an address that many people share. They block known-bad categories and they review exceptions, because a permanent bypass for one executive becomes the path everyone copies. They protect the proxy itself, since every user session depends on it and a compromised proxy sees a great deal. They keep the trust certificate for inspection under change control. They do not describe the proxy as a virtual private network. Users do not get a routed interface to the whole internal network. They get a service that fetches approved destinations.\n\nStudents confuse a proxy with a jump server. The jump server is an interactive bastion for administrators. The proxy fetches on the client\'s behalf and writes down who asked. Students also think hiding internal addresses is the same as making the far end trustworthy. The proxy can conceal a workstation and still be fetching something hostile if the rule is wrong. Filter, log, and hide are the three jobs. Each one has to be configured.',
        'r0': "A forward proxy accepts client requests, often on TCP port 8080, authenticates the user, and connects outward under the proxy's address. The log should name the user and the URL, as in a malware-category block for a specific account, rather than only a workstation address. A reverse proxy publishes the application name and hides the internal servers behind it. Inspection of protected sessions depends on a trust certificate the client was given on purpose. A proxy that only relays opaque bytes cannot apply a content category. Do not confuse that relay with a bastion login or with a routed tunnel that exposes whole networks.",
        'r1': 'A proxy fetches on behalf of someone else. You can filter, log, and hide addresses. The far side sees the proxy. User proxies face the people. Application proxies face the outside. Name the person in the log, not only the desk. A block page should match a written rule. Review exceptions before they become permanent. Protect the proxy because everyone depends on it. Hiding an address is not the same as trust. This is not an admin desktop you shell into. This is not a tunnel to the whole network.',
        'r2': "Picture a hotel concierge who collects parcels for guests. The courier meets the concierge, not the guest's room number. The concierge writes who asked, refuses a parcel on the do-not-accept list, and can open a parcel only under a house rule the guest already accepted. If the concierge is away, the parcels stop, so the desk itself needs a backup plan. Letting a guest walk into the alley to meet every courier removes the list and the log. The concierge is the proxy: a middleman you run so requests can be filtered and logged, and so internal rooms are not published to every caller.",
        'r4': 'Identify each proxy as user-facing or application-facing. Read a log line and confirm it names a person and a destination. Do not add a bypass during this pass. Note any exception that has no expiry. Check that internal application addresses are not published directly. Confirm the inspection certificate is tracked as a change. Write down who patches the proxy. Record whether internal addresses stay hidden.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to call a proxy a jump server. A jump server is a bastion an administrator signs into. A proxy makes the request for the client, then filters and logs it. Another trap treats address hiding as trust in the destination. The far site can still be hostile if the rule allows it. A reverse proxy hides application servers. A forward proxy identifies users. A relay that cannot see content cannot enforce a category.',
    },
    'Intrusion detection and prevention': {
        'body': 'Detection watches traffic or host activity and raises an alert when a pattern or a behavior looks wrong. Prevention can also block that activity. Both depend on where the sensor sits and on how carefully it is tuned. A detector that only receives a copy can warn. It cannot drop the original packet. A preventer placed inline, or an agent on the host that can kill a process, can stop the action and can also stop a legitimate one when the rule is wrong. Detection warns. Prevention can also stop the activity.\n\nThe mechanism is a signature, a protocol decode, or a behavior rule, plus a placement decision. A signature names a known pattern, sometimes with an identifier the analyst can look up. A decode catches violations of how a protocol is supposed to look. A behavior rule catches rates and odd sequences, and it needs a baseline so Tuesday does not page the team for ordinary payroll. Alerts should include the source, the destination, the time, and the rule. Prevention actions include drop, reset, or quarantine, and each action needs an owner who can reverse a mistake. Tuning is the work of disabling noisy rules, narrowing them, or adding a short-lived exception. Turning the whole system off because one rule misfired is how the next real event passes in silence.\n\nOn the clinic network a detection sensor raises "ET SCAN suspicious probe" from a workstation to many server addresses and pages the on-call analyst. A prevention engine in front of the billing application sees a matching payload bound for that application and writes "prevented sid=200001". The same pattern aimed at a segment where the sensor is only tapped produces an alert and no drop. Ticket SOC-88 records both outcomes so the team does not claim the tap prevented anything. The analyst also notes three false blocks on a file-upload URL last month, which were handled by narrowing the rule rather than by removing the prevention engine.\n\nDefenders place prevention where a block is worth the operational risk, and they place detection on copies where the goal is breadth without becoming a failure point. They keep signatures current and they review the top noisy rules every week. They write exceptions with an expiry and a ticket. They test a known pattern in a change window so they know whether the expected result is an alert or a drop. They correlate the alert with the host and with identity, because an address alone is a weak story. They do not describe detection as if it healed the system. An alert is a signal. Containment and repair are later work.\n\nStudents use the two words as synonyms. Detection does not block just because the product brochure says security. Prevention is the control that can stop the packet or the process, and only when it is actually in a position to do so. Students also think more signatures always mean a healthier network. An untuned preventer becomes a source of outages, and staff will bypass it. Placement and tuning decide whether the tool warns, stops, or merely makes noise.',
        'r0': 'Detection alerts on a signature, a protocol break, or a behavior. Prevention adds a drop, a reset, or a quarantine, and it must sit inline or on the host where it can affect the action. A copy-only sensor can raise "ET SCAN" and still cannot claim a prevent. A useful prevention log names the signature, such as sid=200001, plus source and destination. Review false positives on a weekly cadence and attach an expiry to every exception. Disabling the entire engine because one upload failed is an availability choice that also blinds the next event. Test a known pattern so the expected action is proven.',
        'r1': 'Detection raises a signal after something looks wrong. Prevention can stop that something. A copy of traffic cannot drop the original. A blocker must sit where it can act. A bad rule can stop real work. Tune noisy rules instead of switching all off. Give every exception an end date. Test a known pattern on purpose. Write down whether you expected a warning or a stop. An alert does not repair the host. Pair the signal with a person who responds. Keep signatures current and the placement honest.',
        'r2': 'Picture a museum gallery. A rope alarm tells the desk that someone leaned too far. A guard standing in the doorway can stop the next person and can also stop a school group when the order is wrong. The alarm still matters in rooms where a guard would block the only exit. Silencing every alarm because one child tripped a rope leaves the gallery deaf. Moving the guard into a closet to "keep the peace" means the guard can hear nothing and stop nothing. Detection is the rope alarm. Prevention is the guard who can actually intervene, and only while posted where the visitors must pass.',
        'r4': 'For each sensor, write alert-only or able to block. Read one recent alert and note the rule identifier. Do not disable a rule during this pass. If a block is possible, find the last false block and its ticket. Check that exceptions have expiry dates. Confirm a copy-only feed is not described as prevention in the runbook. Name the person who answers the page.',
        'exam': 'Detection alerts. Prevention can block, and only if it sits in the path or on the host. The trap is to say a tapped sensor prevented an attack because an alert fired. Another trap turns the whole engine off after one false block. Tune the rule and expire the exception. A third trap describes detection as if the alert itself restored the system. The alert is a signal. Containment and repair are separate actions. Placement and tuning are the facts that make the product real.',
    },
    'Load balancers': {
        'body': 'A load balancer spreads work across servers and can stop sending new work to a server that has failed its health check. Users aim at one name or one virtual address. The balancer chooses a real server. That choice can be as simple as taking turns, or it can prefer the server with the fewest current sessions. The balancer is not a copy of the application data. It does not, by itself, keep two databases in agreement. It shares the arrival of requests and it can skip a sick server.\n\nThe mechanism has a listener, a pool, a health check, and a persistence rule. The listener is the address users reach. The pool is the set of real servers. The health check is a repeated question, often a page or a TCP connection, with a threshold for failure and a threshold for return. When a server fails, existing sessions may drain or break, depending on the design, and new sessions go elsewhere. Persistence pins a client to one server when the application keeps session state in memory. That pin can overload one server if you are careless. A layer-4 balancer forwards on address and port. A layer-7 balancer can read the web request and choose a pool based on the name or the path. The balancer becomes a critical hop: if it dies, the name dies, so serious designs use a pair.\n\nTwo web nodes, app-a and app-b, sit behind the clinic portal address. The balancer requests /health every five seconds. App-b begins returning errors. The log reads "health fail app-b, removed from pool". New sessions go to app-a. Ticket PLAT-19 shows the node was left out until the disk was cleared, then a passing check put it back. Nobody edited patient rows on the balancer. The database behind both nodes stayed a separate system with its own copy strategy. The balancer\'s pair failed over in a test the month before, and that test record is why the team trusted the virtual address during the node failure.\n\nDefenders treat health checks as production controls. A check that always passes is not a check. They monitor the balancer itself and they practice the failover of the balancer pair. They notice when persistence glues everyone to one node. They limit administrative access to the balancer, because a bad pool edit is an outage. They do not store the only copy of a secret solely in a balancer configuration without a backup of that configuration. They explain to application owners that adding a node does nothing if the node is not in the pool and passing the check.\n\nStudents confuse a load balancer with a cluster that shares or takes over data. Spreading web requests is not the same as a second database node that can finish the same write. Students also think the virtual address is magic availability. If every node depends on one database and one balancer with no pair, the design still has single points of failure. The balancer shares work and skips a failed member. It does not replace backup or a data-tier design.',
        'r0': 'A load balancer publishes a virtual address and chooses among pool members using a schedule such as fewest connections or round robin. Health checks, for example a request to /health every five seconds, remove a member after a failure threshold and return it only after passing checks. Layer 4 balances on address and port. Layer 7 can select a pool from the requested name or path. Session persistence pins a client to one member when memory on that member holds the session. The balancer is a critical hop and usually needs a pair. It does not replicate the database behind the pool.',
        'r1': 'Work is spread across more than one server. A sick server can be skipped. Users aim at one name. A health check decides who stays in the pool. A check that always passes is useless. Some sessions stick to one server on purpose. That stickiness can pile work on one box. The balancer is not a copy of the database. If the balancer dies, the name may die. Practice the failover of a pair. Adding a server does nothing until it joins the pool. Watch the pool, not only the application.',
        'r2': "Picture a post office with two sorting tables and one public window. A clerk at the window hands parcels to whichever table is keeping up, and stops handing parcels to a table whose lamp is out. The parcels do not become safer merely because the window exists. The valuables log still lives in the safe, not in the clerk's hand. If the clerk goes home and nobody else knows the window, the public sees a closed door even though both tables are fine. A second trained clerk is what keeps the window open. The window is the load balancer: it shares arrivals and skips a failed table, and it is not the safe that holds the records.",
        'r4': 'Write the virtual name and the pool members. Read the health check and the failure threshold. Do not add or remove a member during this pass. Find the last time a member was ejected and why. Note whether sessions stick to one member. Confirm the balancer itself has a tested pair or an accepted outage. Identify who may edit the pool.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to say a load balancer copies application data the way a database cluster does. It spreads requests and can skip a server that fails a health check. It does not, by itself, keep two databases in agreement. Another trap ignores the balancer as a single point of failure. The virtual address dies if the only balancer dies. Persistence that pins clients to one server can defeat the spread. A health check that always succeeds does not detect a sick member.',
    },
    'Network sensors': {
        'body': 'A network sensor watches copies of traffic and turns them into alerts or records. It is a lookout, not a gate. The copy may come from a tap or a mirror. The sensor decodes what it can, writes logs, and raises a signal when a rule matches. It does not sit in the forwarding path, so it does not block, and a crash of the sensor does not drop the clinic\'s sessions. People sometimes say sensor when they mean an inline preventer. In this topic the sensor is the watcher on a copy.\n\nThe mechanism is capture, decode, and record. The sensor sees frames the mirror actually delivers, which may be less than the link carried if the mirror is oversubscribed. It can reassemble sessions, notice beacons, extract names from common protocols, and store a short record for later search. Rules turn those records into alerts with a severity. Time sync matters, because an alert that is four minutes off cannot be matched to a host log. Placement matters just as much: a sensor outside the firewall sees internet noise and misses the conversation between a workstation and a file server. A sensor on the server tap sees that conversation and misses wireless guests if they never cross the tap. Nobody gets every view from one box.\n\nThe sensor on the clinic server tap writes "beacon 10.4.1.14 -> 203.0.113.50 period=60" for workstation WS-14. Once a minute the host calls an address it has never used. The sensor does not reset the connection. The analyst uses the record to find WS-14, then the access switch isolates that port. Ticket SOC-102 quotes the beacon line and the switch action as two different steps. The next morning the sensor process is down for an hour and the link counters on the tap still rise. The outage of the lookout is its own ticket. It is not an outage of the file server.\n\nDefenders place sensors where the traffic they care about actually flows, and they draw that choice. They alarm when a sensor goes silent, because silence looks like a calm network. They keep storage for the records long enough to investigate, and they restrict who can read captured payloads, since payloads can contain secrets. They align clocks to the same source as the servers. They test with a known harmless pattern and expect an alert. They do not tell leadership that the sensor blocked an intrusion. If a block happened, some other control did it.\n\nStudents grade a sensor by how many threats it "stops." A sensor that only sees copies stops nothing. Students also place the sensor at the internet edge and assume internal misuse will appear there. East-west traffic between servers never hits that edge. Watch copies where the question lives, and pair the sensor with a control that can actually contain a host.',
        'r0': 'A network sensor consumes a tap or mirror copy and writes records or alerts. It is not inline, so a sensor crash must not drop production traffic. A useful record names both ends and a rhythm, such as a workstation beaconing to 203.0.113.50 every 60 seconds. Placement decides the question you can answer: a server-link sensor sees east-west server traffic, while an edge sensor sees internet crossings and misses purely internal flows. Oversubscribed mirrors drop frames. Silent sensors should alarm. Synchronize clocks with the servers before you try to join an alert to a host log. Payloads in the capture are sensitive and need access limits.',
        'r1': 'A sensor watches a copy of traffic. It writes alerts and records. It does not block the original packets. If the sensor dies, the link should stay up. Put it where the traffic of interest flows. An edge view misses talks that stay inside. A quiet sensor may be a dead sensor. Alarm when the records stop. Keep clocks aligned with the servers. Limit who can read captured payloads. Test with a harmless pattern you expect to see. Use a different control when you need a block. Do not call a lookout a gate.',
        'r2': 'Picture a harbor watch standing on a balcony with a notebook. The watch writes which boats passed and how often one small boat returns. The watch cannot close the channel, and boats still pass if the watch steps inside. A notebook left blank for an hour is a problem with the watch, not proof the harbor was empty. The balcony only sees the channel in front of it. Boats in a back inlet never appear on that page. The balcony watch is the network sensor: it turns copies of traffic into records, and some other control must act if a boat needs to be stopped.',
        'r4': 'Name each sensor and the link it copies. Confirm the production path stays up if that sensor stops. Do not move the sensor during this pass. Read one alert and note whether both directions were visible. Check the clock source against a server. Alarm on a sensor that sends nothing. Write who may open a captured payload. Note the mirror or tap that feeds the sensor.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to say a network sensor blocked the traffic it described. A sensor watches copies and writes alerts or records. A block requires an inline control or a later action on the host. Another trap places one sensor at the internet edge and expects it to see every internal conversation. Placement chooses the traffic. A silent sensor is not a healthy network. Oversubscribed copies can miss frames. Do not treat the lookout as the gate.',
    },
    'Port security and 802.1X': {
        'body': 'Port security and 802.1X stop an unknown device from simply plugging in and joining the network. Plugging a cable in is not the same as being admitted. Port security is the simpler control on a switch port: it limits how many hardware addresses may appear, and it can remember an allowed address or shut the port when a stranger appears. 802.1X authenticates a device onto a switch port. The port stays unauthorized, passing almost nothing, until the device proves an identity. A lobby jack that lights up and hands out a full network address to anything with a plug is neither control.\n\n802.1X uses three roles. The device is the supplicant. The switch or access point is the authenticator. The authentication server, commonly RADIUS on UDP port 1812, decides. Between the device and the switch, the port-access frames carry the authentication conversation. That conversation uses the Extensible Authentication Protocol as its framework, including methods that protect a password or present a certificate. The switch does not need to understand every method. It carries the exchange and opens the port only after an accept. Failed devices can land in a guest segment or nowhere. Port security based only on a hardware address is weaker, because addresses can be copied, and it does not prove a credential. It still helps against a casual extra laptop on a port that should have one machine.\n\nA lobby wall port, Gi1/0/12, stays dark to the user network until a clinic laptop completes 802.1X. The switch log reads "Gi1/0/12 authorized user=host/clinic-laptop14". A visitor laptop on the same jack gets no route to file servers. A conference-room port uses a hardware-address limit instead, and ticket NET-70 records the difference: that room checks an address, not a credential. When an attacker clones an allowed address, the conference-room port will not save the clinic. The lobby port still asks the laptop to authenticate. The engineer does not call both ports equal in the audit notes.\n\nDefenders turn 802.1X on for ports where unknown people can touch a jack, and they keep a process for devices that cannot speak it, such as a printer, using a separate zone rather than a blanket exception. They watch for authentication failures and for ports set back to a wide-open mode during troubleshooting. They do not leave those exceptions in place after the ticket closes. They pair the accept with an authorization result that chooses a zone, so a phone and a workstation do not receive the same access merely because both proved they belong to the organization. They inventory switches that still rely only on a remembered hardware address.\n\nStudents say 802.1X is the password method itself. The method is the Extensible Authentication Protocol carried inside 802.1X. 802.1X is the port admission. Students also say a hardware-address lock is the same strength as a credential check. Copying an address defeats the thinner control. Authenticating a device onto the switch port is the stronger idea, and the port should stay closed until that succeeds.',
        'r0': '802.1X authenticates a device onto a switch port. The supplicant is the device, the authenticator is the switch, and RADIUS on UDP 1812 is the usual authentication server. The port stays unauthorized until an accept, then a zone can be chosen from the result. Frames on the link carry Extensible Authentication Protocol methods such as a certificate method or a tunneled password method. Classic port security instead limits or remembers hardware addresses and can shut a port on violation. Addresses can be cloned, so that control is thinner than a credential. Do not leave a troubleshooting port in open mode after the ticket. Printers that cannot speak 802.1X belong in a separate zone, not in a global bypass.',
        'r1': 'Plugging a cable in is not joining. The switch port stays closed until the device is known. One control checks a hardware address only. Addresses can be copied, so that check is thin. The stronger control asks the device to prove itself. A server makes the allow or deny decision. The switch opens the port after an allow. Failed devices get a guest corner or nothing. Do not leave a port open after troubleshooting. Printers that cannot speak the method need a separate corner. Prove the block with a stranger laptop.',
        'r2': 'Picture a loading dock where a cable reel sits by a side door. A simple list of truck colors lets a painter fake an allowed truck. A better door asks the driver for a badge the office can check before the latch moves, and a rejected driver waits in the yard. The person at the door does not invent the rule. The office does, and the door only moves after the office says yes. Leaving the latch taped open after a delivery is how the careful door becomes a hole. That door is port admission: the jack does not join a device to the network until the device authenticates, and a remembered paint color is a weaker substitute.',
        'r4': 'Pick a wall port in a public area. Write whether it checks a credential or only a hardware address. Do not disable the check during this pass. Plug nothing new in as a test unless change control allows it. Read one authorization log and note the zone assigned. Find any port left open after troubleshooting. Place devices that cannot authenticate into a separate zone on paper.',
        'exam': '802.1X authenticates a device onto a switch port. The trap is to call it the same thing as the Extensible Authentication Protocol. That protocol is the framework the port control carries. Another trap treats a remembered hardware address as equal to a credential. Addresses can be copied. The port should remain unauthorized until an accept. A guest corner for failures is not the same as a wide-open jack. Do not leave a troubleshooting exception in place.',
    },
    'Extensible authentication protocol': {
        'body': 'Extensible Authentication Protocol is the authentication framework that 802.1X carries between a device and the authentication server. It is not the switch port, and it is not the whole network-access product. The framework defines how an identity is asked for and how a result comes back. Specific methods plug into that framework. Some methods send a weak challenge. Useful methods protect the password inside a tunnel or replace the password with a certificate. The access point or the switch often only relays the exchange. The method is interpreted at the device and at the authentication server.\n\nThe mechanism starts when the port or the wireless association demands an identity. The device and the server negotiate a method. A certificate method proves the device with a client certificate and can check the server as well, so a rogue access point is easier to refuse. A tunneled method first builds a protected channel, often using a server certificate, and only then carries the password inside that channel. The password is not exposed in the clear to the access point. An outer name can be a generic label so the real username is also inside the tunnel. Older challenge methods that do not build that tunnel are poor choices, and policy should reject them. The server\'s accept or reject is what 802.1X uses to open or keep the port closed. Changing the method does not change the fact that the port control is still 802.1X.\n\nClinic wireless uses a tunneled method so the laptop password is not visible to the access point. The controller log shows "method=tunneled-password result=success" for a nursing laptop, and a test laptop offering only a weak challenge method is rejected. Ticket WIFI-12 records the reject and the allowed method list on the authentication server. A second test uses a certificate-backed method for a medical device that has no person typing a password. Both tests are the same framework with different methods. The engineer does not write "we turned on 802.1X" as if that sentence named the method. The port control and the method are configured in different places.\n\nDefenders set the allowed methods explicitly and disable weak ones. They install server certificates that devices can verify, because a tunnel built to an impostor server defeats the point of the tunnel. They protect the client certificates and revoke them when a device is retired. They log the method and the result, not only a vague success. They keep the authentication server reachable from the switches and access points on a path that itself is not the user network. They teach support staff not to "fix" a failure by allowing any method.\n\nStudents merge this framework with 802.1X. The port-admission control is what lets a device onto the switch port after proof succeeds. This protocol is the framework that port control carries, including methods that protect the password. Students also think any method under the framework is fine. A method that exposes the credential to the access point is a failed choice even if the port eventually opens. The way the door asks for proof is not the door itself.',
        'r0': 'Extensible Authentication Protocol is the framework inside 802.1X, not the port-admission feature itself. Methods include certificate proof, often called EAP-TLS, and tunneled password proof such as PEAP, which keeps the password off the access point. Weak methods such as EAP-MD5 should be disabled on the server. The switch or access point relays the exchange to RADIUS on UDP 1812 and opens the port only after an accept. Devices should validate the server certificate before they send a credential into a tunnel. Log the method name and the result. A generic outer identity can hide the real username until the tunnel exists.',
        'r1': 'This is the way the network asks for proof. The switch port is a different control. The port control carries this framework. Some methods use a certificate. Some methods hide a password in a tunnel. Weak challenge methods should be refused. The access point often only passes messages along. The server decides success or failure. Devices should check the server before trusting it. Log which method was used. Do not allow every method to fix a ticket. Retire certificates when devices leave. Name the method in the design, not only the port feature.',
        'r2': 'Picture a clinic door that always asks for proof, and a choice of how the proof is given. One morning the desk accepts a photo badge. Another morning it accepts a passphrase spoken into a hood so the hallway cannot hear it. A third method, shouting the passphrase across the lobby, is written on a reject list. The door latch is unchanged. Only the questioning changes. If the desk accepts any answer at all after a complaint, the hood and the badge stop mattering. The questioning method is this authentication framework. The latch that opens the network port is the neighboring control, and it only moves after the chosen proof succeeds.',
        'r4': 'List the proof methods the server allows. Mark any weak challenge method for removal. Do not change the list during this pass. Confirm devices check the server certificate. Read one success log and one reject log. Note whether the method name appears. Separate that note from the switch-port feature. Plan revocation for a device that will be retired. Write the certificate name devices should trust.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to say this framework is 802.1X. 802.1X authenticates a device onto a switch port. Extensible Authentication Protocol is the framework that exchange carries. Another trap treats every method as equal. A method that exposes the password to the access point is not acceptable. Certificate methods and tunneled methods are the usual safe choices. The switch often only relays the messages. The server decides. Log the method, and do not "fix" failures by allowing any proof at all.',
    },
    'Web application firewalls': {
        'body': 'A web application firewall inspects web requests and responses for attacks against the application, such as injection, forged requests, and obvious scanner noise. It looks at web requests. It is not a general network firewall that only decides on addresses and ports, and it is not a next-generation firewall whose main claim is understanding many applications. A next-generation firewall understands applications. This control understands HTTP conversations in front of a site. Allowing the site\'s port is a different decision from allowing a particular request inside that port.\n\nThe mechanism parses the request: the method, the path, the headers, the query, and the body. Rules look for injection patterns, unexpected file types, oversized bodies, and sessions that do not match the application. Some deployments start in a learning mode that alerts only, then move selected rules to block. A score can add up across several weak signals until a threshold blocks the request. The application server never sees a request that was blocked. Placement is usually a reverse proxy or a module on the web server, which means protected sessions must be visible to it. If the inspection ends somewhere else, this firewall sees only opaque bytes and cannot do its job. Locks and exceptions belong to a specific rule and a specific path, with an expiry, because a sitewide bypass returns you to having no inspection.\n\nThe billing application receives a request whose query contains a classic injection fragment, the sort that tries to make a condition always true. The web application firewall drops it and logs "rule 942100 score exceeded uri=/pay action=block". The application log for that second shows no request. Ticket APP-55 attaches the firewall line. A network firewall in front of the same site allows TCP 443 and would have passed the request, because the port and the address were legitimate. The engineer does not add the network allow and consider the application defended. The web inspection is the control that saw the payload.\n\nDefenders put this firewall in front of internet-facing applications and tune it with the application owner. They start noisy rules in alert mode when a block would stop clinics from working, and they promote a rule to block once the false positives are understood. They review the bypass list monthly. They make sure the certificate ends where inspection happens, or the box is only passing mystery bytes. They still fix the application. A signature that drops a famous payload does not repair a query that concatenates user input. The firewall is a shield while the code is corrected, not a reason to leave the code broken.\n\nStudents call every firewall a web application firewall. A port filter does not read the request. Students also call this box a next-generation firewall. Application awareness across many protocols is the neighboring idea. Looking at web requests is this one. A bundle of many functions in one appliance is a third idea. Do not merge them because one product brochure uses all three phrases.',
        'r0': 'A web application firewall parses HTTP: method, path, headers, and body. It blocks patterns such as injection before they reach the application, and the log should show a rule identifier, a score, and a block action on a specific URI. Allowing TCP 443 on a network firewall does not perform that inspection. The device must see the request in the clear, which means the protected session ends at the inspection point. Use alert mode while tuning, then block. Attach an expiry to every bypass. This does not replace a code fix for concatenated input. It is also not the same control as a firewall that classifies many non-web applications.',
        'r1': 'This firewall reads web requests. A port allow does not read the request. It can stop an injection before the app sees it. It must be able to see the content. Mystery bytes cannot be judged. Start noisy rules as alerts if a block would hurt care. Move a rule to block after tuning. Put an end date on every bypass. Keep fixing the application code too. A dropped payload is not a repaired query. Do not mix it up with a box that knows many apps. Do not confuse it with a bundle of unrelated features.',
        'r2': 'Picture a mail room that opens letters addressed to the billing desk. The clerk rejects a letter whose body is a trick instruction and stamps the reason on a slip the desk never has to read. Letters that are merely "mail" still get opened, because the fact that they arrived does not make the words safe. If the clerk is handed a locked pouch and no key, the clerk can only pass the pouch along. A rule that starts as a warning, then becomes a rejection after the staff agree, keeps the desk working while the filter learns. That clerk is the web application firewall: it looks at web requests, and a door that only counts envelopes is a different control.',
        'r4': 'Name the applications that sit behind web inspection. Read one block log and note the rule and the path. Do not add a sitewide bypass during this pass. Check that the inspector can see the request and not only encrypted mystery. List exceptions and their end dates. Confirm the application owner still has a code fix open for any recurring payload. Separate this note from a plain port allow.',
        'exam': 'A web application firewall looks at web requests. The trap is to treat a port allow as the same control. Legitimate ports still carry injection. Another trap calls this box a next-generation firewall. A next-generation firewall understands applications across more than web requests. A third trap treats a bundle of many features in one appliance as proof of web inspection. The inspector must see the request content. A bypass without an end date undoes the control. The application code still needs repair.',
    },
    'Unified threat management': {
        'body': 'Unified threat management bundles several network functions in one appliance. A typical small-office box combines a firewall, web filtering, and a basic detection signature set, and it may add virtual private networking as well. The point of the design is one chassis, one management screen, and one purchase for a site that cannot staff five separate products. That convenience makes the box important. If it is down or unpatched, every function inside it is down or exposed together. A UTM bundles several functions in one box. Bundling is the idea to remember.\n\nThe mechanism is a single policy engine with several engines behind one interface. Traffic enters one appliance and is matched against address rules, category filters, and signatures in an order the administrator must understand. A mis-ordered policy can allow a flow before the filter ever sees it. Licenses often turn features on and off, so a box that looks complete may have an expired filter and a silent gap. One update cycle patches the whole bundle, which is efficient and also means a bad update affects every function. Logging from each function should still be distinguishable, or an incident becomes one vague "security event." Capacity is shared: turning on every signature and every decrypt option can exhaust a small chassis and slow the site.\n\nA branch clinic uses one appliance for the firewall, the web category filter, and a basic signature set. The console shows three feature licenses on one hostname, utm-branch3. Firmware is two versions behind. Ticket UTM-9 cites a published hole in the web-filter component and notes that the same chassis is also the only router for the branch. The office cannot "turn off just the risky part" without losing the path to the main clinic. The administrator schedules a maintenance window, confirms the configuration backup, and applies the firmware. The backup matters because the only copy of every policy lives on that one box.\n\nDefenders patch a unified box on a calendar, not when someone remembers. They back up the configuration before an upgrade and they know how to restore it. They check license dates so a lapsed filter is not mistaken for protection. They monitor CPU and session counts, because an overloaded bundle fails open in practice when staff demand a bypass. They export logs to a separate server so a dead appliance does not take the history with it. They ask whether the site has grown past what one chassis can honestly do. A bundle is a reasonable small-site design and a risky core for a hospital unless a second unit exists.\n\nStudents treat the bundle as a next-generation firewall. A next-generation firewall understands applications and users, not only ports and a pile of bundled features. A product can be both, but the exam distinction is the claim: several functions in one box, versus application awareness. Students also think buying the bundle finishes the job. An unpatched single box is one outage and one hole for every function it contains.',
        'r0': 'Unified threat management places several controls, commonly firewall, web category filtering, and signature detection, on one appliance with one policy screen. Feature licenses can expire independently, so confirm each function is actually entitled. A shared chassis means a firmware hole or a full CPU affects every bundled function at once, and the branch may have no second path. Back up the configuration before an upgrade. Export logs off the box. Policy order matters: a flow allowed before it reaches the filter is not filtered. Application and user awareness is the neighboring claim of a next-generation firewall, not the definition of the bundle.',
        'r1': 'Several jobs share one box. That box becomes very important. If it fails, every job fails together. If it is unpatched, every job shares the hole. Check that each feature is still licensed. Watch the load so the box does not melt. Back up the settings before an upgrade. Send logs somewhere else. Read the order of the rules. An early allow can skip a later filter. A small site may reasonably use one box. A growing site needs a second look. Bundling is the point to remember.',
        'r2': "Picture one service truck whose driver is mechanic, dispatcher, and guard for a small yard. The yard gets all three jobs without hiring three people, and a sick driver stops repairs, routing, and the gate together. The truck's checklist can show three subscriptions, and a lapsed subscription quietly removes one job while the truck still rolls. A toolbox that lives only on that truck is lost if the truck is lost, so a copy of the checklist stays in the office. One person doing several jobs is the bundle. In the network, several functions share one appliance, and that appliance has to be patched, backed up, and watched because everything rides on it.",
        'r4': 'List the functions enabled on the single appliance. Check each license end date. Do not upgrade during this pass. Confirm a configuration backup exists and where it is stored. Note the firmware version against the current fix. See whether logs leave the box. Write the outage impact if this chassis is the only path. Name a second route if this chassis is the only one.',
        'exam': 'A unified threat management box bundles several functions in one appliance. The trap is to define it as application awareness. A next-generation firewall understands applications and users, not only ports. A product might do both, but the ideas are not the same. Another trap forgets that one chassis is one failure and one patch domain for every bundled function. Expired licenses and rule order can disable a function you still think you bought. Back up the configuration and keep logs somewhere else.',
    },
    'Next-generation firewalls': {
        'body': 'A next-generation firewall can tell applications and users apart, which a rule based only on ports and addresses cannot do. An older firewall can allow TCP port 443 and stop there. Payroll, webmail, and file copying can all hide inside that single port. If a rule cannot name the application and the group of people, the rule is not doing this job.\n\nThe firewall identifies an application from the way the traffic behaves, not from the port number alone. It can tie a session to a user, often after a directory sign-in, and then apply a rule such as finance may use the payroll application. Encryption hides the details, so some deployments inspect the session in the middle. That inspection needs a certificate the user machines trust, and it needs a written limit on which staff traffic may be opened. Without inspection, the firewall may see only a port and a name from the handshake, and the rule should not pretend to know more than it saw. A useful log shows the application, the user, and the action. Empty fields mean the rule is still a port rule wearing a new label.\n\nThe old clinic rule allowed any TCP 443 from the finance desks to the internet. Priya could reach the payroll application, and the same rule allowed a file-sharing application. A spreadsheet of pay data left on that path. The replacement rule allows the payroll application only for the finance group and denies the file-sharing application. The good log line shows user priya, application payroll, and action allow. The bad log line shows user priya, application file-share, and action deny. Both lines still show port 443. The application name is what changed the decision.\n\nDefenders replace broad port allows with application and user rules when the risk is which application and which person. They read the logs and confirm the application field is populated. Where inspection is required, they record the certificate, the scope, and the traffic that stays private, such as health notes the policy says not to open. The box still needs patches, because a firewall that understands applications is still a computer sitting on the path. Leaving any TCP 443 in place as a stand-in does not finish the work.\n\nStudents confuse this device with a web application firewall. A web application firewall inspects HTTP requests for attacks such as injection and forged requests aimed at one web application. The separating fact here is application identity and user identity on network traffic, not a special focus on web injection. A plain port filter is the other neighbor, sometimes called layer 4. Layer 4 sees addresses and ports. If the answer cannot name an application, the answer is that older filter. Unified threat management is a third neighbor: several jobs packed into one appliance. Packing jobs together is not the same fact as understanding the application.',
        'r0': 'Match rules to applications and users, not only to ports. TCP port 443 can carry payroll, webmail, and file copying together, so an allow of any TCP 443 does not say which application ran. A directory sign-in lets the rule name a person, as when finance may use payroll and may not use file sharing. Logs should show user, application, and action, for example user priya, application payroll, action allow, on port 443. If encryption hides the application, inspection needs a trusted certificate and a written limit, and health notes may stay unopened. Without that inspection, do not pretend the rule saw more than the handshake. A filter aimed at web injection is a different device. A bundle of many features in one appliance is a different fact too.',
        'r1': 'This firewall can name the application. It can also name the person. A door number alone is not enough. Many applications can share one door. Allow the payroll tool for the finance group. Deny a file copying tool on that same door. Read the log for person, application, and action. Empty application fields mean you still have a simple rule. Opening sealed traffic needs a trusted certificate. Write down which mail you will not open. Health notes may stay sealed. Patch the box, because it sits on the path. A filter that only hunts bad web forms is a different tool.',
        'r2': 'Clinic reception can do more than notice that someone used the front door. A careful desk asks the department and the errand, and it turns away a file courier who is not the payroll clerk even though both used the same door. The door alone would have waved both through. A sealed envelope shows only the outside name unless the clinic agreed to open it, and a doctor note can stay sealed. The log should name the person, the errand, and the decision. The department and the errand are the application and the user a next-generation firewall can see beyond a bare door. A desk that only checks the door is the older filter, and a desk that only hunts bad web orders is a different job.',
        'r4': 'Look at wide destination-port rules and write down which applications use them. Read logs for user and application fields. Do not delete the old port rule yet. Add the application and user rule, then confirm allow and deny for one wanted application and one unwanted application. Do not inspect health traffic yet. Write the certificate plan and the traffic that stays private first. Patch the firewall on a planned window. Do not treat a bundle of features as an application rule.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to treat any new firewall box as this control, or to confuse it with a web application firewall. A web application firewall looks for attacks in web requests, such as injection. The separating fact is a rule that names the application and the user, not only the port. An allow of any TCP 443 is still a port rule. Layer 4 sees addresses and ports and cannot name the application. Packing several jobs into one appliance is a different fact. No application field in the log means the rule missed this job.',
    },
    'Layer 4 and layer 7 filtering': {
        'body': 'Layer 4 filtering sees ports and addresses. Layer 7 filtering sees the application content. A rule that allows a port is a transport decision: this address may open that service port. A rule that understands the application looks at what the session actually is, even when the port is the one everyone uses for protected web traffic. Allowing the common web port does not mean you allowed only browsing, because many programs reuse that port to look ordinary.\n\nThe mechanism at layer 4 matches the protocol, the source, the destination, and the port, and then allows or denies the segment. It is fast and it is blind to the payload. The mechanism at layer 7 terminates or decodes the application enough to name it: a particular web application, a file-sharing client, a tunnel hiding inside the allowed port. That decode can use the requested name, the headers, or a protocol fingerprint. It fails closed or fails open depending on design when it cannot identify the traffic, and that choice should be written down. Inspection of protected sessions requires the filter to see the content, just as a web-specific firewall does. A next-generation firewall is often the product that performs the layer-7 view and ties it to users. The layer itself is the idea here, not the product label. A web application firewall is a specialized layer-7 filter for web requests, not the whole story of application filtering.\n\nThe clinic allows outbound TCP 443 on the layer-4 rule so clinicians can reach approved sites. A file-sharing client then rides that same port. The layer-7 policy identifies the client and the log reads "app=fileshare action=deny" even though the port matched an allow. Ticket NET-88 shows both lines side by side: the port allow and the application deny. A tester who only proved that port 443 was open would have called the control finished. The file-sharing session is the proof that the port view was not enough. The engineer also notes a second application that could not be identified, and the policy denies unidentified outbound tunnels rather than allowing them.\n\nDefenders write rules at the view they actually mean. If the requirement is "payroll application for the finance group," a port allow is the wrong instrument. If the requirement is "this server may receive connections on the web port from the user zone," layer 4 may be enough and is easier to predict. They test with a forbidden application that uses an allowed port. They document what happens when identification fails. They keep the inspection point able to see content. They review application signatures, because a renamed client can slip past an old fingerprint until the signatures are updated.\n\nStudents memorize "layer 4 is ports, layer 7 is applications" and then treat a port allow as if it named an application. The port is not the application. Students also think layer 7 is always a web application firewall. Web inspection is one layer-7 case. Identifying a non-web client on a common port is another. Use the deeper view when the risk hides inside an allowed port.',
        'r0': "Layer 4 matches addresses and ports, such as an allow for TCP 443, and does not read the application. Layer 7 identifies the application, so a file-sharing client hiding on TCP 443 can be denied while ordinary browsing on that port remains. The log should show the application name and the deny, separate from the port allow. Unidentified traffic needs an explicit fail-open or fail-closed choice. Seeing content requires the filter to terminate or otherwise inspect the protected session. A web application firewall is layer 7 aimed at web requests. A next-generation firewall's application view is the broader layer-7 claim. Update fingerprints or a renamed client will imitate an allowed port again.",
        'r1': 'Ports and addresses are the shallow view. The application is the deeper view. An allowed port can carry a forbidden program. Test with a program that uses a common port. Write down what happens when a program is not recognized. The filter must see content to judge it. Mystery bytes cannot be named. A web-only inspector is one deeper filter. Naming other programs is a wider version of that idea. Update the names when software changes its disguise. Do not call a port allow an application allow. Put both results in the ticket if both exist.',
        'r2': 'Picture a warehouse gate that checks only that a truck arrived at dock four. Any cargo that fits the dock is waved through, including crates the warehouse meant to refuse. A second inspection opens the crates and reads the labels, and it can send back a truck the dock had already counted as allowed. If a crate has no label, the crew needs a standing order to hold it or to let it pass. A clerk who cannot open the crate cannot read the label at all. Checking the dock is the shallow filter. Reading the crates is the deeper filter, and an allowed dock does not mean the cargo itself was approved.',
        'r4': 'Find one rule that allows a common web port. Ask whether an application name is also required. Do not add a broad allow during this pass. Test, or read a past test of, a forbidden program on that port. Note the log line that names the program. Write the choice for traffic the filter cannot name. Confirm the filter can see content rather than mystery bytes.',
        'exam': 'Layer 4 sees ports and addresses. Layer 7 sees application content. The trap is to treat an allow of the common web port as an allow of one application. Other programs reuse that port. Another trap says layer 7 always means a web application firewall. Web requests are one case. Identifying a non-web client is another. A next-generation firewall understands applications, which is the layer-7 idea in product form. Decide what unidentified traffic does. A port match alone is the shallow view.',
    },
    'Virtual private networks': {
        'body': 'A virtual private network builds an encrypted path across a network you do not trust, often the internet. It does not make the far end trustworthy, and it does not replace a password. When the path comes up, the user or the site has a protected pipe. Identity, device health, and authorization are still separate questions. People who treat the tunnel as a free pass are skipping the controls that belong at the arrival dock.\n\nTwo common constructions show up on exams and in clinics. IPsec protects IP packets, often for a site-to-site or remote-access tunnel, with its own keys and policies that name which traffic may enter. A transport-layer tunnel protects a session and is often easier to pass through a firewall that already allows ordinary protected web traffic. Either construction can be called a virtual private network. Packet protection can cover many protocols between two networks. Session protection covers the session that was built for that client. Split tunneling sends only some traffic into the pipe and lets the rest go directly to the internet, which reduces load and also bypasses inspection. Full tunneling sends everything to the organization first. The choice belongs in the design note.\n\nA nurse at home starts a packet-protection client. The concentrator log shows the tunnel established from the home address. A second line a moment later reads "mfa fail user=r.okonkwo". The tunnel being up did not open the charting application. Ticket RA-40 records both lines so nobody tells the nurse that the network connection was the sign-in. The same week a site-to-site tunnel between the clinic and a lab comes up, and the policy names only the lab-result subnet. A tester who tries to reach payroll across that tunnel is denied. The interesting traffic list is what limits it. The nurse\'s laptop is still a home laptop, not a trusted clinic workstation, until device checks say otherwise.\n\nDefenders require a strong sign-in on top of the tunnel and they log both the tunnel and the application sign-in. They name the traffic allowed into a site-to-site policy instead of encrypting a wide-open route between entire networks. They choose split or full tunneling on purpose and they say what inspection the split path skips. They rotate keys and remove accounts when people leave, because a tunnel profile on a departed laptop is still a door. They monitor for a tunnel that stays up with no user, which can be a forgotten appliance. They do not describe the path as encryption of data at rest. The bytes are protected while they ride the path. Disks at either end are a different state.\n\nStudents say the tunnel replaces the password, or that the far laptop became safe because the packets were encrypted. Encryption hid the payload from the internet. It did not identify the person and it did not patch the laptop. Students also swap packet protection for transport-session protection. IPsec protects IP packets. The session protocol covers one transport connection rather than the IP packets. A virtual private network is the encrypted path, and it still ends at a dock that must check the cargo.',
        'r0': 'A virtual private network is an encrypted path, not an authorization decision. IPsec protects IP packets and is common for site-to-site tunnels, negotiating on UDP port 500 and using ESP to carry the protected packets, with the policy naming the permitted subnets. A TLS-based remote-access tunnel instead protects a transport session, often on TCP 443. Multi-factor failure must still be possible after the tunnel is up, as in an "mfa fail" log beside an established tunnel. Split tunneling leaves some traffic off the pipe and off central inspection. Full tunneling does not. Remove profiles when staff leave. The path does not encrypt data at rest on either disk.',
        'r1': 'An encrypted path crosses a network you do not trust. The path is not a free pass. The person still has to sign in. The far laptop is not safe just because packets were hidden. Name which networks may use a site path. Sending only some traffic into the path skips inspection. Sending all traffic home costs more and sees more. Remove old profiles when people leave. A tunnel with no user may be a forgotten box. Packet protection and session protection are different builds. Neither one replaces a password.',
        'r2': "Picture an armored car between a clinic and a rented lab. The car keeps bystanders from reading the crates, and the dock still opens every crate and checks the driver's badge. A car that is allowed to carry only lab results cannot be loaded with payroll envelopes just because the armor is strong. If some parcels travel by ordinary taxi, they never get the armor or the dock inspection, and someone should have chosen that on purpose. Losing a key to the car matters until the lock is changed. The armored car is the encrypted path. It does not make the far end trustworthy, and it does not replace the sign-in at the dock.",
        'r4': 'Write whether each tunnel protects packets or a session. Read one log where the path came up and note the sign-in result after that. Do not widen the permitted network list during this pass. Record split or full for remote users. Find profiles tied to people who have left. Confirm site-to-site rules name specific networks. State that disks at rest are a separate control.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to treat the encrypted path as a password replacement or as proof the far laptop is safe. The path hides traffic. Sign-in and device checks still happen. Another trap swaps IPsec and transport layer security. IPsec protects IP packets, often for this kind of tunnel. Transport layer security protects a transport session. Split tunneling bypasses inspection for the traffic left outside. A site policy that encrypts every network both ways is not the same as a policy that names the lab subnet.',
    },
    'Remote access': {
        'body': 'Remote access lets people work from outside the building. It needs strong sign-in, a limited landing zone, and logging. The outside-in path is a door the organization designed, not a convenience that grew from an open port. Contractors and staff at home are not automatically inside every zone once they connect. They arrive at a place you chose: a published application, a virtual desktop, or a bastion for administrators. Everything else stays unrouted.\n\nThe mechanism stacks several checks that students often collapse into one product name. The person proves identity with a factor that resists simple password theft. The device may have to show a health result. The landing zone is enforced by the access gateway, by network rules, or by an application proxy that publishes one program and not a whole subnet. The session log records who connected, from where, to what, and when. Administration is a narrower landing zone than ordinary charting. An administrator may reach one jump server. A nurse may reach only the charting application. Neither person receives a route to the plant controllers or the backup store. A virtual private network can be part of this design. It is not the whole design. An open remote-desktop port on the internet is the failure mode, not a solution.\n\nA contractor finishes a phishing-resistant sign-in and lands on jump-01. The gateway log reads "remote session user=contractor.lee via=rdgateway target=jump-01". A test from that session toward the file server on the server zone fails. Ticket RA-51 stores the log line and the failed test. The contractor\'s daily work does not include a full internal address. Separately, a clinician\'s home session opens only the published charting application, and the application writes its own audit entry. Two landing zones, two logs. The engineer rejects a request to forward a desktop port through the firewall "just for the weekend," because that request deletes the landing zone and the log in one rule.\n\nDefenders inventory every outside-in path, including vendor tools left behind after a project. They require the strong sign-in on each of them. They review the landing zone with a real test account that should be refused everywhere except its target. They alert on a session that targets something new. They remove access the day a contract ends, and they check that removal against the gateway, not only against a spreadsheet. They keep logs long enough to answer who was on last Tuesday. They treat a personal computer that lands in a privileged zone as a different risk from a managed computer that lands in one application.\n\nStudents equate remote access with a virtual private network. The tunnel is one way to carry the session. The design is the sign-in, the limited place you land, and the record. Students also think administrators and ordinary users should share one flat remote network because both are "remote." The landing zone follows the job. Outside in, through a door you designed.',
        'r0': 'Remote access is the designed outside-in path: strong sign-in, a limited landing zone, and a session log. A contractor record such as "user=contractor.lee via=rdgateway target=jump-01" should be matched by a test that the same session cannot open unrelated servers. A clinician may land only in a published application, while an administrator lands on a bastion. A virtual private network can carry the traffic and still is not the landing-zone decision. Remote desktop published directly on TCP 3389 to the internet is the pattern to reject. Remove vendor tools and accounts when the contract ends. Keep the gateway log long enough for an investigation.',
        'r1': 'People outside need a door you designed. Make them prove who they are. Land them in one limited place. Log who came, from where, and to what. A nurse and an admin do not land in the same place. A tunnel can carry the session. A tunnel is not the whole design. An open desktop port on the internet is a mistake. Test an account that should be refused. Remove access when the contract ends. Look for old vendor tools. Keep the log long enough to answer questions. Check the gateway, not only a spreadsheet.',
        'r2': 'Picture a clinic side door used after hours. The night pharmacist signs the book, enters a vestibule, and can open the pharmacy. The vestibule does not lead to the records basement or the roof panels. A courier with a different job gets a different vestibule that opens only onto the parcel cage. Propping the alley door because a delivery is late erases the book and the vestibule together. A covered walk from the parking lot can be added and still should end at the vestibule, not in a hallway that reaches every room. That side door is remote access: strong sign-in, a limited landing place, and a log, rather than a propped path to the whole building.',
        'r4': 'List every outside-in method, including vendor tools. For each, write the landing target and the sign-in required. Do not open a new port during this pass. Read one session log and confirm it names a person and a target. Test, or cite a test, that the session cannot reach an unrelated zone. Remove any account whose contract has ended. Note how long the gateway keeps logs.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to define remote access as a virtual private network and stop there. The tunnel can carry traffic. The requirement is strong sign-in, a limited landing zone, and logging. Another trap gives contractors a route to the whole internal network. Land them on one jump host or one application. Publishing a desktop port to the internet is not a designed door. Removing access in a spreadsheet while the gateway account still works is a failed removal. Ordinary users and administrators do not share one flat landing zone.',
    },
    'Transport layer security': {
        'body': "Transport layer security protects a transport session. It encrypts the session and authenticates the far end, usually after a certificate check. The protection starts when the handshake finishes and ends when the session ends. It does not encrypt the IP packets as a whole, and it does not protect a file that is sitting on a disk after the session is gone. People say the letters as if they were a padlock emoji. The useful version names what was protected, whose name was checked, and which version and cipher the two ends actually agreed.\n\nThe handshake is the mechanism. The client says which versions and cipher suites it will accept. The server presents a certificate. The client checks that a trusted authority signed it, that it is not expired, and that the name in the certificate matches the name the person asked for. A modern session then agrees on keys so that a later theft of the server's long-term key does not open this old session. The bulk of the data rides a symmetric cipher inside that session. If the name does not match, a careful client stops. If the version is ancient, a careful server refuses. Ending the session at a middlebox so the middlebox can inspect the content is a deliberate trust decision. The client must trust that middlebox's certificate, or the client will, rightly, treat the middlebox as an impostor.\n\nThe patient portal listens for these sessions. The certificate name matches the portal name. A tester points a client at the right address with the wrong name, and the browser warns. The server log records a handshake alert for the name mismatch. Ticket WEB-19 saves that alert beside a successful session's negotiated version, TLS 1.3, with an ephemeral key agreement and AES-256-GCM for the records. The same ticket states what this did not do: the portal database files are a disk-encryption question, and a site-to-site path that must cover many protocols is a packet-protection question. The portal session is protected. The name was checked. A stolen session key from last month does not open today's session if the ephemeral agreement held.\n\nDefenders disable obsolete versions and weak ciphers on servers they control, and they watch for clients that still offer only the obsolete ones. They automate certificate renewal so a weekend expiry does not become an outage or a temptation to click past a warning. They inventory places where inspection breaks the session on purpose, and they treat an unexpected break as an incident. They do not train people to bypass name warnings. A warning is the control working. They separate this control from data-at-rest encryption in the asset notes so an auditor can see both.\n\nStudents say this protocol protects IP packets. IPsec protects IP packets, often for a virtual private network. Transport layer security protects a transport session. Students also think a padlock means the application authorized the user. The session can be authentic and encrypted while the application still needs a sign-in. A certificate name that does not match is a reason to refuse the session, not a prompt to click through.",
        'r0': 'Transport layer security protects a transport session, commonly HTTPS on TCP 443. The client checks the certificate chain, the dates, and the name, and it should refuse a mismatch. Prefer TLS 1.3 with an ephemeral agreement such as ECDHE and a record cipher such as AES-256-GCM, so a later theft of the server key does not open old sessions. Disable obsolete versions. A handshake alert for a wrong name is the control working. IPsec is the neighboring protection: it protects IP packets, often for a tunnel, rather than one transport session. Disk encryption is a third control and does not come from this handshake. Inspection middleboxes must present a certificate the client is configured to trust.',
        'r1': 'This protects one session on the wire. It checks the name on the certificate. A wrong name is a reason to stop. It encrypts the session after the handshake. It does not encrypt a file sitting on disk. It does not cover every packet the way a tunnel can. A padlock is not an application login. Old versions should be refused. Renew certificates before they expire. Do not teach people to click past warnings. A middlebox that opens the session must be trusted on purpose. Stolen long term keys should not open old sessions.',
        'r2': 'Picture a speaking tube between a front desk and a records room. The tube is sealed for one conversation, and the desk checks that the voice at the other end matches the room that was called. If a stranger answers, the desk hangs up even though the tube itself is intact. When the conversation ends, papers left on the records table are not protected by the tube. A courier who must carry many kinds of parcels between buildings needs a different kind of locked cart, not a longer speaking tube. The sealed conversation is transport protection: one session, with a name check, and not a promise about files at rest or about every packet between the buildings.',
        'r4': 'Pick one public site your users rely on. Record the certificate name and the expiry. Do not click through a warning during this pass. Note the version a current client negotiates. Find any place that breaks the session for inspection. Confirm those clients trust that inspector on purpose. Write disk protection as a separate line in the notes. List obsolete versions the server still accepts.',
        'exam': 'Transport layer security protects a transport session, usually after a certificate name check. The trap is to say it protects IP packets. IPsec protects IP packets, often for a virtual private network. Another trap treats the padlock as application authorization. The session can be encrypted while the user still must sign in. A name mismatch is a reason to refuse. Obsolete versions should be off. This handshake does not encrypt data at rest after the session closes.',
    },
    'IPsec': {
        'body': 'IPsec protects IP packets. Clinics use it as a tunnel from one network to another, and a traveling person can use it as a remote-access tunnel as well. The protection brings its own keys and its own policy. The policy names which traffic may enter the tunnel. Traffic that misses the policy is not covered by that tunnel.\n\nThe peers negotiate with IKEv2 on UDP port 500, and they move to UDP port 4500 when NAT is on the path. In that case the encrypted packets ride inside UDP 4500. The peers prove themselves with certificates or a long shared passphrase, then build security associations that hold the keys and the lifetimes. ESP encrypts and checks integrity. AH, the authentication header, checks integrity and does not encrypt, so AH is the wrong pick when secrecy matters. Tunnel mode wraps the original packet and is the usual form between sites. A common set is IKEv2 with ECDHE or Diffie-Hellman, then ESP with AES-256-GCM, which encrypts and checks integrity together. SHA-256 is the hash when a cipher needs a separate integrity check. Key agreement creates a shared secret. Forward secrecy holds when the private values are ephemeral, so a later theft of a long-term key does not open old packets. Signatures on certificates prove the peer. Key agreement does not by itself prove a person to the application.\n\nTwo clinics, one north and one south, share a tunnel. The policy says the allowed traffic is 10.8.1.0/24 at the north clinic talking to 10.8.2.0/24 at the south clinic. The north log reads IKEv2 established, peer 203.0.113.10, ESP, selectors 10.8.1.0/24 to 10.8.2.0/24. A payroll query from 10.8.1.20 to 10.8.2.30 is encrypted. A browser session from the north clinic to a public website does not match the selectors, so that session stays out of this tunnel. Renee confirms the split on a capture of the outside link. The payroll packets appear as ESP. The public web packets do not.\n\nDefenders write down the peer addresses, the selectors, the algorithms, and how the peers authenticate. They alert when the tunnel goes down and when a peer address changes. They prefer certificates to a shared passphrase copied into many files. They do not treat the tunnel as a reason to skip sign-in on the far side. They keep the allowed ranges narrow so the tunnel is not a flat bridge. Lifetimes stay finite, and someone reviews them.\n\nStudents call every encrypted path by this name, or they treat it as TLS. TLS protects one application connection after a certificate name check. IPsec is not the same mechanism as TLS. Packet protection here can carry many applications between two networks once the selectors match. A VPN is a broader encrypted path and can be built in more than one way. The separating fact is packet protection with its own keys and a traffic policy. AH is not ESP, because AH does not encrypt. The tunnel also does not replace a password at the far end.',
        'r0': 'Protect IP packets with a tunnel that has its own keys and a traffic policy. IKEv2 negotiates on UDP port 500 and moves to UDP port 4500 when NAT is present, and ESP then rides inside UDP 4500. Peers use certificates or a long shared passphrase, then store algorithms and lifetimes in security associations. Prefer ESP over AH, because AH does not encrypt. AES-256-GCM encrypts and checks integrity together. SHA-256 is the hash when integrity is separate. Key agreement uses ECDHE or Diffie-Hellman, creates a shared secret, and gives forward secrecy when private values are ephemeral. Signatures prove the peer. Key agreement alone does not prove a person to the application. Selectors such as 10.8.1.0/24 to 10.8.2.0/24 name the traffic allowed in. A public web session that misses them stays outside.',
        'r1': 'This builds a protected path for network traffic. Two sites can share one tunnel. A traveler can use a tunnel too. The path has its own keys. A written list says which networks may use it. Traffic outside that list stays outside the tunnel. The two ends prove who they are. A long shared secret or a certificate can do that proof. Hide the contents, not only the seal. Keys should expire and be replaced. The far building still checks the person. A protected path is not a free pass. One application lock is a different tool.',
        'r2': 'Two clinic buildings share a locked indoor corridor. Only carts on the gate card may enter, and a cart bound for the street never uses it. Guards check a badge or a long shared phrase, then seal the cart for the trip. A seal that shows the cart was not opened, without hiding the contents, is the weaker seal. The usual seal hides the contents and shows tampering. Once a month the guards close the corridor and notice if the far guard is a stranger. The corridor does not decide who may open the pharmacy. That check still happens at the pharmacy door. The locked corridor is the packet tunnel, and the gate card is the traffic policy. A sealed trip still leaves a separate sign-in at the far room, because the tunnel does not replace that check.',
        'r4': 'Look at the peer address, the allowed ranges, the lifetime, and whether peers use certificates or a shared passphrase. Write down the algorithms and which choice hides the packets. Do not point production traffic at a new tunnel yet. Confirm the negotiation succeeds and the selectors match the ticket. Do not widen the ranges until that narrow policy is proven. Alert when the tunnel is down or the peer changes. Do not skip user sign-in on the far side. Prefer certificates when a passphrase would be copied widely.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to treat every VPN or TLS session as this mechanism. TLS protects one application connection after a name check. IPsec protects IP packets with its own keys and a policy of which traffic may enter. A VPN can be built in more than one way. AH does not encrypt. ESP encrypts, so ESP is the secrecy choice. UDP 500 carries negotiation, and UDP 4500 is used when NAT is present. Key agreement does not prove a person. Wide selectors turn the tunnel into a bridge.',
    },
    'Software-defined WAN': {
        'body': 'A software-defined wide-area network steers traffic across several links based on policy. Voice can stay on the clean private circuit while bulk copies ride the cheaper broadband. The organization still has real underlay paths, such as a circuit and an internet connection. What changes is that a controller, not a pile of one-off static routes, chooses the path for each kind of traffic and can change that choice when loss, delay, or jitter crosses a line you set. Several roads, and a policy that picks the road.\n\nThe mechanism separates the underlay from the overlay. The underlay is the actual links, each with a measured quality. The overlay is the encrypted or logical connectivity among sites. Policy rules name an application or a destination and a preferred link, plus a fallback. The edge device at each site measures the links and applies the rule without waiting for someone to edit a router at midnight. Central management keeps the rules consistent, which is the software-defined part. If the controller is unreachable, the edge should keep forwarding on the last policy, and that behavior needs a test. Security inspection is not automatic. Steering a flow onto broadband can also steer it around the inspection stack that lived on the private circuit. That consequence belongs in the same design note as the steering rule.\n\nThe clinic has broadband and a private circuit. Voice is pinned to the circuit. When broadband loss passes two percent, the edge log reads "policy voice prefer=circuit reason=loss". Imaging copies continue on broadband because they can tolerate delay and because the circuit is kept for calls. Ticket WAN-6 records a morning when an older router would have sent both flows into the failing broadband by default. The controller change avoided that. The same ticket notes that web traffic steered onto broadband no longer passed the main-clinic filter. The network lead did not call that a security improvement. It was a path change with an inspection gap to solve on purpose.\n\nDefenders write the policy in application language and they keep a fallback when the preferred link fails. They test controller loss. They measure whether the quality numbers match user complaints, because a rule that never triggers is a rumor. They review what inspection each link provides before they steer a sensitive flow onto it. They limit who can push a policy, since one push can move every site. They do not claim this design is the same as inspecting traffic near the user. Path selection and local security services are neighbors. This topic is the dispatcher that picks the road.\n\nStudents think any two links with a routing protocol are this design. Ordinary failover that sends everything down one backup is a single spare tire, not a policy that treats voice and bulk differently. Students also merge this topic with secure access at the edge. Steering is not the same as checking web traffic near the branch. You can steer badly and still have no inspection, or steer well and send flows around the only filter you owned.',
        'r0': 'Software-defined WAN steers by policy across underlays such as broadband and a private circuit. Quality thresholds, for example voice leaving a link when loss exceeds two percent, belong in the edge log with the chosen path and the reason. Bulk flows can remain on the cheap link while voice pins to the clean one. The edge should keep last-known forwarding if the controller is unreachable, and that mode needs a test. A policy push is a privileged action. Steering onto a link that bypasses the existing filter is a security change, not only a performance change. This is not, by itself, inspection near the user.',
        'r1': 'Several links exist, and policy picks among them. Voice can prefer the clean link. Bulk copies can use the cheap link. The choice can change when quality drops. Write the quality limit in the rule. Test what happens if the controller is down. A spare route for everything is a simpler design. This design treats applications differently. Ask which security checks sit on each link. Steering around a filter is a real gap. Limit who can push a new policy. Path choice is not the same as inspecting near the user. Read the log for the reason a flow moved.',
        'r2': 'Picture a shipping desk with a fast toll road and a cheap highway. Ambulances are sent to the toll road, and furniture trucks stay on the highway. When the highway clogs, the desk does not also throw the ambulances onto it. If the dispatcher is sick, the drivers still follow the last written plan rather than guessing. A plan that saves fuel by skipping the checkpoint on the toll road has to be admitted out loud, because the parcels then arrive uninspected. Two roads with one rule that dumps every truck onto the spare when the first fails is a different, simpler yard. The desk that picks a road per cargo type is the wide-area steering design, and the checkpoint is a separate decision.',
        'r4': 'List the underlay links at one site. Read the policy for voice and for a bulk flow. Do not push a policy change during this pass. Note the quality threshold that moves a flow. Ask what forwarding remains if the controller is unreachable. Identify any link that bypasses inspection. Name who is allowed to change the policy. Save the log line that explains the last path change.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to call ordinary active-backup routing a software-defined wide area. A spare that carries everything is not a policy that pins voice to the clean link and bulk to the cheap one. Another trap assumes steering includes security inspection near the user. That combination is the neighboring edge-access design. Steering onto a link can bypass the only filter. Controller loss should leave last-known forwarding, and that behavior has to be tested. The log should say which path was chosen and why.',
    },
    'Secure access service edge': {
        'body': 'Secure access service edge combines wide-area steering with security checks near the user, so branch traffic is inspected without hauling every session back to one office. The user connects to a nearby security edge. That edge can check the web destination, the identity, and the device, and then the remaining traffic is steered to where it must go. The point is to stop pretending that every branch is a dumb pipe to a single headquarters firewall.\n\nThe mechanism is a set of edge services, usually reached over the local internet, plus identity. Web traffic is categorized and scanned close to the branch. Application access can publish a specific application without placing the user on the whole internal network. A cloud-access broker can sit in the same edge story when the destination is a rented service. Steering policy still chooses a path for voice or for a private application, which is why this design contains the wide-area idea rather than replacing it. The headquarters firewall is no longer the only inspection point, so logging and policy have to live at the edge and be consistent across regions. If identity is down, the edge should fail in a way you have tested, not in a way the first outage invents. A laptop that bypasses the edge with a local exception is outside the design even when the slide looks complete.\n\nA branch nurse opens a link that the old design would have backhauled to the main clinic. The edge log, from a regional point of presence, reads "user=clinic\\a.mensah pop=regional-east category=phishing action=block". The session never crossed the main-clinic circuit. Ticket EDGE-4 contrasts that block with last year\'s backhaul, which added delay and hid the branch behind one pair of firewalls. Voice for the same branch still pins to the private circuit under the steering policy. The security check and the road choice are both visible in the design, and they are not the same line in the log. The engineer also finds a local break that lets the conference-room laptop reach the internet without the edge, and that break becomes the next ticket.\n\nDefenders verify that branch traffic actually arrives at the edge and is not exempted by a convenience route. They test a known-bad category and expect a block at the regional edge, not at headquarters. They publish applications instead of wide network routes. They keep identity highly available because every edge decision depends on it. They store edge logs where investigators can search across branches. They review the steering rules so a private flow that must stay on a circuit is not forced through a web filter that cannot understand it. They treat the edge as production infrastructure, with capacity and a failure plan.\n\nStudents call this "the new wide-area network" and forget the security check. Steering alone is the previous topic. Students also think the design means no controls, because the old firewall is less central. The controls moved near the user and still need operators. Check close to the user, and steer the rest.',
        'r0': 'Secure access service edge inspects branch traffic at a nearby security edge instead of backhauling every session to one office. A log from a point of presence should show the user, the region, the category, and the block, without a matching deny on the headquarters firewall. Web filtering, malware scanning, and per-application access are typical edge services, tied to identity. Wide-area policy still steers voice or private flows onto a circuit when that is the right road. A local bypass that skips the edge is a hole even if the diagram looks complete. Test identity failure. Headquarters-only logs are the wrong evidence for this design.',
        'r1': 'Check traffic near the user. Do not haul every session downtown first. Steering still chooses a road when a road matters. The check and the road are two decisions. Identity should drive the edge decision. Test what happens when identity fails. A local bypass skips the design. Look for the block in the edge log. Do not require the old central firewall to have seen it. Publish one application instead of a whole network. Keep logs where all branches can be searched. Capacity at the edge is a production need. The old central box is no longer the only guard.',
        'r2': 'Picture a chain of clinics that used to send every visitor downtown to be checked at one front desk. The trip was slow, and a closed downtown desk stopped every clinic. The new pattern puts a trained checker in each town. Visitors are looked at locally, and only the cases that truly need the central lab are sent on. An ambulance can still take the private road when the highway is wrong for it. A side gate at one clinic, left open for a delivery, skips the local checker and recreates the old risk without the long drive. Local checking plus a deliberate trip when needed is the edge design: inspect near the user, and steer the rest, instead of backhauling every bag to one office.',
        'r4': 'Pick one branch. Confirm web traffic is logged at a nearby edge, not only at headquarters. Do not create a local exception during this pass. Find any local route that skips the edge. Read one block and note the user and the place. Separate that line from the steering rule for voice. Ask what the edge does if identity is unavailable. Name who can search logs across branches.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to treat this design as wide-area steering alone. Steering picks a path. Secure access service edge adds security checks near the user so traffic is not all backhauled to one office. Another trap says the old central firewall must have seen the block. The edge log is the evidence. A local bypass that skips the edge defeats the move. Identity failure needs a tested behavior. Publishing an entire network to the remote user is not the same as publishing one application.',
    },
    'Choosing effective controls': {
        'body': 'Choosing effective controls means the control you pick reduces the risk you named, at a cost the team can actually run. The newest product is not what effective means. A control aimed at a different risk is a distraction, even when the control is well reviewed and expensive. Match the risk first. Then compare products that can perform that match.\n\nStart from the loss. Name the asset, what can go wrong, and what a working control would change. A missing restore is addressed by a backup that someone has restored in a test, not by a camera and not by a firewall. A stolen password is addressed by multifactor sign-in and by disabling the account, not by a new switch in a closet. Then price the work to run the control: people, patching, false alarms, and the minutes required on a bad day. A control nobody can operate does not reduce the risk. Write one line that links the pieces: this risk, this control, this evidence. If the evidence does not show the risk getting smaller, the control is not effective yet.\n\nThe clinic register says the billing database has never been restored in a drill. A vendor pitches a new firewall and a door camera. Elena writes on ticket 4412 that the risk is a restore nobody has practiced, so the control is a quarterly restore onto a spare server. The evidence Elena files is a restore of the Tuesday snapshot. The restore finished in 40 minutes and produced a row count matching the live billing database. The camera remains the control for the door. The firewall remains the control for traffic. Neither object is entered as the fix for the backup risk. The register changes only after that restore evidence exists.\n\nDefenders reject a control that does not touch the named risk. They prefer a smaller control the team will run over a large control that will sit in a crate. They record cost in hours as well as money. They revisit the choice when the risk changes. They do not stack products merely to look thorough. A camera or a log is detective. A detective control does not substitute for a preventive control or a corrective control when the loss is a missing copy of the data.\n\nStudents choose the newest firewall, or any stronger box, for every risk on the page. The separating fact is the match. A camera does not restore a database, and a tested restore does not watch the door. Buying both can be right when the register holds two different risks. It is wrong to claim the camera closed the backup gap. Risk acceptance is the other mix-up. Accepting a small risk, in writing, with an owner, is a real decision. Pretending a product handled a risk the product does not touch is not acceptance, and it is not an effective control.',
        'r0': 'Pick a control that reduces the risk you named, at a cost the team can run. A billing database that has never been restored is not fixed by a door camera or a new firewall. Ticket 4412 records a quarterly restore onto a spare server, with evidence that the Tuesday snapshot came back in 40 minutes and matched the live row count. A stolen password is matched by multifactor sign-in and by disabling that account. Price people, patching, and false alarms, because a control nobody operates does not reduce loss. A camera does not replace a corrective restore. Accepting a small risk in writing, with an owner, is a real decision. Buying the newest product for an unmatched risk is not that decision.',
        'r1': 'Name the risk before you name the product. The control has to shrink that risk. A camera does not restore a database. A tested restore does shrink that risk. A new firewall does not fix a missing backup. A stolen password needs a better sign-in. Disabling the stolen account also fits that risk. Write down the cost to run the control. Skip a control nobody can operate. Keep evidence that the risk got smaller. A log alone does not replace a fix. Accept a small risk only in writing. The newest box is not automatically the right box.',
        'r2': 'The clinic stores spare medicine in a fridge beside a hallway camera. The risk card says the fridge has never been shown to hold temperature through a power cut. A seller offers a brighter camera and a new closet lock. Neither offer keeps the medicine cold. The useful control is a morning test: switch the fridge off, read the thermometer, and write how long the spare doses stayed in range. The camera still watches the hallway. The closet lock remains a lock. The card changes only after the reading is attached. A control the night shift cannot run stays on the shelf. The thermometer test matches the cold-storage risk, and the brighter camera does not. Choosing that test, at a cost the night shift can run, is picking a control that matches the risk rather than the newest product.',
        'r4': 'Look at the risk register and copy the exact loss onto the ticket. Write the control that reduces that loss, plus the evidence you would accept. Do not buy the pitched product yet. Price hours and false alarms before you commit. If nobody has practiced the restore, schedule the test and file the result. Leave unrelated cameras in their own rows. Do not mark the risk closed until the evidence exists. If the team accepts the risk, write the owner and the reason.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to pick the newest firewall for every line on the register. Effective means the control reduces the named risk at a cost the team can run. A camera does not restore a database. A tested restore does. Buying both can be right for two risks. Claiming the camera handled the backup is the trap. Logs do not replace a restore when the data copy is missing. Accepting a risk means a written owner. An unmatched product is not acceptance. Disabling a stolen account fits that account, not a failed backup.',
    },
    'Regulated data': {
        'body': 'Regulated data is information a law tells you how to handle. Health records, payment card data, and student records are the examples clinics and schools meet first. The statute or the regulation sets duties: who may see the data, how long it is kept, what must be reported if it leaks, and which safeguards are named. An internal label such as confidential may also be applied. The label is not what makes the data regulated. The statute supplies the duty. A trade secret can be sensitive with no statute naming that file. Regulated data is sensitive because a legislature or a regulator said so.\n\nThe mechanism is mapping the data type to the rule, then to a handling procedure. A clinic note with a diagnosis is health information. A card number is payment data. An education record tied to a student is student data. Each mapping points at a procedure the staff can follow: where it may be stored, which vendors are allowed, whether it may leave the country, and who is told after a loss. The procedure has to show up in tools, not only in a binder. A paste into personal chat, a mailbox forward, and a vendor without a contract are the ordinary violations. Training helps people recognize the data. Technical blocks help when someone is rushed. Neither replaces the inventory of where the regulated copies actually sit.\n\nA nurse starts to paste a clinic note into a personal chat. The note contains a diagnosis and a medical-record number. The data-loss tool blocks the paste. The event line reads "blocked health-record pattern to personal webmail". Ticket PRI-18 cites the health-privacy rule and the clinic procedure, not a generic "be careful" slide. The same week a payment file is found in a shared drive that the card rules do not allow. That file is also regulated, under a different rule, and it gets its own ticket. The privacy officer does not call either file a trade secret. Competitors are not the audience the law is worried about. Patients and cardholders are the people those rules protect.\n\nDefenders keep an inventory of regulated collections and the rule that applies to each. They put vendors through the contract the rule requires before data moves. They block the common leak paths and they still investigate, because a block is not the end of the copies. They know the clock for notifications. They separate this inventory from the classification scheme so a file can be both restricted and regulated, and so a regulated file is not missed because someone forgot the label. They do not store card data "for convenience" when the payment rules say not to store it at all.\n\nStudents call all sensitive data regulated. A formula that is secret because it is valuable is a trade secret, and it may not be named by a privacy statute. Students also think a confidential label creates the legal duty. The label is an internal instruction. The law creates the regulated category. Handle health, payment, and student records the way the rule says, and know which rule you are under.',
        'r0': 'Regulated data is defined by a legal duty, not by an internal label. Health information, payment card data under PCI DSS, and student education records are common categories, each with its own handling and notification rules. A block log such as "blocked health-record pattern to personal webmail" is evidence of a control and also a prompt to look for other copies. Do not store card numbers when the card rules forbid retention. Vendor contracts have to exist before the data is uploaded. A confidential stamp can sit on the same file and still is not the source of the duty. Trade secrets are a different category: value from secrecy, not from a statute.',
        'r1': 'A law tells you how to handle this data. Health, payment, and student records are common cases. An internal label is not the law. A secret formula may be sensitive and still not regulated. Know which rule applies. Put that rule into the tools people use. Block the paste into personal chat. Do not keep card numbers the rule forbids. Contract with a vendor before you send data. Learn the clock for telling people about a loss. Find the copies, not only the blocked one. Keep this list apart from the label list.',
        'r2': 'Picture a pharmacy where some bottles are ordinary shop stock and some are controlled medicines. The controlled bottles are special because the law says how they are locked, logged, and destroyed, not because a clerk wrote "careful" on the shelf. A family recipe in the back office can be precious and still not be a controlled medicine. Mixing the two lists makes the staff guard the wrong cupboard. A delivery to another shop needs the paperwork the law names, or the bottles do not go. The controlled bottles are regulated data. The law, not the shelf tag, decides the handling, the vendor, and the report you owe if a bottle leaves.',
        'r4': 'List the collections you believe a law covers. Write the rule next to each collection. Do not move a new copy during this pass. Find one control that blocks a forbidden sharing path. Note the notification clock for that rule. Check that any vendor holding a copy has a contract. Separate this list from internal sensitivity labels. Record the report deadline if the rule names one.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to call every sensitive file regulated. A trade secret is valuable because it stays secret, and a statute may never name it. Regulated data is information a law tells you how to handle, such as health, payment, or student records. Another trap thinks an internal confidential label creates the legal duty. The label is a handling hint. The law is the duty. Card data that the payment rules say not to store is not kept "for the weekend." Vendor copies need a contract first.',
    },
    'Trade secrets': {
        'body': 'A trade secret is information that is valuable because it stays secret. The value and the legal claim both depend on reasonable efforts to keep it from people who should not have it. A formula, a pricing model, or a process can qualify. A careless post destroys the value and can destroy the claim. Once outsiders have it, you cannot get secrecy back by labeling the file. This is not a patent. A patent trades publication for a time-limited exclusive right. A trade secret works only while it remains unpublished.\n\nThe mechanism is identification plus access hygiene. Counsel and the business owner name the secret and the people who need it. Those people are few. The files live in a store with permissions, not in a general ticket system. Contracts with employees and vendors include a duty to keep the secret. Public tracks are treated as hazards: an internet-facing ticket, a slide at a conference, a repository that is world-readable, a screenshot in a chat. If disclosure happens, the organization writes down when and how, because later arguments about the claim will turn on that timeline. Technical controls support the effort. They do not replace the decision to stop talking about the secret in open channels.\n\nA compounding formula is valuable only while competitors lack it. A scientist pastes it into a vendor ticket "so the vendor can see it." The ticket system is reachable from the internet by every support account. Counsel records the time of the paste in matter note TS-3 and says the secrecy the claim needed is likely gone. The vendor could have been shown the formula under a contract, in a restricted workspace, with a named reader. The public ticket was not that workspace. The scientist did not steal a patented brochure. The scientist published a secret. Intellectual property is the broader idea that the organization owns or licenses creations. This formula was one secret inside that broader idea, and publication is fatal to it in a way publication is not fatal to a copyrighted brochure.\n\nDefenders ask the business to name its secrets instead of marking every document "secret" until the word means nothing. They keep those files out of wide tickets and public repositories. They review vendor access. They train the small group that actually handles the material. They look for the secret in logs and code searches after a leak so they know how far it spread. They do not promise that a nondisclosure clause fixes a file that is already on a public page. The clause is part of the reasonable effort. It is not a time machine.\n\nStudents call every confidential file a trade secret. Patient records are regulated. A logo is a trademark. A secret is this narrower thing: value from remaining unknown, protected by real effort. Students also think a patent and a trade secret are two words for the same filing. Publication is the point of one and the end of the other. Keep the secret in the channel meant for it, or stop calling it a secret.',
        'r0': 'A trade secret stays valuable only while it stays secret, and the legal claim depends on reasonable secrecy efforts. A formula pasted into an internet-facing ticket is a disclosure event, even if the motive was to help a vendor. The safer path is a named reader, a contract, and a restricted workspace. Patents publish the method in exchange for a timed exclusive right, so they are the opposite handling. Copyright and trademarks are other intellectual property and do not require secrecy in the same way. Do not mark every file secret or the control becomes noise. Record the time of a leak. A contract clause does not unpublish a public page.',
        'r1': 'The value is that outsiders do not have it. Careless sharing can end the value. Careless sharing can end the legal claim too. Name the real secrets, not every file. Keep them out of public tickets. Use a small reader list and a contract. A patent publishes on purpose. This does not. A logo is a different kind of property. A patient chart is a different kind of duty. Write down the time if a secret leaks. A contract cannot unpublish a page. Stop calling it secret after you post it.',
        'r2': 'Picture a brewery whose yeast strain is the reason the beer tastes right. The strain is worth money only while other breweries do not have a jar. Printing the method on a menu in the window ends the advantage, and a stamp added later does not put the secret back in the jar. Filing a public patent would tell the world on purpose and buy a different kind of protection for a limited time. A painted logo on the door is property too, and it is supposed to be seen. The yeast is the trade secret. Keep it in a closed crock with a short list of brewers, because once it is on the window it is no longer a secret.',
        'r4': 'Ask which information is valuable only while secret. Write where those files live and who can open them. Do not paste a sample into a ticket during this pass. Check whether a vendor can see them without a contract. Find any copy in a public or company-wide system. Note how you would record the time of a leak. Separate this list from logos, patents, and regulated records.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to treat a trade secret like a patent. A patent publishes the idea for a timed exclusive right. A trade secret dies when it is published, and the claim depends on real secrecy efforts. Another trap calls every confidential or regulated file a trade secret. Health records follow a statute. A logo is meant to be seen. A formula in a public ticket is no longer a secret, even if a contract existed. Name the readers and the closed channel.',
    },
    'Intellectual property': {
        'body': "Intellectual property includes creations the organization owns or licenses, such as code, designs, written content, and marks. The right is about who may copy, change, or use the work. It is not the same question as whether a patient record is regulated, and it is not the same question as whether a formula stays secret. A design can be owned even when many people inside the company can see it. A licensed library can be used only inside the terms of the license. Taking the work home because you touched it during a contract is the ordinary violation.\n\nThe mechanism is ownership and license tracking. Employment and contractor agreements say who owns work created for the organization. Repositories and drawing systems record the work and the copyright or license notice. Third-party components are listed with the license that came with them, because a restrictive license can obligate you to share source or can forbid a product use. Trademarks are used as the brand rules describe, not clipped onto a contractor's personal site. When someone copies the work out, the response uses the agreement and the notice: a takedown, a reminder, or a claim. Trade secrets are one slice of this world that additionally requires secrecy. Most code and most designs are still intellectual property even when they are not secrets.\n\nA contractor copies device design drawings into a personal portfolio site. The contract says those drawings are work the company owns. Each sheet carries a copyright notice and a drawing number. Counsel sends a takedown that cites the contract clause and the notice, and ticket IP-7 stores the page capture and the drawing number. The contractor had access in order to do the job. Access was not a license to publish. The same month a developer adds a third-party library whose license forbids the clinic's intended distribution. That library is also intellectual property, someone else's, and the fix is to remove it or to comply. The privacy officer does not handle either issue as a patient-record incident. Nobody's chart was copied. The organization's work and a third party's license were.\n\nDefenders put ownership in the contract before the work starts, not in an argument after the portfolio appears. They keep an inventory of shipped code and major designs with their notices. They scan dependencies for licenses that conflict with how the product is shipped. They remove access at the end of a contract and they watch for bulk downloads in the final week. They teach staff that a license is a limit, not a suggestion. They do not mark every sensitive operational record as intellectual property. A payroll file is financial information. A chart is regulated. The creation you own or license is this topic.\n\nStudents use intellectual property as a synonym for trade secrets. Secrecy is required for one claim and not for a typical copyright. Students also think whoever possesses a file owns it. Possession by a contractor is often access under a contract that says the opposite. Own it, license it, or stop using it. Do not let the portfolio decide.",
        'r0': "Intellectual property covers owned or licensed creations: code, designs, content, and marks. Contractor agreements should state ownership before work starts, and drawings should carry a notice and an identifier you can cite in a takedown. A portfolio copy is a use outside the job, not a transfer of ownership. Third-party licenses are part of the same inventory: a library that forbids your distribution model must be removed or obeyed. Trade secrets are the subset that also dies on publication. Copyright does not require secrecy. A patient chart or a payroll file can be sensitive without being the organization's creative property.",
        'r1': "This is work the organization owns or licenses. Code, designs, and content count. A contract should say who owns the work. Access to do a job is not a right to publish it. A notice on the drawing helps the takedown. Other people's licenses limit how you ship. Read the license before you add a library. A secret formula is a narrower idea. A patient chart is a different duty. Take contractor access away when the contract ends. Watch for large downloads in the last week. Possession is not ownership. Keep a list of what you ship and what you borrowed.",
        'r2': "Picture an architect's drawers full of plans the firm was paid to draw. The contract says the plans belong to the client, and each sheet has a mark and a number. A builder who worked in the room does not get to pin those sheets on a personal website as a trophy. A stencil the firm borrowed from another studio came with a card that says where it may be used. Using it on a public sign outside those terms is a different problem from stealing a patient file out of a cabinet. The plans are the owned work. Intellectual property is the creation you own or license, and a contract plus a notice is how you prove it when a copy walks out.",
        'r4': 'Pick one product design or code base. Write who owns it according to the contract. Do not publish a sample during this pass. Check that notices are present on the drawings or headers. List third-party pieces and their licenses. Flag a license that conflicts with how you ship. Note when contractor access ends. Separate this list from patient and payroll files.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to treat all intellectual property as a trade secret. A trade secret must stay unpublished. Code and designs can be owned without being secret. Another trap says possession is ownership. A contractor who can open the drawings does not own them if the contract says otherwise. Third-party licenses are intellectual property too, and they can forbid your use. A takedown cites the contract and the notice. Patient records and card data are sensitive for different reasons. They are not this category just because they are valuable.',
    },
    'Legal information': {
        'body': 'Legal information includes matters under privilege or under a contract, and it is not dumped into a wide ticket. Attorney-client communications and an attorney\'s work on a dispute need a tight audience or the privilege can be lost. Contract drafts, settlement ranges, and strategy notes are the files people are tempted to paste into a help desk "so someone can upload it." The channel is the mistake. Legal information is defined by the matter and the duty to limit readers, not by the fact that a sentence mentions the word law.\n\nThe mechanism is a dedicated matter space. Counsel and the named client representatives have access. Everyone else does not, including a general help desk and a general leadership channel. Messages stay in that space. Attachments are not forwarded to personal mail. If a filing must be served or registered, that public copy is a deliberate act, separate from the strategy notes that led to it. Vendors who host the matter space are bound by terms that respect the confidentiality. A ticket system used for password resets is the wrong host, because its readers were chosen for operations, not for the dispute. When a leak happens, counsel decides whether privilege survived. Technicians do not make that call in the ticket comments.\n\nCounsel\'s notes on a lawsuit strategy include the line "settle under 200000 if they cannot show notice." Someone files those notes in a help-desk ticket the whole company can read. The ticket queue includes contractors who reset passwords. Privilege is damaged because the audience was far wider than counsel and the client representatives. Matter record LEG-2 captures the ticket number, the time, and the reader group. The remedy starts by closing the ticket\'s wide visibility and by telling counsel, not by editing the sentence to sound softer. A contract draft for a vendor belongs in the contract workspace for the same reason. It is not a knowledge-base article.\n\nDefenders create the matter space before the documents exist, so nobody has to improvise a channel. They audit the group membership. They block pastes of matter identifiers into general trackers where they can. They train the help desk to refuse legal strategy even when the sender is senior. They keep retention long enough for the matter and no wider than counsel asks. They do not store this material in the same open share as facilities requests. They distinguish a public filing, which is meant to be seen by a court, from the private note about how to argue it.\n\nStudents call every policy document legal information. A published privacy notice is meant for patients. A strategy memo is not a public notice. Students also mix this with regulated health data. A chart is regulated because it is health information. A litigation note is restricted because of privilege or contract, even when it quotes no diagnosis. Keep legal matters in the channel meant for them.',
        'r0': 'Legal information here means privileged communications and contractual matter material, kept to counsel and named representatives. A strategy line such as a settlement range does not belong in a company-wide help-desk ticket. Record the ticket, the time, and the reader group if it happens, and let counsel judge whether privilege survived. Public filings are a deliberate disclosure and are not the same file as the private notes. Vendor-hosted matter spaces need terms that match the duty. Help-desk queues are staffed for operations, so their reader list is the wrong list. Do not edit the leak into softer words and call that containment of the audience.',
        'r1': 'Keep legal matters in the channel built for them. Privilege can fail if too many people see it. A help desk queue is usually too wide. Strategy notes are not knowledge articles. Contracts in draft are not public either. A filing meant for a court is a separate act. Counsel decides if privilege survived a leak. Technicians do not decide that in a comment. Limit the reader group and review it. Do not forward matter files to personal mail. Host the files where the terms match the duty. A patient chart is a different problem.',
        'r2': "Picture a lawyer's folder that stays in a locked cabinet outside the general mail room. The people named on the folder may read it. The staff who sort ordinary parcels may not, even when they are trustworthy with parcels. Dropping the folder into the parcel bin because the copier upstairs is closer shows the strategy to every sorter. A document that will be handed to a judge is prepared on purpose and is not the same as the notes about what you hope the judge will do. Softening a sentence after the bin incident does not shrink the audience that already read it. That folder is legal information: privilege or contract material, kept in its own channel, not dumped into a wide ticket.",
        'r4': 'Identify where privileged notes and draft contracts are stored. Write the reader group. Do not paste a sample into a general ticket during this pass. Compare that group to the help-desk audience. Note who is told if a wide copy appears. Confirm counsel, not the ticket system, judges privilege. Check vendor terms if a vendor hosts the matter space. Confirm the files are not in the general help-desk queue.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to drop privileged strategy into a help-desk ticket the whole company can read. The audience destroys the limit privilege required. Another trap calls every policy or privacy notice legal information in this sense. Public notices are meant to be public. Litigation notes are not. Health records are regulated data, not automatically privileged legal files. Counsel judges whether privilege survived. Closing the ticket later does not unsee what the wide reader group already opened.',
    },
    'Financial information': {
        'body': 'Financial information includes accounts, payroll, and card data, and it draws both criminals and regulators. Bank account numbers, salary files, and primary account numbers are useful to thieves and are also covered by duties that tell you how they may be stored and sent. A spreadsheet of card numbers mailed to a personal address is a classic failure. The file is not a trade secret, even though it is valuable. Its value to an attacker is fraud. Its importance to the organization is also a legal and contractual duty, especially when the data are payment cards.\n\nThe mechanism is minimization plus controlled systems. Do not collect the number if a token from the payment processor will do. If you must process cards, the payment rules limit storage, transmission, and who may see the full value. Payroll systems should be the only home for salary data, with a small access group and a log of exports. Bank files for payments should travel on the bank\'s channel, not in a mail attachment. Data-loss tools look for the patterns of account numbers leaving the environment. Masking can show a clerk the last four digits. Masking is a display control. It is not a reason to keep the full number in a world-readable sheet. Encryption protects a file at rest or a session in transit and still does not decide whether you were allowed to store the number.\n\nA payroll analyst exports a sheet of primary account numbers and tries to mail it to a personal address "for the weekend." The data-loss log shows "pan count=40 destination=personal mail blocked". Ticket FIN-12 records the block and a follow-up search for other copies of the same export. One copy is found on a department share with access far wider than finance. That copy is removed under the payment-card procedure, which would rather the full numbers were not stored at all. The analyst can still do the job with a report that shows the last four digits. The full values were never required in the mail. Criminals would use the sheet to commit fraud. Regulators and the card contracts care that it existed.\n\nDefenders locate every store of account, payroll, and card data. They shrink retention to what the process requires. They block personal mail and unmanaged storage for those patterns. They review who can export. They use the payment processor\'s token so internal systems do not hold the card number. They train finance staff that a weekend convenience is a leak path. They do not file this incident as a trade-secret disclosure. Nobody stole a recipe. Someone mishandled money data that thieves and rules both care about.\n\nStudents lump financial files with every other confidential file. The neighbor topics are real: card data is also regulated, and a display that hides digits is masking, not permission. The distinguishing point is the content. Accounts, payroll, and card data attract fraud and specific duties. A design drawing is intellectual property. A settlement memo is legal information. The till tape does not go home in a pocket.',
        'r0': 'Financial information covers bank accounts, payroll, and payment card data, and it attracts both fraud and regulatory duty. PCI DSS limits how primary account numbers are stored and transmitted. Prefer a processor token so the full number never sits in a clinic spreadsheet. A data-loss event such as "pan count=40 destination=personal mail blocked" should trigger a search for other copies, not only a celebration of the block. Masking that shows the last four digits is a display aid. It is not permission to keep the full value in a wide share. Encryption of a forbidden copy does not make the copy allowed.',
        'r1': 'Money data draws thieves and regulators. Accounts, payroll, and card numbers all qualify. Do not collect a card number if a token will do. Do not mail the full numbers home. A last four display is only a display. Search for extra copies after a block. Keep payroll in the payroll system. Send bank files on the bank channel. Limit who can export. A design drawing is a different category. A lawsuit note is a different category. Encryption does not bless a copy you should not have stored. Weekend convenience is a leak path.',
        'r2': 'Picture a cashier cage where the till tape and the wage envelopes stay in the cage. A clerk who wants to finish counts at home is not given the tape in a coat pocket. The cage can show a customer the last digits of a number without laying the full tape on the counter. A thief wants that tape for fraud. An inspector wants it kept under known rules. A secret beer recipe in the brewery next door is valuable for a different reason, and a stack of blueprints is valuable for yet another. The tape is financial information. It stays in the system built for money, because both criminals and the rules care about it.',
        'r4': "Locate stores of card, bank, and payroll data. Do not export a fresh copy during this pass. Note whether full card numbers could be replaced by tokens. Read one data-loss event if one exists and search for sibling copies. Check who can export from payroll. Confirm bank files use the bank's channel. Separate this inventory from designs and legal strategy files.",
        'exam': 'The trap is to treat card and payroll files as trade secrets. Thieves want them for fraud, and payment rules may forbid storing them. Another trap says masking the last four digits solved the storage problem. Masking hides part of a value for display. Tokenization swaps the value for a token kept in a vault. A blocked mail still requires a search for other copies. Encryption does not authorize a copy the rules say not to keep. Financial information is accounts, payroll, and card data.',
    },
    'Human-readable and machine data': {
        'body': 'Some data people can read, such as a note or a message. Some data is for machines, such as a log, a token, or a key file. Both can be sensitive. The mistake is to treat readable prose as the only thing worth protecting, or to treat junk-looking text as harmless because a person cannot pronounce it. A memo can be regulated. A session token can be a secret that replays a login. The form of the bytes does not decide the duty.\n\nThe mechanism is classification and handling that follow the meaning, not the typeface. Human-readable stores include mail, tickets, documents, and screenshots. Machine data includes application logs, database dumps, tokens, certificates, and configuration that embeds a secret. A debug bundle often contains both: a paragraph a nurse wrote and a token the application issued. Access rules, retention, and leak checks have to cover both. Log systems are a common miss because they were built for engineers and then filled with identifiers, tokens, and free-text complaints that quote a diagnosis. Screenshots are a common miss in the other direction because they look like a picture and still contain a full record. A token that looks random is not encrypted just because you cannot read it. It is a bearer secret until it expires.\n\nA developer attaches a debug bundle to a ticket. Inside it, a memo names a patient in plain language, and a line reads "sess=8f3c91aa" from the production authentication log. The memo is human-readable and regulated. The session value is machine data and is still a secret: anyone who presents it can ride that session until it dies. Ticket APP-90 holds the bundle and a note to rotate the session and to redact the memo. The developer had called the log "just noise" and the memo "just a sentence." Both needed the same careful channel. A screenshot of the same screen, taken for a training slide, would have been a third copy of the readable record.\n\nDefenders review logging standards so tokens, passwords, and full identifiers are not written in the clear. They set retention on log stores that matches the sensitivity of what actually landed there, not the sensitivity people hoped to log. They include object storage and crash dumps in data inventories. They teach staff that a picture of a screen is a copy. They run the same leak checks on text files and on structured logs. They do not assume a machine format is safe to paste into a public issue tracker. They label exports that mix notes and tokens as sensitive even when half the file looks empty to a casual reader.\n\nStudents protect the memo and publish the log. Students also protect the log and paste the memo into a wide chat because it was "only words." Human-readable data and machine data can each be sensitive. A coat-check ticket looks meaningless and still claims a coat. A diary looks ordinary and is private. Handle both, or the half you ignore becomes the leak.',
        'r0': 'Human-readable data includes notes, mail, and screenshots. Machine data includes logs, session tokens, keys, and dumps. A single debug bundle can hold a plain-language diagnosis and a session identifier such as sess=8f3c91aa, and both are sensitive for different reasons. The token is a bearer secret until expiry, not harmless noise and not encryption. Logging standards should forbid passwords and long-lived tokens in clear text. Retention follows what the log actually contains. A screenshot is a copy of the readable record. Crash dumps and object stores belong on the inventory beside the document system. Treat a crash dump as a possible copy of memory secrets and restrict who can download it.',
        'r1': 'People can read some data. Machines use other data. Both can be sensitive. A memo can be regulated. A session token can replay a login. Random looking text is not automatically safe. A picture of a screen is still a copy. Logs often collect more than you planned. Do not paste tokens into public tickets. Do not paste notes into wide chats. Set retention for what the log really holds. Include crash dumps in the inventory. Redact and rotate when a bundle leaks. The shape of the bytes is not the duty.',
        'r2': 'Picture a lab bench with a handwritten notebook and a barcode sticker on a vial. A visitor can read the notebook and learn a patient\'s story. The visitor cannot pronounce the barcode, and the barcode still opens the storage record if it is scanned at the door. Throwing the notebook in a public bin is one leak. Pinning the barcode photo to a public board is another. Calling the barcode "only a code" does not empty the vial. The notebook and the barcode travel together in the same sample bag more often than people admit. Human-readable notes and machine values can each be sensitive, and a bundle that holds both needs the careful handling of both.',
        'r4': 'Open one recent debug bundle or log export. Mark each part as readable text or machine data. Do not attach it to a wider ticket during this pass. Circle any token, key, or full identifier. Circle any regulated sentence. Note whether logging rules forbid those tokens. Include screenshots and crash dumps in the same review. Set a follow-up to rotate anything secret you found.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to protect only text a person can read. A session token looks like junk and can still replay a login. Another trap ignores the memo because machine logs feel more technical. A plain diagnosis can be regulated data. Random characters are not encryption. A screenshot is a copy. Logging standards and retention have to match what was actually stored. Human-readable data and machine data can each be sensitive, including when they share one bundle.',
    },
    'Data classifications': {
        'body': 'Classifications such as public, internal, confidential, and restricted tell people how careful to be. The label is useless if nobody handles the file that way. Public may sit on a website. Internal stays inside the organization. Confidential needs a tighter group. Restricted is the smallest audience and the strongest handling. The names vary by organization. The handling scale stays the same idea. A label records a decision. Permissions, encryption, and sharing behavior have to match the decision or the label is decoration.\n\nThe mechanism is a short scheme with examples, an owner who can label, and controls that read the label or at least do not contradict it. Staff need a rule of thumb: a press release is public, an ordinary procedure is internal, a personnel file is confidential, a small set of secrets or highly limited records is restricted. Tools then enforce the handling. A restricted file does not belong on a share granted to everyone. A confidential mail does not get forwarded to a personal account. Automated labels can follow the store, and people can set them when they create a document. Disagreement is the defect to hunt: the banner says restricted, and the access control list says everyone can read. Regulated data often maps into confidential or restricted, but the statute is not the label. The label is your instruction. The statute remains even if someone forgets the stamp.\n\nAn auditor opens a file whose banner says restricted. The share permission is "Everyone: Read". The path is a department drive the whole clinic can open. The audit note says "restricted object, world-readable path". Ticket DATA-30 assigns the records owner to either relabel the file, if the banner was wrong, or fix the permission, if the banner was right. Leaving both in place is the failure. The same review finds a public brochure stored in the restricted vault, which blocks the marketing site from the approved copy and trains people to ignore the vault. Too high a label and too low a label are both defects. The scheme works when the handling matches.\n\nDefenders publish the four labels with examples and with the handling each one requires. They sample stores for disagreement between banner and permission. They do not add a fifth label every time a project wants a special word. They map regulated collections onto the scheme without pretending the map replaces the law. They give creators a default that is safe, usually internal, rather than a default of everyone. They review sharing links that bypass the label. They measure the program by mismatches found and fixed, not by how many files received a stamp in a rush.\n\nStudents think classification is the control by itself. A stamp does not stop a read. Permission restrictions do that. Students also think the labels are the same thing as legal categories. Public, internal, confidential, and restricted are your handling scale. Health information is regulated because of a law, whether or not the stamp is present. The label only works if the handling matches.',
        'r0': 'Use a small scale such as public, internal, confidential, and restricted, each with an example and a handling rule. The defect is disagreement: a banner that says restricted on a path where the access control list grants Everyone Read. Fix the label or fix the permission. Do not do neither. A public brochure locked in the restricted vault is the opposite defect and teaches people to route around the vault. Regulated collections usually map to confidential or restricted, and the statute still applies if the stamp is missing. Default new files to internal. A sharing link that ignores the label is a mismatch. Extra homemade labels make the scale noise.',
        'r1': 'Labels tell people how careful to be. Public, internal, confidential, and restricted are enough. A label without matching handling is decoration. A world readable restricted file is a mismatch. Fix the label or fix the access. Do not leave both wrong. Locking public material away also trains bad habits. Laws are not the same thing as labels. A missing stamp does not erase a law. Default new files to an internal label. Watch links that bypass the banner. Keep the scale short. Measure mismatches, not stamp counts.',
        'r2': 'Picture a warehouse with four shelf colors. White stock may sit near the public window. Blue stock stays in the staff aisles. Yellow stock needs a smaller cage. Red stock needs the inner cage and a named list. A red tag on a box in the window is a lie or a careless shelf. A white box locked in the inner cage makes workers invent a side pile that nobody labels. The paint does nothing if the doors do not match the color rules. Those shelf colors are data classifications. The label only works when the handling matches, and a stamp by itself never locked a door.',
        'r4': 'Write your four labels and one example of each. Do not invent a new label during this pass. Sample ten files and compare banner to permissions. Record every mismatch. Decide later whether the banner or the permission is wrong. Note any public file hidden in the tightest store. Check whether new files default to a safe label. Write the handling action for the tightest label.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to think a classification stamp is the control. If a restricted file is world-readable, the label and the handling disagree. Permissions are what stop the read. Another trap treats public, internal, confidential, and restricted as the names of statutes. They are an internal scale. Regulated data can map onto that scale and remains regulated when the stamp is missing. Too many custom labels make the scale useless. A public file stored as restricted trains people to bypass the store.',
    },
    'Data at rest, in transit, and in use': {
        'body': 'Data at rest sits on disk or another stored medium. Data in transit moves across a link or a path. Data in use is being processed in memory, not the same as data at rest or in transit. A laptop can encrypt its disk, a mail path can protect the session, and a clerk can still open the record on a screen in a busy lobby. Three states, three controls. Protecting one state and claiming the record is covered is the usual exam miss and the usual real miss.\n\nThe mechanism matches a control to a state. At rest, full-disk encryption or database encryption keeps a stolen drive from being readable without the key. The key has to live somewhere that is not the same unprotected file. In transit, a protected session or a tunnel keeps eavesdroppers on the path from reading the bytes. The name check and the version still matter for the session. In use, the application has decrypted the data to work on it. The working copy sits in process memory and may appear on a screen. A memory dump, a stolen unlocked session, or a shoulder surfer sees it even though the disk and the wire were protected. Controls for the in-use state include locking screens, keeping sensitive processing off shared lobby machines, limiting what the process retains, and, where the platform supports it, protected computing features that reduce exposure in memory. Those features do not appear because you encrypted the disk.\n\nA clinic laptop uses full-disk encryption with AES-256, so a powered-off theft does not hand over the files. Mail to the portal uses a protected transport session. A clerk then opens a patient record at a lobby desk. The screen faces the waiting room. The same bytes are now data in use: processed in memory and visible. A support technician who captures a memory dump of that process can read the record in the dump. Ticket PRIV-44 lists the three states on three lines and marks only the third as failed. The lobby screen threw the in-use state away. Moving the workstation around the corner is the fix for that state. Buying another disk product does not fix the lobby screen.\n\nDefenders draw the three states for each sensitive flow and refuse to check only the disk box. They confirm transit protection on the actual path, including paths to vendors. They watch for unlocked sessions and lobby screens. They limit debug tools that dump memory on production systems. They do not log the in-use content back into a file that then becomes a new at-rest problem. They explain to leadership that encryption at rest does not hide an open chart. They revisit the states when a new integration copies the data to a place nobody drew.\n\nStudents collapse in use into in transit because both feel "active." In transit is the wire. In use is processing in memory. Students also think disk encryption covers a backup tape that was never encrypted, or a screenshot saved later. Each copy has its own state. Sitting, moving, or open in memory: name which moment you protected.',
        'r0': 'Data at rest is stored, and AES-256 full-disk encryption is the example to teach for a stolen powered-off laptop. Data in transit is on the wire, protected by a transport session or by IPsec for packets. Data in use is being processed in memory, which is also when a screen can show it. A memory dump of the live process reveals the record even when the disk and the session were protected. Screen locks and careful placement address the in-use state. Disk encryption does not. Do not write the decrypted content back into casual logs or you create a new at-rest copy. Check all three states on each sensitive flow.',
        'r1': 'Stored data, moving data, and data being processed are different. Disk protection covers a stolen powered off machine. Path protection covers eavesdroppers on the wire. Memory is where the program actually works. A screen can show that working copy. A memory capture can steal it. Lock screens and move private work away from lobbies. Do not say the disk product solved the open screen. Do not say the wire product solved the disk. Avoid writing secrets back into casual logs. Name the moment you protected. Check every new copy the same three ways.',
        'r2': 'Picture a restaurant. The pantry lock protects food on the shelf. The sealed delivery van protects food on the road. The cutting board is the moment the cook is actually using the food, and a stranger at the pass can see it even though the pantry and the van were fine. A photograph of the cutting board, left on the counter, becomes a new copy on the shelf and needs the pantry treatment again. Buying a stronger pantry lock does not hide the board. The pantry, the van, and the board are the three states. Stored data, data on the move, and data being processed need three different protections.',
        'r4': 'Pick one sensitive record. Write the at-rest control, the in-transit control, and the in-use exposure. Do not disable encryption during this pass. Note whether a live screen faces a public area. Ask whether anyone can dump process memory on that system. Check that logs do not store the decrypted content. Add any new vendor copy to the same three lines.',
        'exam': 'Data at rest sits on disk. Data in transit moves. Data in use is being processed in memory, not the same as the other two. The trap is to say disk encryption covered an open screen or a memory dump. The trap in the other direction says a protected session encrypted the database files. A third trap calls in-use data merely "in transit" because the user is active. The wire and the memory are different moments. Each copy, including a screenshot or a log, has its own state.',
    },
    'Data sovereignty': {
        'body': 'Data sovereignty means the laws of the place where data sits or is processed can apply, which can surprise a team that only picked a region from a menu. Storage location and processing location are legal facts, not just latency facts. A clinic that must follow its home health-privacy duty can conflict with a foreign rule that lets authorities demand access under different conditions. The surprise is not theoretical once the bytes are in that place. Moving them later is slow, expensive, and sometimes too late for a copy that already replicated.\n\nThe mechanism is a region decision tied to counsel, written into the contract and the configuration. Each store, database, backup, and support tool gets a named region. Defaults are suspect, because a default region is whatever the vendor chose. Subprocessors and support staff who can log in from another country are part of the same question, even when the disk icon says the home region. Replication for resilience can copy data to a second region you did not intend. Keys are part of the map: if the key is held in the foreign region, the practical control follows the key. An exit plan says how data comes home and how foreign copies are deleted. A geolocation rule that blocks a login from a country is a different control. It governs where a person may stand. Sovereignty governs which law reaches the stored processing.\n\nPatient records were created in a storage bucket whose console defaulted to a foreign region. The clinic\'s counsel compares that country\'s access rules with the clinic\'s duty at home and finds a conflict. Ticket SOV-5 says "bucket region=foreign, counsel hold" and stops further uploads. A replica for "durability" had already copied the bucket to a second foreign site. The engineer lists both regions, the support vendor who can open either, and the key location. Nobody fixes this by blocking foreign logins to the portal. Staff could still be at home while the disks and the legal exposure sit abroad. The login rule and the storage law answer different questions.\n\nDefenders inventory regions for production, backups, logs, and support tools. They forbid a new region without a counsel check. They read replication settings. They put the allowed regions into the contract with each vendor. They test deletion of a foreign copy, not only the creation of a home copy. They keep customer-managed keys in a region they have accepted when the threat includes compelled access to provider-held keys. They do not describe a content delivery cache as automatically outside the question if it stores regulated content.\n\nStudents think sovereignty is a firewall country block. Blocking a sign-in is geolocation restriction. Sovereignty is which law applies because of where the data sits or is processed. Students also think a vendor\'s headquarters address is the data\'s location. The region of the disks and the processors is the fact. Where it sits can decide which law applies.',
        'r0': "Data sovereignty is the legal effect of the place data is stored or processed. A bucket that defaults to a foreign region can put patient records under a conflicting access law, and a durability replica can add a second foreign copy. Record every region for primary data, backups, logs, keys, and support access. Counsel should accept the region before upload. Customer-managed keys kept in an accepted region matter when compelled access to provider-held keys is the worry. Deletion of the foreign copy has to be tested. A login block based on the user's country is geolocation restriction, not sovereignty. Vendor headquarters is not the same fact as the processing region.",
        'r1': "Where data sits can decide which law applies. Processing location counts as well as the disk. A menu default is not a legal decision. Replicas can copy data somewhere new. Support staff abroad can be part of the path. Put allowed regions in the contract. Ask counsel before the first upload. Test that a foreign copy can be deleted. Keys have a location too. Blocking a foreign login is a different control. A vendor's home office is not the disk location. Write every region down, including backups and logs.",
        'r2': "Picture a warehouse the clinic rents in another county because the signup form picked the nearest city. The other county's rules about who may open a crate can conflict with duties back home. A second warehouse, filled automatically so a fire does not destroy the stock, doubles the problem. The company that owns the forklifts may live in your town and still not be the place the crates sit. Refusing to hand a key to a driver from that county does not move the crates. You still need a way to bring the crates home and to empty the far building. Those warehouses are data sovereignty: the law of the place where the data sits or is processed, which a login rule about the driver does not decide.",
        'r4': 'List regions for the primary store, backups, logs, and keys. Do not enable a new region during this pass. Note any replica you did not intend. Ask whether counsel accepted each region. Check where support staff can open the data from. Separate this list from login country blocks. Confirm a tested way to delete a foreign copy. Include backup regions on the same list.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to treat data sovereignty as a block on foreign logins. That block is a geolocation restriction. Sovereignty means the laws where data sits or is processed can apply. Another trap trusts the vendor headquarters instead of the configured region. Defaults and durability replicas create foreign copies. Keys have locations too. Counsel accepts the region before upload. Deleting the foreign copy is part of the control, not a hope for later.',
    },
    'Geolocation restrictions': {
        'body': 'Geolocation restrictions limit where a sign-in or a data access may happen. A payroll application can refuse a sign-in from a country where the company has no staff. The control uses the source location the identity system believes, often from the network address, and compares it to a policy. Some places are simply not allowed. This does not decide which law governs the database. It decides whether this attempt, from this place, may proceed.\n\nThe mechanism is a policy on the identity provider or the application: allow lists, deny lists, or risk that rises when the place is new. A user who travels needs an exception that is time bounded and approved, not a personal tunnel that hides the country and defeats the rule. Device location and network location can disagree, so the policy should say which signal it trusts. Impossible travel, such as two countries minutes apart, can be a separate signal in the same product and is not the same rule as a flat country ban. Administrators must be covered too. A break-glass account that works from anywhere is the account attackers will use. Logs should say the user, the decision, and the place that was seen, so a block can be explained and a bypass can be investigated.\n\nThe payroll application refuses a sign-in. The identity log reads "blocked conditional access location=denied-country user=payroll.admin". The source is a country on the deny list. Ticket ID-61 confirms the user is not traveling and leaves the block in place. A different user, a clinician on a known aid trip, has a seven-day exception scoped to the charting application only. The exception does not cover payroll. The engineer also finds a personal tunnel profile that would make the clinician appear to be at the clinic while sitting elsewhere. That profile is banned for payroll users because it exists to defeat the location check. The database region is unchanged throughout. Nobody claims the country block moved the legal home of the records.\n\nDefenders write the allowed and denied places with the business, not only with a map that looks tough. They test from a denied place and from an allowed place. They expire travel exceptions. They alert on successful sign-ins that contradict the policy, which means the policy is not bound to that application. They include privileged accounts. They explain the difference to leadership so a sovereignty project and a login project are not closed by the same checkbox. They accept that location signals can be wrong or spoofed, so this control is one layer beside strong sign-in, not the only layer.\n\nStudents swap this control with data sovereignty. Sovereignty is which law applies to storage and processing. A geolocation restriction is an access decision about where the person or the session appears to be. Students also think a virtual private network is always a legitimate way to satisfy the rule. A tunnel that hides a forbidden place is a bypass. Some places are not allowed, and the log should show the decision.',
        'r0': 'Geolocation restrictions deny or allow a sign-in or a data access based on place. A conditional-access result such as "blocked conditional access location=denied-country user=payroll.admin" should name the user and the decision. Travel exceptions need an end date and a scope, and they should not spill from charting into payroll. Personal tunnels that hide the true country are bypasses for this control. Impossible-travel alerts are a related signal, not the same as a country allow list. Cover break-glass accounts. Test both an allowed place and a denied place. Data sovereignty remains the legal effect of where the bytes are stored or processed, which this login rule does not change.',
        'r1': 'Some places are not allowed for a sign in. The rule looks at where the attempt seems to be. Keep a log of the user and the decision. Travel needs a short, approved exception. Do not let that exception cover every application. A personal tunnel can hide a forbidden place. Treat that hide as a bypass. Include admin accounts in the rule. Test from a denied place and an allowed place. Wrong location data means this is not your only control. This rule does not choose the law of the stored data. End the exception when the trip ends.',
        'r2': "Picture a clinic key that opens the pharmacy only when the bearer is at that building. A copy of the key presented from another city does not turn. A pharmacist on a planned trip can be given a dated pass for a different door, and the pass does not also open the payroll drawer. A costume that makes the traveler look as if they never left town is a cheat, not a pass. The rule about the key is not the same question as which county's inspectors may audit the room where the records are filed. The key rule is a geolocation restriction: it limits where a sign-in or an access may happen, and it leaves the legal home of the stored records to a separate decision.",
        'r4': 'Write the places a sensitive application allows. Do not add a country during this pass. Read one blocked sign-in and confirm the user and the place are in the log. List travel exceptions and their end dates. Check whether privileged accounts are covered. Note any tunnel that would hide the true place. State in one line that storage law is a different review.',
        'exam': "The trap is to say a geolocation restriction chooses which country's law governs stored data. That question is data sovereignty. This control limits where a sign-in or a data access may happen. Another trap accepts a personal tunnel that hides a denied country. That is a bypass. Exceptions need an end and a narrow scope. Break-glass accounts that work from anywhere defeat the list. Location signals can be wrong, so the control sits beside strong sign-in rather than replacing it.",
    },
    'Masking, tokenization, and obfuscation': {
        'body': "Masking, tokenization, and obfuscation each hide or replace a value, and none of them is a substitute for access control by itself. Masking hides part of a value for display, or shows a fake stand-in on the screen, while the real value may still exist behind the display. A support clerk sees the last four digits of a card and cannot reconstruct the rest from that screen. Tokenization swaps a value for a token stored in a vault. The application keeps the token. The vault keeps the original and the mapping. Obfuscation makes the real value hard to see, including by hiding a message inside something that looks ordinary. Obfuscation is not a serious secrecy control. Anyone who knows the trick, or who simply has the file, may still recover the content.\n\nMasking fails when a second screen, an export, or a log shows the full value, or when the masked view is treated as proof the full value is gone. Tokenization fails when the application can ask the vault to detokenize without a tight permission, or when the token is just a reversible scramble with no vault and someone learns the scramble. Obfuscation fails because hiding is not access control. A comment buried in a file, a renamed extension, or a message concealed in an image still leaves the file in the hands of whoever can read the file. Encryption is the neighboring idea students force onto all three. Encryption is reversible with a key and is meant as a cryptographic control.\n\nSupport staff at the clinic see only the last four digits on the ticket screen. The payment system stores a token, and the card number sits in a vault the support role cannot query. The payment log shows the token and never the full number. A developer also hides a note inside an image attached to a build, hoping a reviewer will not notice. The image is still in the repository, and the note is still there for anyone who looks. The reviewer does not accept the image as an access control. Permission restrictions, which name the accounts that may read the vault or the repository, remain necessary.\n\nDefenders decide which of the three they meant before they buy a tool that advertises all the words. They test an export and a log to be sure masking was not only a stylesheet. They restrict detokenization to the payment service account and they alert on bulk detokenize calls. They refuse obfuscation as a control for regulated data. They keep encryption, where they need confidentiality of a stored or moving copy, as its own line. They train staff that a last-four view is not a permission to request the full number. They review the vault's access separately from the application's access.\n\nStudents say masking is encryption, or that a token is only a scrambled copy of the number sitting in the same database. Obfuscation makes something hard to see and still needs a real permission behind it. None of the three decides who may ask.",
        'r0': 'Masking shows a partial or fake value, such as the last four digits, and the full value may still live behind that view. Tokenization replaces the value with a token and stores the original in a vault. Detokenization should be limited to a specific service account and alarmed when it runs in bulk. A reversible encoding with no vault is not tokenization. Obfuscation, including a message hidden in an image, is not access control. Encryption with a key is a different control from all three. Test exports and logs, not only the screen, before you claim the full card number is absent. Permissions still decide who may read the vault.',
        'r1': 'Masking hides part of a value on screen. The full value may still exist behind it. Tokenization swaps the value for a token. The original stays in a vault. A scramble with no vault is not that swap. Limit who can turn a token back into the value. Obfuscation only makes something harder to notice. A hidden note in a file is still in the file. None of these replaces a permission check. Encryption with a key is a different tool. Test the export, not only the pretty screen. Alarm on bulk lookups in the vault. Say which of the three you meant.',
        'r2': 'Picture a teller window that shows a customer only the last digits of an account, while the full number stays in a drawer the teller cannot open. A coat check is a different trick: the customer holds a stub, and the coat hangs in a room the stub does not unlock by itself. Invisible ink on a poster is a third trick, and anyone who knows the lamp, or who simply owns the poster, can try to read it. The window, the coat room, and the ink solve different problems, and none of them is the lock on the door. Masking is the partial display, tokenization is the stub with the real value stored in a vault, and obfuscation is the ink. Who may open the drawer is still a separate rule.',
        'r4': 'For one payment screen, write whether staff see a partial value or a token. Do not turn on a broad lookup during this pass. Trace where the full value is stored. Confirm a vault mapping if tokens are claimed. Open an export and a log and look for the full value. Note who can exchange a token for the original. Reject hidden file tricks as if they were permissions.',
        'exam': 'Masking hides part of a value for display. Tokenization swaps a value for a token stored in a vault. The trap is to call either one encryption. Encryption uses a key and is reversible by design. Another trap says a masked screen proves the full number is gone. Check exports and logs. Obfuscation, including a hidden message, is not access control. A scramble stored beside the application with no vault is not tokenization. Permissions still decide who may ask.',
    },
    'Permission restrictions on data': {
        'body': 'Permission restrictions limit which accounts can read or change the data. This is the control masking does not replace. A masked screen can still sit in front of a store the wrong account can open. An access control list, a database grant, or an application role names the accounts and the actions: read, write, delete, share, export. Who may open it, by account, is the whole topic. Labels, encryption, and partial displays support a careful program. They do not answer the account question if the grant is still everyone.\n\nThe mechanism is a default of no access, then explicit grants to groups that match a job. The records group can read the restricted share. The help desk cannot, even though help-desk staff are employees and even though they can see a masked ticket that mentions the file. Service accounts get the minimum action, not a standing administrator role copied from a human. Sharing links and "anyone with the link" are permissions too, and they are easy to forget because they do not look like a user in a group. Owners review grants when people change jobs. A permission that follows someone into a new department is how payroll data spreads. Changes to powerful grants are logged. A backup of the data inherits the question: if the backup is readable by a wide admin group, the primary permission was theater.\n\nThe restricted share allows the records group to read and denies everyone else, including the help desk. The access line reads "Records: Read, HelpDesk: Deny". A support analyst who can see the last four digits in the ticket tool cannot open the share. Ticket DATA-44 tests that deny with the analyst\'s account and saves the refusal. A second test shows a link created by a departing employee that still grants access to anyone who has the URL. The link is the real permission, and it contradicts the group. Classification of the share remains restricted. The classification did not remove the link.\n\nDefenders start from no access and add groups for jobs, not for individual favors. They include links, service accounts, and backups in the review. They remove access when roles change, on a short clock. They alert on a grant to everyone or on a public link to a restricted store. They test with an account that should fail, not only with an administrator account that always works. They keep masking, where a display should hide digits, and they still verify the underlying grant. They do not accept "we encrypted it" as a reason a broad group may read the files. Encryption protects against a thief who lacks keys. The group\'s keys are the permission.\n\nStudents think a confidential label restricts accounts. The label tells people the intended care. The permission restricts the accounts. Students also think masking or tokenization performed this job. A display mask conceals part of a value on the screen. A token stands in for a value kept in a vault. Neither one is the list of accounts allowed to read the file. Set that list, test a refusal, and include the link nobody remembers.',
        'r0': 'Permission restrictions name which accounts may read or change data. Default to no access, then grant a job group the specific action. A restricted share that shows "Records: Read, HelpDesk: Deny" should be proven by a failed open from the help desk, including staff who can see a masked value elsewhere. Sharing links and service accounts are permissions. Backups that a wide group can read undo the primary grant. Role changes should remove access quickly. A classification banner does not edit the access control list. Masking and tokenization do not either. Alert on a grant to everyone and on a public link.',
        'r1': 'Decide which accounts may read or change the data. Start from no access. Add a group for the job, not a personal favor. Test with an account that should fail. Include links that grant access without a group. Include service accounts and backups. Remove access when a job changes. A label does not edit the account list. A masked screen does not either. A token in a vault is a different system. Encryption is not a reason for everyone to have the key. Log changes to powerful grants. Look for a link left behind by someone who left.',
        'r2': 'Picture a records cage with a key list on the inside of the door. Only the names on the list get a key. A clerk who may see a redacted slip at the front desk is not automatically on that list. A spare key taped under the mat is still a key, even though it does not look like a line on the list. When someone moves to a different job, their key comes back the same week. A sign that says "private" does not pull a key out of a pocket. The key list is permission on the data. It limits which accounts can read or change the records, and a redacted view at the desk does not replace it.',
        'r4': 'Open the access list on one restricted store. Write the groups and the actions. Do not add a grant during this pass. Test an account that should be refused and save the result. Search for sharing links and service accounts. Check whether backups are readable more widely than the store. Note how fast access ends after a job change. Keep masking and labels as separate notes.',
        'exam': 'Permission restrictions limit which accounts can read or change the data. The trap is to think a classification label did that work. The label is an instruction. The access list is the control. Another trap says masking or tokenization replaced permissions. A display mask only conceals part of a value on screen. A vault token is not the account list. A public link is a permission even when the group list looks tight. Test a refusal with a real account. Include backups and service accounts.',
    },
}
